{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/673743-irin-the-announced","id":673743,"title":"Irin: The Announced","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-09-21T19:16:12-07:00","id":673743,"title":"Irin: The Announced","content":"Halfway through writing my fansplat <b>Irin: The Watchful </b>I realized I was quite dissatisfied with where things were going. Themes were getting muddled, ideas were growing extremely cumbersome, and I had some kind of weird 1e/2e hybridization going on. Suffice to say, it was going nowhere fast and it needed an overhaul. I spent some time thinking on what I really needed to focus on, thematically, and have been rewriting the whole thing. I'm not done yet; as a matter of fact, I'm not even close to where I was in my first version, but I'm catching up fast and I hope the end result will be a whole lot better than it was originally going to be.<br /> <br /> There will be references to a few new mechanics in these early pieces, most notably Maxims, Exousia, and Temptation. Without getting too far into the semantics or the fluff, think of Maxims as a combination of the Arisen Pillars, and Uratha Renown. Exousia is the new Integrity-equivalent, measuring the connection of the Announced to the Divine Fire and how much leeway they possess in terms of action. Temptation is a condition similar to Torment, wherein the Announced tends to fall back on their obsessions and baser urges.<br /> <br /> <b>Introduction</b><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">You were meant for something.<br /> <br /> That\u2019s what your parents always told you; that you were meant for great things. They never doubted, they always knew, and they treated you like the world rested on your shoulders. The expectations were enormous, but so was the drive. Your obsession mounted into something sublime, and your soul <i>burned</i>.<br /> <br /> Humanity has always been drawn to stories of the divine made manifest. Those stories captivate our imagination- the idea of the sublime being close enough to touch, for a divinity to feel as you\u2019ve felt. It is a compelling idea, especially when it takes beings unapproachable and untouchable in their perfection and makes them a little more human. But there\u2019s one thing more compelling than a god slumming it for a while with the mortals- and that\u2019s a god who chooses to stay.<br /> <br /> <b>Word made Flesh</b><br /> <br /> In this game, you play one of the Irin- born of a human mother and Announced by an angelic being; you were born for a purpose within the World of Darkness. The Irin, commonly known as Angels, the Luminous, or the Announced, have no idea what they are meant for- only that purpose and destiny are expected of them. Upon becoming aware of their nature, and experiencing it for the first time in Confirmation, it is impressed upon them that in spite of the lofty promises proceeding their birth they have only a single purpose: to bear witness. You don\u2019t know what that means, or what is expected of you, save for that it is not your place to participate, only observe. Will you obey the dictates of heaven? Will you choose to hurry destiny along? Or will you throw yourself headlong into the world? Purpose or no purpose, you\u2019re part of it, and that can\u2019t be helped.<br /> <br /> Whatever this means to your character, in <b>Irin: The Announced </b>you are a divinity born into the mortal realm, possessed entirely of free will- but bound in chains forged in the very furnaces of creation. You can feel Divine Fire roaring in your blood, you can hear the universe singing joyfully in your presence, you can wander the limitless light of realms rarely reached by man- and you\u2019re asked to stay quiet, to watch attentively, and above all, <i>obey</i>. The ancient world knew of the Irin- legends are told of them. The Sumerians remember demigod sages, the Apkallu, who taught them of civilization. The Saptarishi are revered in Hindu and Vedic texts as messengers and teachers from Brahma. And then, there are their noble forebears- the Grigori. The Book of Enoch is sacred among the Irin, and forms the basis of much of their culture. Ultimately, its story is one they can all relate to. Fulfill your purpose- observe and obey -or, <i>act</i>. Instead of living adjacent to the world, live <i>within</i> it.<br /> <br /> But to be Irin is to be born apart from humanity. For years, you can pretend to be human but everyone, including your parents, knows that there\u2019s more to you. Pyros burns in your blood, the light of Glory shines through your pores whether you want it to or not. Unlike the wandering Prometheans, the Pyros in the Irin doesn\u2019t incite the fear of the outsider, but a reverence of the divine. It isn\u2019t the enraged terror of an obsessed bigot, but the fervor of worship. For beings that are not typically supposed to attract attention, the Fire still calls out to any who are willing to see it. The heat of the Fire runs through their emotions- towering rages, fierce passions, and all-consuming sorrow -these emotions often lead to rash action, and rash action is often rebellion. The Irin would do well to remember the end of the Grigori- not a fall from grace, but unending chastisement. Endless castigation in the depths of the Pleroma.<br /> <br /> <b>Theme: The Price of Choice</b><br /> <br /> Everyone makes choices, and everyone has to live with them, but for the Irin these choices can come with a little more weight and a lot more regret than those most people have to deal with. Adam and Eve chose to eat the fruit- and for that they were expelled from Eden. The Grigori chose to abandon duty for love, and then had to watch as all of their work was torn asunder. An Irin can choose to respect the power and authority they have been given, or abuse it. And in abusing it, they can wrap themselves in chains.<br /> <br /> Choices have unintended consequences- and the power of the Irin means that for as high as they rise, they have that much further they can fall.<br /> <br /> <b>Mood: Frustrated Pride</b><br /> <br /> Make no mistake about it, the Irin are divine. Fallible, and totally capable of being as screwed up as the next person, if not more so, but still divine. And there\u2019s nothing they can really do to acknowledge that. The awe and terror experienced when viewing the true form of the Irin can be lethal; their powers can- pushed to their limits -level towns. Any joy they could feel by making use of the full extent their abilities is curtailed by the fear of what will happen if they unintentionally abuse them. All that power, all that potential, and they are expected to squander it on trivial matters- it\u2019s easy to see how frustration builds. That is, of course, only if they\u2019re unwilling to pay the price that comes from satisfying their need to be recognized for who, or what, they are.<br /> <br /> <i>Angels, Angels, Angels</i><br /> <br /> Many, many entities have been called angels in the World of Darkness. It\u2019s really not that hard- bear a message have wings, express divine power and majesty. Some spirits of the Shadow, especially those of Virtues, can be confused with angels.<ul><li>The biomechanical horrors of the God-Machine often build off of popular ideas and images of angels, and for all intents and purposes fulfill the purpose and often the description of an angel to a tee.</li> </ul><ul><li>The Empyrean Angels mentioned briefly in <b>World of Darkness: Inferno</b> are called angels, mostly because they are the exact opposites of the Fiends of the Inferno. Not a whole lot is known about them other than that they exist. An alternative name for them used in this text will be Messengers.</li> </ul><ul><li>Some of the Geists who bond with Sin-Eaters believe themselves to be angels.</li> </ul><ul><li>Mages who Awaken to the Watchtower of the Golden Key speak of the living symbols of Forces and Prime- the Supernal Seraphim and Cherubim. In the Temenos, it\u2019s possible to find just about any being dreamt of by humanity, and that would include angels.</li> </ul><ul><li>Some of the True Fae may take the image of angels or craft Changelings in that shape. Some folklore says that the Fae are actually angels too evil for Heaven and too mad for Hell.</li> </ul><ul><li>Finally, there are the <i>qashmallim</i> that Prometheans often encounter. These are the angels we\u2019ll most concern ourselves with, as these are also the same angels that presage, and often cause, the birth of the Irin.</li> </ul><b>Annunciation</b><br /> <br /> Someone always knows the Irin is coming, even before they are born. They are the Announced; their birth is heralded in the stars, the vaults and flames of heaven dance joyfully when it occurs. However, that is not to say that things are of such obvious pomp and recognition on earth. It is <i>possible</i> that the Irin was Announced by a <i>qashmal</i> in its full glory using some of the more\u2026 dramatic Numina at its disposal. It is <i>possible</i> that they were the result of Pyros triggering a spontaneous pregnancy, or that their parents always knew what they were and with both fear and admiration prepared them for their eventual Confirmation.<br /> <br /> The default assumption of the setting, however, calls for something subtler. A lesser <i>qashmal</i> in a mortal mask makes an off-handed comment about the future child that lights a spark in a parent\u2019s eye, instilling a powerful imperative that will drive the child to Confirmation. An overheard conversation on the bus inspires a couple to pour everything into conceiving. There are endless, subtle means for the Principle\u2019s most trustworthy servants to accomplish their mission, and trigger the birth of an Irin. But the <i>qashmallim</i> don\u2019t choose to who they appear, and if they are unequipped- whether by power or by knowledge -they can make mistakes. Perhaps a few too many Irin are simply the result of a bumbling <i>qashmal</i>, and their missions cannot be altered or reattempted. The Principle works with what it has.<br /> <br /> Whatever the circumstance, the Annunciation often colors how, and by whom, the child is raised- it affects expectations, demands, and even parenting styles. Some parents, obsessing over a potential \u2018perfect child\u2019 will press the Announced into higher and higher standards of excellence. Some, fearful of the child\u2019s possible divinity, will be cold and distant- afraid of what could happen when their Glory begins to shine through. Some, resentful of the eventual importance of their child, will even be abusive. All these things will eventually affect how the child will come to see the world- and may even influence their Confirmation.<br /> <i>The Shedim</i><br /> <br /> The Principle is one- Divine Fire is Divine Fire, but it\u2019s expression is different from manifestation to manifestation. <i>Qashmallim</i> for Azoth- the drive of creation; the desperate obsessive power of imagination, emotion, and the human spirit. To interact, connect, and relate to others. Azoth is life- evolutionary, transformative. It unites, it binds, it makes whole. On the other hand, there\u2019s Flux. Where Azoth leads to union, Flux is ever changing- chaotic, sluggish in its gathering but full of the might of entropy. It is transformation through destruction, through breaking things down. It is the great divider.<br /> <br /> And just like Azoth, it has its Announced.<br /> <br /> Called the Shedim, their lives and powers are often rather different from Announced born of Azoth and watched over by <i>qashmallim</i>. Instead, the Shedim can hope for the baleful gaze of the <i>lilithim</i>. Normally, these characters would make an easy opposing faction for the protagonists- but I would prefer for playing a Shedim to be as valid an option as playing an Irin. So, for those of you who want to play destroying angels and those who hold the key of the Pit tight in their grasp- sit tight. The Shedim still follow many of the same rules as the Irin, but with a few twists, so we\u2019ll be covering them first.<br /> <br /> <b>Confirmation</b><br /> <br /> It is perhaps fitting that changing from simply a human with an unusual prenatal history into one of the Irin is a matter of choice and obsession. Everyone wants excellence- to push themselves past simply <i>good</i> and into greatness. Overachievers, perfectionists, individuals with abiding obsessions- all of these are often states analogous to a budding Irin. Something- be it pride, desperation, need, or a cause -requires an individual to give more than their all, to engage in a truly Herculean effort. Whatever the motivator is, it is deeply personal, unyielding, and full of intense hunger and desperation. This can be invigorating, sublime, or utterly traumatizing and horrific.<br /> <br /> The athlete who pushes themselves farther than they believe or know they can endure might suddenly feel the Pyros burning within their chest, Glory coalescing around them. They perform beyond admirably, and exult in their success. For a brief moment, they are a god on the field, and everyone can see it.<br /> <br /> The musician is living paycheck to paycheck on small gigs, and then suddenly something clicks and they sing with the voice of an angel. The crowd is so stunned, that they are totally silent until the performance ends- and then they roar. Afterwards, they speak of how the spotlight behind the band seemed to give the musician a halo- but there was no spotlight.<br /> <br /> The student whose parents have great expectations stares blankly at an exam, until suddenly in a fit of inspiration he writes two flawless booklets full of an essay so powerful that their teacher feels a need to discuss it in class. The words are beautiful; every so often a teacher will take it from the drawer where exams are stored and read it, just to enjoy the words.<br /> <br /> A soldier in combat finds himself and his buddies ambushed. In absolute need to get away and protect his comrades, he can feel Pyros turning his blood to steam- and then nothing. His next memory of being surrounded by scorched earth, the smell of burning flesh and his buddies dead; apparently of sheer terror.<br /> <br /> A teenager trying to explain herself to her parents is met with nothing but abuse and intolerance. She needs them to <i>see</i>, and they do. Glory burns through their minds, and they fall to the ground, screaming prayers until they die.<br /> <br /> A husband desperate to see his wife and child through a troubling birth insists on staying with her. The fire burns through her and the infant. The child is born with a terrible fever, and later dies. The wife finds herself eternally cold once the Pyros runs its course. She spends most days in bed, loss making her cold inside and out.<br /> <br /> <i>Creator and Created</i><br /> <br /> Should one of the Created who has studied the Fire, and knows of the possibility of modern Demiurges, ever run into one of the Announced they will notice some rather disturbing similarities. The process that allows a Demiurge to harness Pyros to create a Promethean, and that which transfigures the Announced is in many ways identical. The only difference is, the kind of obsession that drives a man to try and create life ultimately ends with success, but that which drives the Announced remains. A Demiurge gets a foot in the door to get just enough Pyros to make a life, and then the passion that gave them the metaphysical leverage to force the door open in the first place dissipates, slamming it closed. Announced fling the door wide open, and step through.<br /> <br /> <b>Purpose</b><br /> <br /> The Confirmation is the first taste of glory for the Irin, and the only example of it intended to be for themselves to revel and enjoy- Divine Fire hitched (for the first and only time) wholly to their will. After the Confirmation they receive a vision, which if they ever manage to track down one of the <i>qashmallim</i> to ask about, is supposedly directly from the Principle. They are given the basic commands that help guide responsible use of their authority relating to their purpose, and a basic mission- something to which they can bear witness. The Irin, heralds of Azoth, are meant to heal, to build up, to guide, to make whole. But they are also meant to punish those who would misuse the Divine Fire, offer warnings, and scourge the unworthy- even if the Principle\u2019s idea of what is \u2018unworthy\u2019 is rather idiosyncratic. The Shedim, masters of Flux, are agents of punishment and entropy, of chaos temptation and torment. They scourge the souls of the righteous to test them, they tear down the unworthy to keep the profane from the divine. And they often hate themselves for it.<br /> <br /> Regardless, the Announced are universally given knowledge that <i>something</i> will happen. Details on who, why, when, where, and how can be sketchy- with information too detailed and focused to discern anything, or too broad to be of much use. It is often cryptic, usually symbolic- a riddle of destiny to be untangled. Regardless, the Irin must figure out what it is they are to witness, let it come to pass, and then act as they will in the aftermath of the event. What the Irin witness and observe are often horrifying in their scope, or barely trivial. What matters is what is expected of them. An Irin may be called to murder an adulterer for reasons known only to the Principle, or stand by and watch as a drunk driver tears a hole through a family. The Principle\u2019s motives, agenda, and direction are ineffable, and inscrutable. The temptation to let punishments for crimes the Irin can\u2019t comprehend slide, or prevent a disaster from occurring. Every Irin, almost without exception, will stand against the tide at some point in their long lives.<br /> <br /> Maybe, they\u2019ll punish someone \u2018sufficiently\u2019 but the Principle calls for increasingly harsh responses- mounting from the unsettling to the outright horrific. Maybe they are expected to stand by and let some disaster occur, and they simply <i>will not</i> allow it to come to pass. The Principle does not like to be disobeyed, but it is also the ultimate executor of free will, and choice. It is self-determination and predestination rolled into one, and it cannot take away freedom that hasn\u2019t been surrendered. So when the Irin defy their master, they begin to see the divine plan fall apart. A mission can only be undertaken <i>once</i>, regardless of result. The Irin abusing their authority to act against the Principle will start to see tragedy in the situation. Little acts of subversion will trigger misfortune for people who could really do without it, greater acts of sabotage could mean the death of innocent bystanders. Outright defiance could lead to true disaster. The Irin must, therefore, weigh every possibility when making a judgment call. Most only defy the Principle in the most dire of circumstances.<br /> <br /> Some study the disasters, try to figure out the logic in the Principle\u2019s plans, or determine how to curb their damage. Some build vast social networks, enabling them to enact the will of the Principle without getting their hands dirty. And some will even manipulate the world <i>around</i> their mission, so that it has no choice but to change its parameters to fit their will. In the end, it is about finding a medium- most likely not at all happy -between what you want, and the desires of an unknowable god. Between the freedom to use your power, and the wisdom to not abuse it- so much of modern fiction has to deal with when an authority may have a point, and when it is better to question it. And the Irin have to wonder; can they go beyond their calling? Their power is rarely matched among the other creatures of the World of Darkness- they could be an avenger, a hero. They could right wrongs and uproot corruption. They could be a god or a saint among men, or simply take what they want with all the backing and might of a divine agent. And the most enterprising of all seek to understand the Principle\u2019s alien mind- if it has one. If they can figure out what mission will come next, and <i>why</i>, then they can intervene and enact the Principle\u2019s will without the need for tragedy. The choice, in the end, is ultimately theirs.<br /> <br /> <b>Communion</b><br /> <br /> The Irin all have a complicated relationship to the divine. It touched their lives in impossible ways during the Confirmation, and continues to influence them through their sometimes <i>very</i> long lives with missions and the chains of authority inherent to Exousia. The Irin chafe at their manacles- they want to be free, but they never can be, because no mere servant of the divine can actually plead their case to it\u2026 or can they? The Irin pursue Confirmation because it is the only way out of being slavishly obedient for the rest of their lives. Very few Irin have reached Communion with the divine, but rumor has it those that have are unbound- limitless. Truly divine, truly immortal, and a free agent in the universe. No one is quite sure how to pursue it, but all of the Irin Sects have some opinion on it, and many actively seek it out.<br /> <br /> <b>Inspiration</b><br /> <br /> <u>Literature</u>: <i>Something More Than Night</i> by Ian Tregellis. A dead Archangel leads to a noir mystery novel. Bayliss makes a wonderful <i>qashmal</i> and Molly an excellent Irin, fresh to the supernatural world. One of the key codifiers of ideas in this text, especially when it comes to the Pleroma and the metaphysical sanctuaries called Magisteriums.<br /> <br /> <i>The Golem &amp; The Jinni</i> by Helene Wecker. An atmospheric piece in 19<sup>th</sup> Century New York, a Golem and a Jinni (Who could just as easily be a very confused Irin) end up meeting and striking up an unusual friendship. The Jinni in particular is an excellent portrait of the frustration and passion of the Irin.<br /> <br /> <i>Horns</i> by Joe Hill. While slightly more evocative of the Fiends of the Inferno, Ig Perrish could just as easily be an Irin with a slightly more demonic form. There are also the Shedim to consider. Tortured and tormented by the changes that have come over him, Ig\u2019s search for the truth ends up costing him dearly.<br /> <br /> <i>Angelology</i> by Danielle Trussoni. Demonstrates evocatively the duality of the Irin; mixed sublime beauty and nightmarish terror. The angels presented in this book, technically closer to the Nephilim than the Grigori, are beautiful, prideful, passionate, and monstrous. An example of the Irin at their worst.<br /> <br /> <u>Film</u>: <i>The Prophecy</i> Directed and Written by Gregory Widen. Some of the most terrifying angels ever put to film; Christopher Walken\u2019s Gabriel is a driven, horrifying antagonist who wants to win at all costs. If God still speaks to him, he isn\u2019t listening. An excellent example of how to do an Irin antagonist.<br /> <br /> <i>The Hunchback of Notre Dame</i> as envisioned by Disney. While it certainly helps that the Latin mass is ever-present in the film, and religious imagery is used continuously- the biggest qualifier is the characters themselves. All of them have powerful fixations, most notably Frollo and Quasimodo, but other characters also possess them to a lesser degree. Esmeralda\u2019s passion for justice could easily be the fervor of a Malakim, while Frollo presents as a <i>particularly</i> ruthless, entitled, and horrifying Elohim.<br /> <br /> <u>Television</u>: <i>Dominion</i> on Syfy. A loose sequel to the movie <i>Legion</i>, the higher angels presented who can take human form under their own power (Not counting Michael, Gabriel, or Uriel) are pretty good examples of the Irin. Most just want to live in peace and are impossible to differentiate from regular humans until they bring out the wings.<br /> <br /> <u>Other</u>: <i>The Book of Enoch</i>. Read it, know it. The quintessential story of the Irin, the compelling characters of the Grigori form a huge portion of the inspiration for the Irin, and grace much of their lore and culture.<br /> <br /> <i>Barlowe\u2019s Inferno</i> by Wayne Barlowe. A visionary fantasy, science fiction, and horror artist, his visions of hell and its inhabitants are unique, grotesque, and strangely beautiful. While his body of work doesn\u2019t have a specific influence on this game, the aesthetic of the work is incredibly useful for getting ideas for the Celestial Forms of the characters, the wide vistas of Sheol and the Pleroma, or their varied and fascinating antagonists. This is what the <i>penitentes</i> see in their dreams. Remember that.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Chapter 1: Genesis</b><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><i>There was a war in Heaven. An eternity ago, when the Divine could touch the mortal realm- though at the time it was little more than a formless void. A squabble between aspects of the Divine (which were really part of a single union) grew into a true war, and in the end a faction lost and a faction won. And, as would become traditional in all conflicts, this fallen aspect was painted as a scapegoat for the entire situation, and bore the weight of that responsibility on its shoulders. The Fallen One was cast out, and this had consequences entirely unforeseen. Now, there was \u2018self\u2019 and \u2018other\u2019. There was Divine, and there was Profane. Material reality existed. Instead of being part of a great union, the Fallen One was an individual. And it knew what it was to be totally, utterly alone. But the Fallen One was not solitary in its descent, for it had torn a rent into the Divine Fire, and these smaller shards too had identity and thought. They were anguished and furious in the loss of union with the Divine, and they attacked the Fallen One and dealt It a mighty blow. The Fallen One- who would become known as the Prodigal Demiurge -mourned for eons, trying to contemplate the why and how of Its descent, and how it was possible to be so\u2026 lonely. Struck such a blow by Its fellow exiles, and cast down in the first place, the Prodigal was not whole, and it sought to return to the Divine at all costs. Its first simple pleas fell on deaf ears. It could touch the Divine Fire, but what had once been like a shroud to the Prodigal was now a wall of limitless fire that It could wield, but never pass through. The physical world didn\u2019t offer much solace either, even though the Prodigal\u2019s influence was greater there, those unprepared for a brush with even a small portion of the divine went away afflicted by madness. Though it mourned the destruction inflicted, the Prodigal had no other option except to be shunned eternally, and so attempted to find a way back to union with the Divine.</i><br /> <br /> <i>This is when humanity enters the story.</i><br /> <br /> <i>Somehow, humanity came to the attention of the Principle. Whether some were born from the first Prometheans created by the Prodigal Demiurge, or came about naturally, they drew the Principle\u2019s attention. Was it because, like the Divine Fire, humans sought connection? A relationship with members of their own kind, other living things, and the world as a whole? Could it be because humans, as artificers, dreamers, and creators, could call the Fire into the world? Whatever the case, the Principle was instantly fascinated with humanity, and the first </i>qashmallim<i> arrived in the mortal realm. The other fragments of the Divine Fire, watching and waiting through all of creation for a sign from the Principle, made every effort to appeal to their fellows, to find a way back home. But without mortal form, the </i>qashmallim <i>could not see them, for they were of the same substance, and the servants of the Principle could not differentiate between one source of Pyros and another, they were incapable of seeing themselves as individuals. These Watchers went to the Prodigal Demiurge, whom they had struck down in their anguish, and begged for the art of making mortal bodies for themselves. The Prodigal, sensing an opportunity, made a deal. The Prodigal could not make them perfect bodies- the damage done had made sure all its creations would be flawed -but if they gave up an infinitesimal piece of themselves, and pleaded its case to the Principle, it could teach them the art. The Watchers agreed. Clad in mortal form, and capable of interacting with the </i>qashmallim <i>they begged and pleaded for a way to return to the Divine. No one is quite sure what was negotiated, or what was sacrificed- but what is known is the end result. The Principle managed to connect with the Watchers, but it was not until much later did they realize the implications of what had been done.</i><br /> <br /> <i>The Watchers would serve the Principle; they would be intercessors between It and the Prodigal Demiurge. They were sent to watch over and protect nascent civilizations, and they discovered that the passion, curiosity, and obsession inherent to the Fire would be a detriment. They grew fond of those they watched over, punished, scourged, and guided. They grew to love humanity, but seemed bewildered that the Principle took no direct action on their behalf. It would be trivial for them to reveal themselves, and with the benefit of the Divine Fire send humanity bounding ahead. The Watchers became obsessed with the mortal world and its pleasures. They abused the authority and power their connection to the Divine gave them to seek out the limits of the human experience. And it was then they realized a terrible truth. Their connection to the Principle gave them mission parameters, things forbidden to them to ensure that the Divine Plan would be obeyed. But after such a long time as individuals free to act as they desired, the will of the Principle was like a chain. So long had they spent alone, separate from the Fire, that the Principle was incomprehensible, its plans and their missions seemed illogical. They were frightened. And like many who find themselves unable to understand the situation they\u2019re in, the Watchers became angry. They railed against the Principle, opposed it however they could. They turned their children, the Nephilim, into nightmarish armies to wield against one another and the Principle itself- and it was then that they realized another grave mistake. In handing fragments of themselves to the Prodigal Demiurge to patch the wound they had inflicted, and then in acting as Its intercessors to the Principle, they had unwittingly handed it the ends of their long leashes. Though the Prodigal had fallen with them, it had not experienced life in the mortal world; it was still a divinity, still alien in mind compared to the Watchers. For untold millennia it had tried to atone for whatever sin had cast it down, and now it saw those who had agreed to help it destroying in mere decades what had taken eons to prepare. The Prodigal saw Its chance to return to the Divine evaporating, saw that the Watchers would </i>never<i> return to the Pleroma now. In desperation, it took hold of the Watchers\u2019 chains and pulled them taut, tried to force them into obedience. The Prodigal, desperate to prove that Its thoughts were not their thoughts, attempted to appease the Principle. And when It felt as though that had failed, it sent a flood of Pyros into the world- the largest Firestorm in history -to scourge the civilizations and cults the Watchers had built from the world.</i><br /> <br /> FORBIDDEN. <i>It said</i> YOUR DAYS SHALL NUMBER 500 YEARS, AND YOU SHALL BE BOUND FOR 70 GENERATIONS.<br /> <br /> <i>Ever since, the Announced have felt the bite of their chains should they try to act in a manner contrary to their purpose. Ever since, the Prodigal- terrified of being left alone -has tried to use its control of the Irin as evidence of its dedication to the Principle, in the hope of one day return to the limitless union of the Divine. Ever since, the Luminous have bitterly given the Prodigal Demiurge a new name.</i><br /> <br /> <i>They call it Metatron.</i><br /> <br /> <b>The Grigori</b><br /> <br /> They have gone by many names over the millennia. Many Irin believe them to be the inspiration behind the gods of numerous cultures and religions. The Grigori were the Olympians, the Annunaki, the Igigi, the Asuras, the Daevas, the children of El, the Yazatas, the servants of the Heft Sir- or so the legends of the Announced say. But the name most of the Irin prefer to use is Grigori- the Watchers. The story behind Paradise Lost a couple thousand years before Milton was even born, the legend of the Grigori is probably older than the Book of Enoch in which it is recorded, as Biblical texts that reference it predate the copy that has been widely translated. Of course, in the World of Darkness, there\u2019s a <i>reason</i> the story well predates the text, and that would be because it\u2019s mostly true. The Grigori are out there, and the Announced can find them if they look hard enough. That\u2019s not to say that the Grigori have all the answers, not by a long shot.<br /> <br /> If you ever actually managed to find one of them, they\u2019d probably make vague, high and mighty proclamations, but the truth is they have forgotten most of what actually happened and generally accept whatever story they dredge up and manage to mix with whatever is left of their memories. Most prefer to use the Book of Enoch as a foundation because- in the World of Darkness -the Grigori known as Azazel helped write it sometime around 200 BCE when Announced scholars seeking the truth of their origins managed to track him down and find him in a good mood. Like the story above says, they have been bound for 70 generations of their successors, the Irin. Most Grigori tend to agree that the time immediately before the Deluge was sometime around 11,000 BCE once someone has gone to the trouble of dredging their foggy memories for indicators of the times. Which means, for those of you still counting score at home, that the Grigori are still bound and imprisoned for another 22,000 years. What this means for them specifically will be discussed in a later chapter.<br /> <br /> The important thing to note here, is while the story of the Grigori is central to much of the culture of the Irin, and their potential as mentors, backers, allies, or enemies shouldn\u2019t be discounted, they also <i>shouldn\u2019t</i> be used by the Storyteller to reveal earth-shattering cosmic truths. If they ever knew them, or were even able to articulate them while separate from the Fire (and it\u2019s unlikely they\u2019d understand the question as an ordinary <i>qashmal</i>) they\u2019ve long since forgotten.<br /> <br /> <i>The Nephilim</i><br /> <br /> What, exactly, were the Nephilim? No one is really sure, and most modern Irin wouldn\u2019t want to find out. They were said to be immensely powerful, potentially gigantic in size, and eternally hungry. Stories discussing them directly say the only beings they feared were their parents, or the <i>qashmallim</i>- everything else wasn\u2019t considered a worthy opponent, or even much of a reason to fear. When they were killed in the Deluge, their souls lived on, made immortal by the taste of the Divine in their blood. Perhaps the Nephilim are the source of the first Fiends of the Inferno and Messengers of the Empyrean. Perhaps the Nephilim became the Strix, or served as the progenitors of the Kindred. Maybe the Nephilim were the first mages, or beings akin to the Lucifuge. Perhaps they are the source of the Horrors that fill the souls of the Begotten, with every dead nephil empowering a Beast.<br /> <b>Flames in the Wind</b><br /> <br /> In the centuries that followed the destruction of the Announced civilization, much of their lore was lost. The Grigori scattered to the four corners of the earth, and stayed more or less in isolation- unaware that more Irin were being born into the world with absolutely no idea what they were. Most, quite reasonably, assumed they were gods or demigods. They built up cults and religions, lording over one tribe or another as a local god, competing with other beings who enjoyed the worship of mankind. Missions were obviously their divine insight, the rare <i>qashmallim</i> that showed up were fellow divinities. In such times, it would be exceedingly rare for even a few Irin to know of one another and those that did would often band together in small pantheons.<br /> <br /> As civilizations grew, their ideas of gods similarly advanced. No longer was the local demigod a figure who was counted among the upper echelons of divinity. They had gods like Bull, Snake, Raven, and Wolf to think about. There were Spirits, there were the young True Fae, and in the land that would be called Kemet there were strange, masked immortals who would take up the countenance of animals.<br /> <br /> The gods of humanity were bigger and badder than they had been. The Announced were relegated to servants and heralds, which suited their purposes- if not their pride -just fine.<br /> <br /> <b>Rekindled Flames</b><br /> <br /> After the collapse of the Nameless Empire, other civilizations began to return to some prominence. Uruk became one of the greatest cities of the age, the largest since Irem and the largest in the world for centuries to come. It, naturally, became a hotbed of supernatural activity- filled with Kindred, Scelesti, and Arisen cults, packs of Uratha who would eventually come to rule the city-state of Bau, and others. But among the most prominent of these was the cult of Anu, based from the great White Temple. This was one of the first Irin Sects, a unified group that attempted to seek out and govern other Announced as far as their influence reached. Records would seem to indicate that a similar group was founded in the Indus Valley, while paintings, artifacts, and folklore suggest that the disparate Irin of the Americas were part of a loose community.<br /> <br /> When Uruk fell in the twilight of the third millennium BC, the cult of Anu collapsed with it. He became an impotent deity, whose importance would be superseded by Enlil, then Enki, and then Marduk. None of them had any need for divine servants on earth when they called so fervently for human ones.<br /> <br /> After that, the Announced scattered, carrying broken bits of oral lore, and some precious few clay tablets that would be destroyed during the upheaval of the First Sothic Turn. Relics of the Nameless Empire were curiosities to many, and a reminder that any empire- no matter how many gods love them or how much supernatural aid they possess -could fall. When the Arisen returned in force, they sought their relics with mindless wrath and unimaginable power. The chaos tore apart much of the Fertile Crescent, and may have ended the Sixth Dynasty of Old Kingdom Egypt, which brought on the fractured Egypt of the First Intermediate Period.<br /> <br /> With the rise of the Achaemenid Empire, the Announced once again came together to form a unified society that coalesced around Zoroastrianism and went searching for others. The Announced of the Empire called themselves Yazatas. In the highlands of Persia, they found those who believed themselves to be a kind of semi-divine being called a Peri. In India, they called themselves Daevas and Asuras. In Sumeria, they named themselves Igigi, Lahmashu, and Utukku. In Kemet they were the Hands of Aten. Those the Announced of the Achaemenid Empire found in Israel and Judah used many of the terms used in modern times, for reasons that will become quite apparent soon enough. As the religions they wrapped around themselves as protection began to reach friendly terms and share theology with one another, the Announced did so as well.<br /> <br /> Some Announced organizations will even take credit for the prophet Daniel and his fabled rise amongst the ranks of the court of King Darius the Mede, who is theorized by mortal scholars to have been the legendary Cyrus the Great. Some will even claim that the angel who protected Daniel in the lions\u2019 den was a member of their group, and a colleague of Daniel, who served as a priest of Ahura Mazda- one of the Magi. Some will go a step farther- claiming that Cyrus the Great himself was Irin. Regardless of the truth, the Achaemenid Empire was a place of great religious tolerance and cultural diversity, and the relative peace and stability of the region enabled for a great, united Irin society, above, even, the days of the cult of Anu. This was united society of the sort that entered myth and ideology of a supernatural people, like the Camarilla of the Kindred. And then came Alexander.<br /> <br /> In less than a decade, Alexander tore apart the Achaemenid Empire like it was made out of tissue paper. The legendary society of the Announced collapsed dramatically beneath Macedonian phalanxes. After numerous battles in the shadows of Persian nights, the Irin found themselves ousted from positions of power, and desperately searching for places where they could conceal their precious knowledge. Small groups of Irin, heavily loaded with crates of clay tablets and papyrus scrolls looked for temples, tombs, even caves where the history and lore of their people could be preserved for the future, and retrieved at a later date. Some scatter to the northwest, heading into Armenia. A grand majority flee towards the desert, where they would later insinuate themselves into the Nabataean culture. Another large portion move to Israel; where the Irin had friends and colleagues, as well as an established society, while an equal number stayed in Persia and tried to fly under the radar. Many more fled east, ahead of Alexander\u2019s armies, and hid themselves in India and the Himalayas.<br /> <br /> For a very long time, the Announced found themselves dodging the more numerous supernatural groups. Sumerian vampires insinuated themselves into the new courts of Babylon and Persia. Uratha fought tooth and nail against a flood of spirits of war, death, and violence to try and preserve whatever was left of the spirit ecology. Mages warred back and forth, with the Diadochi precursors to the Seer Ministries dueling with the young and newly congealed Diamond Orders and Scelesti cults from Sumeria. In the midst of all of this, the Announced tried to keep a low profile, and there was always a mission to keep them busy. With all the things they were ordered to bear witness to and clean up after, there was very little time to even try and build a cohesive society for themselves. However, that all changed in the first century BC.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Light of God</b><br /> <br /> There were two massive developments that came out of the exodus of the Announced from the collapsing Persian Empire. One was that a group of Irin who had set up in Nabataea actually managed to find Azazel, who\u2019d established a rather nice estate for himself in the desert. Some brave souls began to interview him, when he was in a good mood, and developed the first genuine lore about their murky origins- things that even the greatest of their scholars didn\u2019t know. This seminal work became known as the <i>Book of the Watchers</i>, and eventually formed the basis for the later <i>Book of Enoch</i>. This knowledge eventually made its way across the Aramaic-speaking world, and then spread farther, as Announced made copies for other Announced, who then spread it to the religions they used to conceal themselves. The <i>Book of Enoch</i> became a point of pride for the Announced, but also a point of great shame. Irin who believed themselves better than humanity used the doctrine of Original Sin to justify their superiority complex, and while the <i>Book of Enoch</i> gave them answers; to accept it they had to give up their ability to wash their hands of the state of the world and blame it on mortals. The <i>Book of Enoch</i>, and by extension Azazel and the Grigori, places the responsibility for the Deluge quite squarely on the shoulders of the Irin.<br /> <br /> In time, lore regarding the Announced followed the <i>Book of Enoch </i>through the Aramaic-speaking world. Those who would oppose beings that sought to lure the people of many nations away from their homes and their gods were met first by knowledgeable hunters, and then by those with power. No one is really sure where ordinary mortals found <i>any</i> of this, except that in time they came together and pursued those beings who would call themselves divine. Announced, Arisen, or even stranger beings, they pursued them all. Some rumors would suggest that these hunters were the heirs of an ancient organization called the Strangers that would appear to have collapsed in the Second Sothic Turn, but no one is really sure. What <i>is </i>known is that this group knew more than enough to make it dangerous. They possessed relics and knowledge, names and power. They could press the Announced into service; they could work the Fire in alchemical processes, and they had enough contacts that those foolish enough to reveal their Name and Title would swiftly be bound to the will of these formidable mortals. Many say that they had a hand in developing the Essene sect, and that in caverns near where the Dead Sea Scrolls were unearthed, some of their knowledge remains.<br /> <br /> At the very end of the first century BC, the world was beginning to change and only the Irin knew it. For the Irin of ancient days, and even now, it is blatantly obvious in their view that the angels of the New Testament are <i>qashmallim</i> and Irin. For many, there has never been any doubt. Irin legend speaks of three ancient Irin, some of the last who remembered the glory days of the Zoroastrian Announced under the banner of Persia, who followed a <i>qashmal</i> from where they lived to a sleepy town called Bethlehem. Many Announced believed that they gave the popular names of the Magi from the names of these legendary Irin- Balthazar, Caspar, and Melchior.<br /> <br /> It is a popular legend that Yeshuah ben Yosef, eventually known to the world as Jesus Christ, was a student of the Essenes, and later a friend to the Irin. Hearsay speaks of ancient Announced who sat and listened to the Sermon on the Mount, or who sat and spoke with him. They would love to claim him as one of their own, but for some reason it never took off, and it never has. Some claim that there were Irin present at the Council of Nicaea, and attempted to guide the burgeoning Catholic Church in ways they thought he would want. In light of what followed, more cynical, young Irin believe these ancients abused their intimate knowledge of Christianity\u2019s origins to manipulate it in a way that benefitted them the best.<br /> <br /> The Announced, as a whole, adore organized religion. More than simply giving them purpose, they can surround themselves in it for understanding, for protection, and most importantly for power. The Catholic Church, the Abbasid Dynasty, the Kingdoms of Israel and Judah- and that is solely among the monotheistic religions. The Announced, standing so close to the Divine, are endlessly drawn to it. Even those who do not use, believe in, or practice a single religion have some opinion on the subject. Religion is an inescapable facet of Announced existence, and for all the comfort it can provide it leads to disturbing questions as well. For millennia, the Shedim have been scorned, hunted, and killed by their own people for having the audacity to have exited the Confirmation with a closer tie to Flux than Azoth, and thus being branded demon, <i>druj</i>, and shaytan. While political divisions and shadow wars have shook the world of the Announced from time to time, most of them boil down to \u201cI\u2019m going to take your house, and God says it\u2019s okay.\u201d<br /> <br /> <b>Today</b><br /> <br /> In modern nights the Announced still wander, pondering the purpose of their long-lived (but not eternal) existence in the wake of reasonable downtime between the Principle\u2019s calls to serve. The older Announced isolate themselves, fearing a world that is changing more quickly now than ever before. Younger Announced wonder about what it means to be a divinity in an era when more and more people are moving away from needing a religion, and still crave cosmic answers. How many angels can dance on a quark? Where in the universe is the Pleroma and Sheol? And the most frightening question of all- is a god more or less than a man.<br /> <br /> <b>Origin of Glory</b><br /> <br /> The Confirmation is a moment of sublime glory. However, it is important to note that neither \u2018sublime\u2019 nor \u2018glory\u2019 is a strictly positive thing. Moby-Dick, the terrifying white whale, is said to be sublime in his wrath. The God of the Old Testament is, by nature, sublime- even when annihilating entire cities and devastating nations. But then there\u2019s the sublime beauty of the Grand Canyon, or of an exceptional musical performance. Point being, while the sublime is often subjective, there is very little that is generally considered moral or even human about it. The Confirmation is brought on, ultimately, by a desperate need for the sublime in some vital aspect of life. A potential Announced who needs an exceptional exam mark just to make it through the grade doesn\u2019t fit the qualifications- but one who needs it to get into Stanford University will. The quality, relationship, activity, or action that requires an influx of sublime glory can\u2019t just be in the moment- it needs to be a defining portion of the individual\u2019s identity. The artists who define themselves by how their art is perceived, the academics who <i>need</i> to be the smartest person in the room, the romantic for whom this one individual is their entire life.<br /> <br /> Threatening the loss of this significant pillar of their identity, or seeking to push it further than ever before, will trigger the Confirmation. The means by which the Confirmation is triggered will affect the Vocation- which affects the kind of Missions they will receive and the Dominions they have power over.<br /> <br /> The five Vocations are the <i>Ishim</i> (Souls of Fire), <i>Cherubim</i> (The Mighty), <i>Erelim</i> (The Valiant), <i>Elohim </i>(Divinities), and <i>Malakim</i> (Messenger/The Lordly Ones). However, among their own people they are commonly known as Torches, Wardens, Visionaries, Powers, and Princes.</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":4152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","user":"Arcanist","date":". 09-21-2015, 10:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-25T06:48:31-07:00","id":677578,"title":"","content":"And now, the first of the X-Splat, Vocations. As always, questions, comments, concerns, and other feedback is appreciated. What you like or don't like about the content, or any ideas you get from it, feel free to share; I can always use more feedback, and knowing if I need to fine-tune some aspects a bit more is always useful.<br /> <br /> <b>Vocations</b><br /> <br /> <i>Ishim</i>- The Souls of Fire, Servants of the Most High, the Torches<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">You fought all your life to obtain meaning, to solidify your identity, and then in a moment of perfection you became the best version of you that you could be. You found yourself, and you found principles. Now, you serve something bigger than your own identity- but you can\u2019t help but notice the details your mission seems to ignore, and one of these days you\u2019re going to let yourself get involved.<br /> <br /> <u>Annunciation</u>: The families the Torches come from are ordinary to the point of being bland. Average for the region to a disturbing degree, at least publically. They, and their children, are everymen and women. If you weren\u2019t paying attention, chances are you wouldn\u2019t notice them. In the best cases, their parents are self-sacrificing, responsible, disciplined, and determined. In less than positive situations, they can also be manipulative, disapproving, and in multiple kinds of trouble. An Ishim with a more positive childhood could have parents who were working poor expecting them to better themselves to the point where they could proudly declare themselves \u201cself-made\u201d. One with a negative childhood could have decided it alone, as their parents were too drunk, too high, or too abusive to care. Regardless of circumstances, the Irin-to-be finds something and builds an identity around it- their chosen field or trade means <i>everything </i>to them. The high school artist whose friends rarely see them outside of the studio, and when they do they\u2019re just taking a needed break then heading right back. The academic who must know everything about their chosen field of study. The dancer or track star that practices to the point of utter perfection- every move must be flawless.<br /> <br /> <u>Confirmation</u>: Ultimately, the Confirmation is brought on by this obsession- their attempts to build an identity they can be proud of. Those who become <i>Ishim</i> are seeking to remake themselves, to turn disparate fragments into a united whole. The childhood is mostly just prelude- it is puberty, the soul searching for identity and meaning that comes with it, that will ultimately push them towards Confirmation. In the end, they are looking for a medium through which they can understand themselves.<br /> <br /> This process of obsession with the thing they\u2019ve decided defines their identity can ultimately render the <i>Ishim</i>, and to an extension all Irin, rather fragile mentally to the point of delusions and utter breakdown. Think of Nina from <i>Black Swan</i>, her relationship with her mother, and her need to become the perfect performer. The entire film could be seen, if she is interpreted as one of the <i>Ishim</i> as leading to her Confirmation, which the film closes on. As this example demonstrates, the Confirmation for the <i>Ishim</i> is an assertion of their identity and a perfection of their craft, they are ultimately fulfilled. Or, at least that\u2019s what they think, initially. Most <i>Ishim</i> actually ignore their first mission for a while, sometimes to the point of disobedience, because they are enjoying the sense of fulfillment which is swiftly dwindling as they realize that they no longer have a purpose now that they\u2019ve accomplished their goal. Their obsession is still a major part of the identity, but the fact they typically don\u2019t <i>grow</i> in it anymore drives them inexorably towards the purpose offered by their missions. Many <i>Ishim</i> are either happy to serve because it fills a void where dedication to reaching a goal once was, or see service as their price for being able to use their sublime supernatural nature to drive their chosen obsession into greater and greater displays of perfection.<br /> <br /> <u>Favored Maxim</u>: Neshama (Seat of Understanding)<br /> <br /> <u>Exousia &amp; Temptation</u>: As people who have worked within a set of rules their entire life and excelled in spite of them, the Torches have little need to chafe against the will of the Principle provided they can comprehend the reasons for Its commands. Other Announced will often state derisively- behind their back or to their face -that they\u2019re squandering their newfound divinity, and that they\u2019re nothing better than servants, or even slaves, to the will of the Principle. The <i>Ishim</i> don\u2019t see it that way. Their new status was a gift, a completion of their identity. Those who didn\u2019t realize the change or receive a mission until after they began to feel something missing from their lives in the wake of completing their goal may even see it as salvation- saving them from a downward spiral into depression and a meaningless life.<br /> <br /> As to those who say they obey blindly, they are rarely- if ever -privy to the inner thoughts of the Torches. Most struggle fiercely for the first few months, burning themselves early and losing their freedom. The Torches are the Announced most likely to try and determine rhyme or reason behind the will of the Principle, and once they come to an explanation that satisfies them, they rarely see reason to question It again unless something challenges their perceptions. The <i>Ishim</i> are obsessive and detail-oriented by nature, and many prefer to get close to their mission before, or after the event. Either to prepare the individuals involved peripherally for what\u2019s to come, or comfort them afterwards. This habit will often lead them to question the justness of the Principle\u2019s actions, as the <i>Ishim</i> often bear witness to suicides, senseless accidents, car crashes, drive bys, and just about anything in between.<br /> <br /> The Torches falter when their personal obsessions come into play, they stumble when others are prevented from pursuing their own goals- the things that define them. When the way they look at the world is challenged, their means of coping with their strange new life collapse. They\u2019ll risk everything when their identity is at risk, or those they have become emotionally connected are in danger. Beyond anything, the Torches are <i>terrified</i> of losing themselves. When they fall into Temptation, Torches recede within themselves, pursuing their obsessions with almost mindless focus. They will expend power relentlessly in the pursuit of their goals, sacrificing Lux and Glory to their chosen profession and their loved ones.<br /> <br /> <u>Dominions</u>: The <i>Ishim</i> exclusively study the Dominion of the Conflagration and the Dominion of Portents.<br /> <br /> <u>Character Creation</u>: The <i>Ishim</i> come from many different paths, but they are united by their obsessions. Their Attributes should reflect this according to the thing they defined themselves by, though it is important to note most <i>Ishim</i> commonly have Social Attributes as their secondary category. Most prefer Finesse over Power or Resistance. It\u2019s incredibly rare to see an <i>Ishim</i> without the Area of Expertise or Encyclopedic Knowledge merits, and other Mental merits meant to aid with observation are also common. Other merits should be chosen to reflect the way they complete their missions, or their life before the Confirmation. Many <i>Ishim</i> have the Hobbyist Clique merit, as well as the Sympathetic merit- the last is especially important for those <i>Ishim</i> who decide to get closer <i>after</i> the event they\u2019re meant to witness has occurred.<br /> <br /> <u>Concepts</u>: Grief counselor, family therapist, personable mortician, \u201cnightcrawler\u201d, noted academic, popular local dancer, former track star, understanding deacon<br /> <br /> <u>Stereotypes</u><ul><li><b><i>Erelim</i></b><b>:</b> Their ego is going to get someone killed, and I\u2019ll have to watch.</li> <li><b><i>Elohim</i></b><b>:</b> They think we serve them- let them keep thinking it.</li> <li><b><i>Malakim</i></b><b>:</b> I\u2019d want to <i>be</i> them- if they didn\u2019t make people a mere detail.</li> <li><b><i>Cherubim</i></b><b>:</b> Do you know when to stop?</li> </ul><ul><li><b>Vampires:</b> I\u2019ve seen what\u2019s left of a family once they\u2019re done with someone. Maybe it\u2019s time someone returned the favor.</li> <li><b>Werewolves: </b>More like a force of nature than a person. You don\u2019t want to see what I\u2019ve seen.</li> <li><b>Mages: </b>They make the same mistakes over and over, but act like they\u2019re the only ones to ever have this happen. Dangerous to themselves, even more so to others.</li> <li><b>Changelings:</b> Broken toys trying to make themselves whole- I\u2019d help, if I wasn\u2019t supposed to watch you destroy yourself.</li> <li><b>Mortals:</b> Why do I never see you smile?</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <i>Erelim</i>- The Valiant, Great Heroes, Visionaries<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">You are a lover of spectacle. More than that, you like to be the center of attention, you love competition, and you love the feeling you get from encouraging other people to rise to the occasion. Your accomplishments mean everything to you, and your reputation must be beyond reproach- now more so than ever. As a divine agent, your reputation must be spotless and you must hold yourself to a code, but what happens when your code and the Principle don\u2019t line up?<br /> <br /> <u>Annunciation</u>: If the <i>Ishim</i> are meant to disappear into the crowd, move unseen and unheard, then the <i>Erelim</i> are meant to never have the public eye leave them if they can help it. In the best cases, well-meaning parents or teachers push the <i>Erelim</i> into the spotlight of excellence. In the worst, it\u2019s a refuge to hide deep-seated shame, abuse, or other mental scars. At one time, the Announced couldn\u2019t or wouldn\u2019t act- and then they decided that had to change, pursuing a passion that gave them their freedom with wild abandon. And it isn\u2019t enough for the \u201chometown hero\u201d football captain, the adolescent rock star, the brooding Byronic Poet, or the omnipresent socialite to bask in the glow of their own freedom and success, they want other to acknowledge it and, if possible, join the party. The hometown hero sees himself like an old commanding officer, a father to his men, and sees it as his duty to bring them greatness. The rock star or the poet believes in taking some lucky good-looking outcast under their wing. The socialite feels the need to defend her cliques viciously, and is prone to expanding them by making the unpopular girl her \u2018project\u2019.<br /> <br /> <u>Confirmation</u>: The Confirmation of the <i>Erelim</i> is brought on by the supreme expression of their freedom, as they understand it, in conjunction with their goal of elevating and glorifying themselves and others. As such, the <i>Erelim</i> tend to be Confirmed younger than most Announced. They want their names to live forever, or at least be the guy or girl everyone will be talking about for a very long time. The core of the Visionary Confirmation is competition- it isn\u2019t the goal that matters, but the journey to reach it. A Torch, a Visionary, and a Power could all struggle to be the best athlete they can- but the Torch would focus on their own physicality, and the Power would focus on recognition. Only the Visionary would care about the sport itself, or at least the pursuit of it.<br /> <br /> In this way, the attitudes of the Visionaries are often more comparable to Bronze Age warriors than they are to modern men and women. There are very few things that matter to the <i>Erelim</i>- their skill, their passions, and the hallmarks of the respect others have for them. Awards, trophies, certificates, all these things are more than keepsakes to them; they\u2019re tangible ties to their own identity. Like Achilles in the Iliad, their keepsakes, the people they have relationships with, and the stories told about them are extensions of themselves. Someone who spreads gossip will be treated almost as ruthlessly as someone who threatens bodily harm, and none are treated worse than those who threaten their fans or hangers on. The Confirmation is merely their greatest triumph. Winning nationals, heading their own local show, getting a poem published and recognized, prom. Their reputation and success manages to propel them to a kind of fragile immortality in the local consciousness, and with that- they become something <i>more</i>. After this supreme success, many become disenchanted and unhappily seek bigger and better things to attempt. They lash out at their friends or their adoring fans, and seek to find something to fill that need for a challenge- to do something noteworthy. And that is where the Principle and its missions come in.<br /> <br /> <u>Favored Maxim</u>: Ruach (Spirit of Vigor)<br /> <br /> <u>Exousia &amp; Temptation</u>: The passions of the Visionaries hover close to the surface, even for one of the Announced. Of all the Vocations, they are the ones that are most likely to chafe at inaction- at being forced to wait until some travesty occurs before being given license to use their not inconsiderable abilities. The reason for their enthusiasm is rather simple, and it\u2019s that they feel more at home performing various acts of heroism and following archaic codes than they do being John or Jane Doe Perfect from Hicksville. They don\u2019t want to be just a familiar face, or be remembered for things they now believe were simply trivial- they want to become immortal on a reputation for true heroism.<br /> <br /> Many assume the <i>Erelim</i> to be headstrong, arrogant- and in a way they would be right -but the Visionaries wish more than anything to act, and fear nothing except for the loss of freedom. As a result, Visionaries burned early by loss of Exousia will keep themselves tightly on a leash, fighting every instinct they possess until the time has come, and then they turn themselves loose. Some eventually turn their missions into a game, racing against the clock to save those the mission indicates will die, or otherwise prevent the tragedy fated to occur. Naturally, this is a massive breach of Exousia- if God says you\u2019re supposed to die, you\u2019re supposed to die, and no lowly angel should be trying to go against it. Eventually, those <i>Erelim</i> whose code has been broken and whose faith has been shaken may descend into outright rebellion, which is an easy way to become Shackled. But more than that, when missions begin to indicate tragedy in the people around them, rare is the Visionary who doesn\u2019t think that an exception should be made.<br /> <br /> Visionaries falter when something pulls at their heartstrings. When they\u2019ve gone too long without acting, or they make the mistake of getting emotionally involved early on, things can go disastrously wrong. When their passions are denied, they try to find another outlet, and that often leads to trouble. More than anything, the Visionaries are <i>terrified</i> at the idea of total inaction, of loss of freedom. Having come from a position where they couldn\u2019t or wouldn\u2019t act, they never want to go back. A Visionary fallen to Temptation is an impulsive tempest, a raging inferno of emotion and Lux. They will sacrifice Glory and Lux to indulge their need to feel free.<br /> <br /> <u>Dominions</u>: The <i>Erelim</i> exclusively study the Dominion of the Crucible and the Dominion of the Firmament.<br /> <br /> <u>Character Creation</u>: It is rare to find a Visionary who doesn\u2019t have Physical or Social Attributes as their primary category, and for the Power Attributes to possess the highest ratings. The rest will be distributed according to their lifestyle, and the field in which they\u2019ve chosen to elevate themselves. The skills needed to accomplish their own self-aggrandizement will be well developed, but any that don\u2019t involve their chosen field will have suffered poorly. They will often have the Fast Reflexes or Indomitable Mental merits, a sturdy repertoire of Physical merits and an extensive list of Contacts or Allies. Many have some level of Fame, or a True Friend. Striking Looks and Inspiring are also common merits. Keep in mind a good reputation doesn\u2019t indicate capability. An egotistical bodybuilder is about as likely to be able to free run as the next guy- probably less likely. But that doesn\u2019t mean his reputation as an athlete isn\u2019t stellar.<br /> <br /> <u>Concepts</u>: Prom queen, hometown hero, vain athlete, frat boy, motivational speaker, hired bodyguard, hitman with a code, hyper-disciplined student of physiology, wrathful vigilante, Machiavellian socialite<br /> <br /> <br /> <u>Stereotypes</u><ul><li><b><i>Ishim</i></b>: Impressive dedication, but they\u2019re lacking in ambition</li> <li><b><i>Elohim</i></b>: Sure, go ahead and play at being a god while I live like one</li> <li><b><i>Malakim</i></b><b>:</b> I\u2019d follow them anywhere, if they ever actually did anything</li> <li><b><i>Cherubim</i></b><b>:</b> I\u2019m not going to sit and wait for something to go wrong</li> </ul><ul><li><b>Vampires:</b> They understand us, they just don\u2019t care- watch what you say, and especially what you do. Honor is a cheap currency with them.</li> <li><b>Werewolves:</b> I\u2019ve put a few down- worthy opponents, all of them. Never turn your back.</li> <li><b>Mages: </b>We\u2019d get along, if they stopped trying to enslave us. Never let them get close to you.</li> <li><b>Sin-Eaters:</b> You know what it is to feel deeply, and fear inaction. I think I\u2019ll buy you a drink.</li> <li><b>Mortals: </b>Anything for a fan, let\u2019s get out of here.</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-30T13:17:35-07:00","id":683461,"title":"","content":"More Vocations. Commentary is always appreciated, even harsh commentary; don't be shy.<br /> <br /> <i>Elohim</i>- The Gods, Lords of the Earth, Powers<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">You are a figure of power, a ruler of men, a master of fundamental forces of the universe. Deserving of your authority, you became unshakeable in your beliefs- God has appointed you to be a representative. The kings of old, who claimed to rule by divine right, had no true understanding of the concept. But in spite of your power and authority, you are compelled not to act by the very being which gave you authority. To disobey is to lose agency, and loss of agency is loss of power. But how long will you allow your rule to be denied?<br /> <br /> <u>Annunciation</u>: Heavy is the head that wears the crown. The childhood of the Powers is rarely a happy one. Whether they are given their power by the family of their birth, or take it by force later, their families are rarely what could be termed functional. The Announced early on realizes they have no control over their life- either their family, their circumstances, or both conspire to limit what they can do. They find the one thing they <i>can</i> control, and pursue it until they feel totally secure in it. No rivals, no challenges, no limits. The Powers know that they are deserving of the authority they\u2019ve been given, and they wield it with casual arrogance and utter confidence. Massive pressure was put upon them in their youth to gain power, either by way or birthright and responsibility, or simply to prevent victimization. While all Announced tend to crave power and authority, the <i>Elohim</i> take it a step further. They are trust fund kids who actually know how to apply leverage, high school Machiavellis, public officials who use every inch of the grasp they\u2019ve been given.<br /> <br /> <u>Confirmation</u>: The Powers tend to be Confirmed later in life, when they have the security and confidence to truly feel limitless. It is more than a single goal or achievement that triggers this feeling. <i>Elohim</i> make entire careers out of their pursuits, not resting until they are an unequivocal expert, capable of going toe-to-toe with others of similar experience. They seek out the authorities and officials of their chosen field, and instead of ingratiating themselves, or yielding to their authority, they butt heads until the Announced is <i>finally</i> recognized as an equal. As such, the <i>Elohim</i> tend to be Confirmed later in life than other Announced.<br /> <br /> They truly believe in the divine right of kings, especially as it applies to them, and they would love to be recognized for it- if it weren\u2019t for the fact that it would be an admission of their limitations; that they require the recognition of others on which to rest their self-worth. When a Power is Confirmed, there comes a soul-crushing moment where they realize that limitless authority, limitless pursuit, doesn\u2019t suit them. Without challengers, without potential conquests, the freedom they sought in destroying limits is pointless. They have no outlet in which they can push their boundaries. Now that they\u2019ve conquered all that they wished to conquer, they can either learn to be satisfied, or find some other kind of conquest to engage in. The highly successful businessman might become a prolific lothario; the politician might make an irrational crusade against some facet of society, the shadowy mob boss might ruthlessly \u2018civilize\u2019 crime in his city. Combined with divine right, and <i>noblesse oblige</i>, the <i>Elohim</i> often see themselves as nothing less than God-given kings to mere mortals.<br /> <br /> <u>Favored Maxim</u>: Chaya (Life Divine)<br /> <br /> <u>Exousia &amp; Temptation</u>: Though the Powers claim they look for a world without boundaries, they will never be happy without boundaries they can push. Of all the Vocations, they chafe most at limitation. They want to exercise authority more than anything, and they want to prove that anything holding them back is merely a delay. Where other Announced would stop, seeing an insurmountable challenge, the <i>Elohim</i> forge ahead. The need for recognition and to push at limits is still there, only now they are harnessed to the Divine Fire and the competition are all minor gods. The game is same, its only the stakes that are different.<br /> <br /> Many assume the Powers are arrogant and irresponsible- that to a degree is true -but the Powers don\u2019t usually spend Glory just for the sake of it. In order to push at boundaries, they must first find them, and the Divine Fire has very few, possibly none. The Powers are constantly trying to push the Announced condition to new heights, positive that there is more Glory and Lux can do than merely serve their purposes as needed. The <i>Elohim</i> are the source of astonishing wonders and mind-numbing horrors, using the might of their Dominions, Edicts, and Celestial Forms to astonishing applications. While being the most willing to spend Glory, the Powers are also the most focused on holding onto it- and are always looking for means to prolong their mystical might. The Powers are always willing to risk Exousia, but most will store it up, using loopholes and exceptions to the rules to avoid risking themselves. The <i>Elohim</i> feel the most sympathy and kinship for Metatron, and by playing by Its rules they hope to receive Its respect and eventually match it on an equal playing field. At some level, every Power knows their connection to the Divine Fire makes it possible for them to challenge the Prodigal Demiurge.<br /> <br /> The Powers falter when their capability is called into question, they stumble when something or someone gets in the way of their ambitions. When they hit a wall, their response is usually to call up a flood to shatter it rather than walk around or find another path. More than anything, the Powers are <i>terrified</i> of losing control, and will do anything to protect or assure control- whether it\u2019s in a broader social sense, or a more individual field. A Power fallen to Temptation is an egotistical deity, a being with no boundaries, no limits. They will do anything and everything to defy their challengers and assert their freedoms- they will burn through Glory and Lux to shout their independence to the heavens even as Exousia bleeds away and Chains wrap tight.<br /> <br /> <u>Dominions</u>: The Powers exclusively study the Dominion of Divinity and the Dominion of the Depths.<br /> <br /> <u>Character Creation</u>: It is rare to find one of the <i>Elohim</i> without Social or Mental Attributes as both their primary, and secondary traits- with the Power Attribute favored in their primary category and Resistance Attribute in their secondary. However, just because the Powers prefer to rule doesn\u2019t mean they can\u2019t put up a real fight- many notable rulers throughout history were also legendary conquerors. Political skill is usually very high, along with Persuasion, Intimidation, Socialize, and Subterfuge. Any other skills fit as per their character and personal history. They will often have Professional Training, possess the Indomitable Merit, and own a fairly impressive Library. Resources are rarely a question for them, and they typically have Retainers or Staff to aid them, as well as a plethora of Allies and Contacts. Nor is it unheard of for <i>Elohim</i>, especially those born into privilege, to possess a Mentor and most have rather high levels of Status.<br /> <br /> <u>Concepts</u>: Civilized mob boss, conquering business tycoon, tyrannical public servant, politically involved cleric, exhausted Casanova, social engineer, divine representative, supernatural diplomat, responsible noble, paranoid judge<br /> <br /> <u>Stereotypes</u><ul><li><b><i>Ishim</i></b>: Noble, steadfast allies- if you can earn their loyalty</li> <li><b><i>Bene Elohim</i></b>: Arrogant pretenders, but useful tools</li> <li><b><i>Malakim</i></b>: Rebels for the sake of revolution, they risk all that we work for</li> <li><b><i>Cherubim</i></b>: You want to protect, but you\u2019re built to destroy</li> </ul><ul><li><b>Vampires:</b> A pestilence and plague- little better than cancerous rebels. Stamp them out with fire and don\u2019t play at court; they know the game.</li> <li><b>Werewolves:</b> Dangerous barbarians perpetually at the gates; make it known to them you won\u2019t suffer their presence in your borders.</li> <li><b>Mages: </b>We could stand on equal footing, you and I. Which is exactly why I should have representation to your betters.</li> <li><b>Mummies:</b> Lost gods; the more powerful they are, the less they know themselves. What does that say about us?</li> <li><b>Mortals:</b> Weak and inconstant, why do you matter so much?</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div><i>Malakim</i>- Messengers or Lordly Ones, Children of Dawn, the Princes<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">You have a fire in your soul that just won\u2019t die and a cause that won\u2019t help itself. Unlike other petty and selfish Announced, you have purpose, direction, mission. You inflamed yourself with it, immolated in its blaze, and now you can direct that fire. Nothing on earth can stop you from seeing your ideal world coming to fruition. But candles that burn twice as bright last half as long. More importantly, they would do well to remember another who was called Son of the Morning. How long will you obey, when God is demanding your cause fall in favor of another mission?<br /> <br /> <u>Annunciation</u>: The Princes are born to fanatics, revolutionaries, and madmen. They can be Announced to just about anyone, so long as they have a passionate tie to some sort of cause that they are guaranteed to try and pass on to their children. The worth of the cause, or its morality, has no bearing whatsoever on the will of the Principle. Their childhood is, to them, idyllic. They\u2019ll rarely- if ever -speak ill of their parents, but childhood lessons to outside observers can look uncomfortably like indoctrination on closer inspection. As children, they always were intently searching for something, and were content to take on the cause of their parents. As young adults, they will often adapt and change this cause, and begin to take action. There are exceptional children everywhere who foster a cause; child soldiers who run underground rescue teams to save their fellows diving time and again into hostile camps, activists willing to get shot for their cause and continue to act in defiance. However, it doesn\u2019t need to be nearly so dramatic, but it must engender true passion- which is where Confirmation comes in.<br /> <br /> <u>Confirmation</u>: The Princes are supremely attractive to other people. Their passion is infectious, their drive is electric, and to people looking at them from the outside they can look flawless. They are looking for a way to express their cause, to bring it to fruition, and in finding it they exult in Glory. But when they do, they often come to understand how there is something deeply wrong with their cause, and by extension themselves. They see in those fires of Divinity, wherein they enacted their own cause to the extreme without heed to any of its ramifications, their own damnation. Pushing harder and harder for an idea that they may, ultimately, hate when they bring it to fruition. Some decide that they are simply too far gone down this path to turn back now- that they must see their cause come to pass. Others see the flaw in themselves, not their ideals, and seek to elevate themselves to the ideal. Still others believe that they must abandon their old cause, and seek a righteous one- and that is where the Principle and its missions come in.<br /> <br /> The main trigger for a Prince\u2019s Confirmation is passion. While this is superficially similar to the <i>Erelim</i>, whose passions define them, the situation is quite different. Passion self-contained does nothing, but passion <i>spread</i>, passion <i>ignited</i>, can change the world. When the words of the <i>Malakim</i> inspire, and spread their desire to act, Glory is called down. \u2018The Cause\u2019 is everything to the Princes. Loss of life, individual damage, all these things will weigh on their conscience, but will ultimately be found wanting. Threaten \u2018The Cause\u2019 and expect to be met with lethal force.<br /> <br /> <u>Favored Maxim</u>: Yechida (Oneness)<br /> <br /> <u>Exousia &amp; Temptation</u>: The Princes are, inherently, rebellious. The kind of cause that sparks the necessary drive to lead to Confirmation rarely relates to the status quo. More than anything, they chafe at forbiddance- of being ordered not to do something, of being forbidden to feel a certain way, of pursuing certain courses of action. As such, they find it difficult to live by the dictates of Exousia, typically finding <i>something </i>objectionable about it or the missions they must undertake. They are totally willing to find a third, fourth, or even fifth option. There is no mission they won\u2019t attempt to solve in their own way. After all, orchestrating the death of the man who is to cause mayhem in the city is a far lesser sin against Exousia and the plans of the Principle than killing him yourself. More than that, they are willing to change the parameters of missions and interfere in the destinies of others.<br /> <br /> The <i>Malakim</i> are always rushing about, confident in their own code of ethics and their cause, attempting to mold others within that framework. They are firebrands who declare change is coming in the heavens, they are revolutionaries who sweep into town, acquire a following, break the social order, and begin legends the Visionaries would give their right arms to be part of. \u2018The Cause\u2019 is everything to the Princes. Loss of life, individual damage, all these things will weigh on their conscience, but will ultimately be found wanting. Threaten \u2018The Cause\u2019 and expect to be met with lethal force. Word of passionate activists- or dangerous extremists -travels far and fast through the right channels but more than that the <i>Malakim</i> are known for being trouble. A Prince is a ticking time bomb in the supernatural community. How long before they incite the local <i>penitentes</i> against the Announced? How long until they go ballistic on the local Elysium? How long until they try to \u2018help\u2019 the Created by forcing mortals to live with them? The question is not <i>will</i> they try and start a revolution the question is <i>when</i>.<br /> <br /> The Princes falter when someone tells them \u2018no\u2019, they stumble when their cause is threatened. To be forbidden something is to encourage a Prince to pursue it, to threaten their cause is to invite disaster down on your head. The Princes sort everyone they meet into potential threats or potential allies, and disagreeing without a lot of personal connection to bank on is an easy way to be reclassified from friend to enemy. More than anything, the Princes are terrified that all their efforts are for nothing- that their cause is not worth their devotion. A Prince fallen to Temptation is an unrelenting force acting in favor of their goals. They are a one-man revolution, a storm of Glory, striding implacably forwards in the service of their ideals and casting aside anything in their way.<br /> <br /> <u>Dominions</u>: The Princes exclusively study the Dominion of the Luminaries and the Dominion of Humanity.<br /> <br /> <u>Character Creation</u>: Even if Social Attributes are not the primary category, it would be remiss to have a Prince without a decent Presence score. After that, the Attributes shake out according to the kind of person the <i>Malakim</i> was before the Confirmation. Skills tend to progress similarly, though they usually have high scores in Occult, Empathy, Persuasion, and Intimidation. Many have Striking Looks, but it is not required, and usually possess Alternate Identities, Anonymity, and Status in various groups related to their cause. Some may even possess Mystery Cult initiation, though obviously any supernatural benefits to this are a moot point now that they are Announced. Other than that, the only other fairly constant merit for the Princes would be Inspiring. Style merits fit as per the person they were before the Confirmation, though many have Fast-Talking.<br /> <br /> <u>Concepts</u>: Frothing fanatic, freedom fighter, anarchist, earnest evangelist, apocalyptic street preacher, political activist, ambitious Mafioso, lone wanderer, Nietzsche wannabe, aggressive moralist<br /> <br /> <u>Stereotypes</u><ul><li><b><i>Ishim</i></b><b>:</b> Focused and skilled, they should apply themselves to greater purpose</li> <li><b><i>Erelim</i></b><b>:</b> You\u2019d be of use, if you stopped worrying about your own glory</li> <li><b><i>Elohim</i></b>: We are coming to shake your kingdom, tyrant</li> <li><b><i>Cherubim</i></b>: You are admirable- now if only you\u2019d expand your horizons</li> </ul><ul><li><b>Vampires:</b> We could sort out those seeking redemption from the pitiless monsters, but it would be easier to burn them all. God will know his own.</li> <li><b>Werewolves:</b> Too wild and uncontrolled to truly ascribe to any cause but their own, they are dangerous to everyone else.</li> <li><b>Mages:</b> Here are people who understand ideals. If only you\u2019d stop profaning them</li> <li><b>Prometheans:</b> We are on the same journey, you and I. So why do I want to fill you with the Fire until you burst?</li> <li><b>Mortals:</b> So small, so broken, and yet I need you to define me</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-02T12:16:43-07:00","id":686045,"title":"","content":"The last of the Vocations, the Cherubim. This is a new one that was missing from the first version of the fansplat, but I feel it's something that adds rather than detracts from the game.<br /> <br /> <i>Cherubim</i>- The Mighty, Bell-Ringers, Wardens<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">There is one thing that anchors your soul, one thing you can depend on in this world. While other Announced move about, inconstant, pursuing their passions to wit\u2019s end, you are constant as stone- stable as a mountain. You were immolated in the blaze set by those who tried to take the foundation of your soul, but instead of being consumed, you were tempered. With a will and form of steel, you will not be moved. Nothing will ever shake your foundation again- not even the thing that others call God.<br /> <br /> <u>Annunciation</u>: The Wardens didn\u2019t have much consistency in their lives. They moved around a lot, maybe their parents lived paycheck to paycheck. Perhaps they spent some time homeless. Whatever the case, they had one stable thing in their life. Maybe it was a person, maybe it was a location, maybe it was something else- but it usually has a physical representation of some kind. Whatever was constant for them, whatever acted like a mooring in the tumultuous sea of their life, means everything to them and they will protect it with everything they\u2019ve got. They are activists preserving historic neighborhoods; they are individuals of faith protecting the sanctity of a house of worship; they are true champions for the arts; they are the demanding exes that will never let go.<br /> <br /> <u>Confirmation</u>: They had a foundation, a rock, something they could cling to in times of trouble. And then one day something came along and threatened to take it away. Maybe the community centre was going to be torn down, maybe the house of worship was threatened by fire, maybe the art gallery was going to sell its best pieces. Maybe the ex was going to leave town. Whatever the case, the Warden saw they were going to lose the thing that had kept them sane, and they couldn\u2019t let it go. Somehow, they made it stay. Exactly <i>how</i> is a matter of debate but the main factor in the Confirmation of a Warden is stability. So long as they keep what they\u2019re looking for consistent, it defines them- differentiates them. An Announced obsessed with their craft could be <i>any</i> of the Vocations- but it is only when they actively move to protect it that they become Wardens.<br /> <br /> The Wardens refuse to move in the face of adversity. They saw in the fires of Divinity wholeness and union that boggles the mind. They knew that they had the power to bring things together, to make them whole, and they would never have to worry about lacking in stability ever again. They are as unshakeable as a mountain, and as dependable. The mountain is never inconstant, and whatever falls under its shadow can be assured of its shelter for as long as it persists. The <i>Cherubim</i> are always looking for people, places, or things in need of their surety. It isn\u2019t enough for them to just protect themselves, not when they know there are people out there who are living lives just as tumultuous as they once had. They are constantly looking to extend their protection, offering sanctuary to those who desire it, and those they believe need it, whether it\u2019s true or not.<br /> <br /> <u>Favored Maxim</u>: Nefesh (Living Soul)<br /> <br /> <u>Exousia and Temptation</u>: Wardens are protectors, guardians. They prefer to wait from the sidelines, hoarding power and license to act until such a time as they feel a need to stretch their metaphysical muscles. The <i>Cherubim</i> find it easy to stay within the boundaries of Exousia- waiting and watching until they believe someone needs their protection, or something encroaches upon their own domain. Most see no reason to push themselves and endanger those under their \u2018care\u2019 and prefer to wait until trouble comes to find them. They watch, they wait, and if someone involved in their mission pulls at their sympathy or falls under their perceived purview, they take them under their (both proverbial and literal) wing.<br /> <br /> The Wardens are introspective, they wait, and they examine themselves and others. When they are sure they understand, they act, though the revelations they come to discover can rarely be described. Of all the Vocations, the <i>Cherubim</i> have the most inscrutable motives, and are the least forthcoming to explain them- often because they cannot. Of all the Vocations, they feel the most kinship for the <i>qashmallim</i>, because they know what it is to have pertinent knowledge, total surety of principle, and be unable to explain it. Wardens have deep feelings of responsibility that hold them back from acting- they know what it\u2019s like to be without something to depend on, and seeing themselves as dependable they do not want to risk drawing attention to those under their protection for fear of bringing danger to their door. However, if they can rationalize their ambitions as protecting those they feel responsible for, they will not hesitate to act, moving implacably towards their goal- fighting with the tenacity of someone willing to die for others.<br /> <br /> The Wardens falter when someone leaves, when security is threatened. They stumble when someone needs it, and is denied it. To threaten someone\u2019s security, to throw chaos into another\u2019s life, is a quick way to make an implacable enemy of the <i>Cherubim</i>. The Wardens are frightened of failing others, and more than anything they are <i>terrified</i> of losing their own consistency. When life crumbles around them, the Wardens will risk Glory and Exousia to shore it up. A Warden fallen to Temptation is a paranoid guardian angel, proactively pursuing potential threats, eliminating perceived dangers, and virtually imprisoning those they would protect. A Warden\u2019s attention is a gilded cage, but it is a cage nonetheless.<br /> <br /> <u>Dominions</u>: The Wardens exclusively study the Dominion of Creation and the Dominion of Flesh.<br /> <br /> <u>Character Creation</u>: Regardless of what Attribute category is primary, the Wardens almost always favor Resistance Attributes. Other Attributes are spread along the lines of the person they were before Confirmation. Skills are spread around normally, but usually have high Survival, Streetwise and Stealth scores. Many dedicate lots of time and effort into making Safe Places, but it isn\u2019t required. Many often have the Indomitable Merit. Otherwise, build the character normally, taking their backstory into consideration.<br /> <br /> <u>Concepts</u>: Involved police officer, hidden sentry, women\u2019s shelter worker, stalker, pillar of the community, injustice collector, legal counsel, urban boogeyman, guerilla<br /> <br /> <u>Stereotypes</u><ul><li><b><i>Ishim</i></b><b>:</b> Selfish- you could be doing so much more.</li> <li><b><i>Erelim</i></b><b>:</b> What is strength without purpose?</li> <li><b><i>Elohim</i></b><b>:</b> Not my god.</li> <li><b><i>Malakim</i></b><b>:</b> What reason could you possibly have to be so smug?</li> </ul><ul><li><b>Vampires:</b> You\u2019re nothing but death. Kinder to destroy you.</li> <li><b>Werewolves:</b> You can\u2019t truly protect. The animal rules you.</li> <li><b>Mages:</b> You meddle in things that aren\u2019t your business. I have to do this. You don\u2019t. Why not be happy?</li> <li><b>Hunters:</b> You think you\u2019re a guardian angel? Right. Of <i>course</i> you are.</li> <li><b>Mortals:</b> Trust me- you want my help.</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> And now, for the new version of the Shedim. I tried writing them as protagonist material in the first version, but frankly they didn't really work. They were sympathetic, horrifying, and heart-breaking, but the attempt then to keep the Announced more human than most splats hampered the writing until I got a more clear idea of what I wanted them to do, what the themes of the game were, and what exactly the Divine Fire stands for in the World of Darkness. I reread the entire Promethean line with an eye for Flux when I was writing this, and hopefully it's better than the first try.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><i>Through a Mirror Darkly</i><br /> <br /> The Vocations above are described in terms of the Azothic Irin, but the patterns follow just as well for the Flux-based Shedim. Like all Announced, they are creatures of incredible passion, but the passions and obsessions of the Shedim focus on dissolution. Flux breaks down, it dissolves, it separates. The differences between the Irin and Shedim are subtle, a mere change in attitude. The goals and ambitions of the Shedim may, in fact, be more moral than those of the Irin- save that they are focused on tearing something down rather than building it up. Their passions might be loneliness, grief, or rage. Their passions drive them to eliminate the source of their pain- to punish, to test, to tempt. They are unafraid to punish or torment, but only if they are sure one deserves it. And the Principle\u2019s archaic and alien necessities for punishment rarely see them seeing eye to eye. Flux is inimical to mind, body, and soul. Where the Irin are sent to observe travesties and work to build up what has been torn down, the Shedim observe triumphs, and are asked to destroy them. The challenge for the Shedim isn\u2019t choosing to act, but choosing to abstain. They also express their Maxims differently- just as obsessively, but warped and shaped by Flux instead of Azoth.<br /> <br /> <b>Vocations</b><br /> <br /> <b><i>Ghogilim</i></b>- Those Who Go Forth Into Emptiness, The Masterless, Chaotes<br /> <br /> See the <i>Ishim</i>. However, their obsession, craft, or field of study takes a very distinctive path. Their interests are morbid, if not disturbing. They might study cancer, decay, or psychopathy. They pursue the secrets of abnormal psychology; they seek out the degenerative sides of humanity, the discordant aspect, to understand <i>why</i> they exist. They chase after the dark side of divinity; they seek out the Things Man Was Not Meant To Know. This does not necessarily need to be a directly antagonistic pursuit (though it can be); the cancer researcher could be looking for a way to destroy it, the individual studying psychopathy could be looking for a source or a treatment. The one looking into Things Man Was Not Meant To Know could be seeking answers to the big questions. But the fact of the matter is, whatever they pursue is not conducive to wholeness of mind. Whether they are any <i>healthier</i> than the obsessions of the <i>Ishim</i> is a matter of debate, but the obsessions of the Chaotes erode at mind, body, and soul until they all give way in a tide of Flux. Their Confirmation is a soul-splintering insight after years of futile searching. Personal triumphs, lifelong goals, fulfilled dreams- these are the things they observe, and they must fight the urge to undo them for esoteric reasons at best. The Chaotes falter when they see great value in an achievement, they stumble when they see punishment undeserved. They try to shelter their identity amidst the horrors with eccentric acts of self-aggrandizement, leaving calling cards to build a quiet notoriety; one aspect of their destructive duties they can take pleasure in. When fallen to Temptation, they act much like the <i>Ishim</i>, pursuing their obsessions with mindless focus, but the self-aggrandizement explodes into full-blown megalomania, and their studies take a turn for the monstrous and macabre.<ul><li>Instead of the Dominion of the Conflagration, they exclusively practice the Dominion of Pestilence</li> </ul><b><i>Theunim</i></b><b>- </b>The Defilers, The Ravens of Death, Hedonists<br /> <br /> See the <i>Erelim</i>. They crave the same attention and reputation with a passion, but no matter how well they do, it somehow goes sour because there\u2019s just something <i>off</i> about them. Whatever their chosen field is, they repress their passions; they subdue their competitive streak. The struggle to keep a lid on these feelings that are as much a part of them as their skin is damaging, eroding away at mind, body, and soul until the Hedonists explode with Flux. Their Confirmation is a cathartic release of passions long suppressed. They gain a vicious edge to their competitive streak; their passions in repression became twisted. The force of Flux hammering through feelings long suppressed turns them into stalkers, berserkers, obsessive-compulsives. For them, there is little difference between feeling something and acting on it. They chase the extremes of sensation, indulging a debt of experience long overdue with severe interest. Some fight to hold onto their identity, trying to remain ascetic save for their one indulgence. Those who resist try to pick their battles, saving Exousia for resisting missions they find genuinely abhorrent. Those who enjoy their new state don\u2019t generally resist at all, instead preferring to leap headlong into missions, spiraling ever deeper into monstrosity and twisted indulgence. Soon, it becomes a game, seeing how long they can draw it out, how long they can torment those they are supposed to punish, how they can make it even crueler or more fitting, until they start risking Exousia for the sake of their enjoyment. A Hedonist falters when their mission would deny them satisfaction, they stumble when they are asked to repress. When fallen to Temptation, they act much the same as the <i>Erelim</i> except any limits or sense of decency they may have possessed evaporates, Flux-aligned Glory and Lux are burned in order to indulge every dark whim, and depths of depravity they wouldn\u2019t normally descend to and may later regret are indulged.<ul><li>Instead of the Dominion of the Firmament, they exclusively practice the Dominion of Violation</li> </ul><b><i>Usilim</i></b><b>- </b>The Ruinous, Ones Adorned with Fire, Tyrants<br /> <br /> See the <i>Elohim</i>. They crave authority and control, but will never wield it in the capacity they truly desire, nor will they ever be respected. But it isn\u2019t really control or authority they seek, that would imply a power play between themselves and others- they look instead for domination. Perhaps they genuinely believe, or have deluded themselves to believe, that the world would be better off if everyone just <i>obeyed</i> them. They are remarkably keen at finding someone\u2019s worst flaws, crushing that person with them, and then demonstrating why they would be so much better as a leader. Some deeply regret what they have undertaken, but still believe their way is the best way. They give warning to those who would cross them, or who they\u2019ve marked for death, by doing things that are blatantly, supernaturally, out of line with your average reality. Attempts to plead for mercy will often be met with powerful speeches of condemnation and derision. Ironically, if there\u2019s one thing these Announced cannot stand, it\u2019s hypocrisy. They risk Exousia and burn Glory to assert their power and destroy potential threats; their shrewd paranoia and need to remain the undisputed master can overwhelm their compassion for, or even acknowledgement of, others. A Tyrant falters when their mission forces them to make concessions, they stumble when they are asked to give up control. When fallen to Temptation they act much the same as the <i>Elohim</i> except they also search the shadows for hidden rivals. They enact inquisitions and witch-hunts; they destroy perceived threats preemptively and turn against allies for perceived slights.<ul><li>Instead of the Dominion of the Depths, they exclusively practice the Dominion of Storms</li> </ul><b><i>Zomilim</i></b><b>- </b>The Revolting Ones, Those of Fearful Light, Anarchists<br /> <br /> See the <i>Malakim</i>. Like the Princes, they have a cause, but if morality rarely enters into the equation for the <i>Malakim</i>, then it is virtually non-existent for the <i>Zomilim</i>. Theirs are causes that would see the world burn and be reborn from the ashes. Their philosophies are more existential and nihilistic than those of the Princes; they cannot deny the monstrousness of humans, or themselves. They descended to horrifying depths for the sake of their causes; they saw how the supposed \u2018limits\u2019 of humanity or morality fell away, and they understood that in order for what is good to grow or persist, what is useless, stagnant, cancerous must be destroyed. The Anarchists have a morbid, horrifying kind of charm. Charismatic and powerful, they are the unsettling rebels of society. They often force people to plumb the dark depths of their soul, in order for the Anarchist to demonstrate (and perhaps justify) the reasoning for their actions. Their life is equal parts exhilaration and horror, liberation and loneliness. The changes wrought by Flux are often so distressing to them that they struggle and risk Exousia to retain their sense of self. Those who deny themselves time to meditate on what they have become often end up far more monstrous than they intended. Anarchists falter when they get a glimpse of what they\u2019ve become, they stumble when they are forced to look inwards and are terrified. When fallen to Temptation they act much like the <i>Malakim</i>, but they also seek to prove that anyone has the <i>potential</i> to do what they have done. They push others to acts that the individual would find repulsive in pursuit of that person\u2019s own cause, they drive people to martyrdom to feel the passion for their own cause reinvigorated.<ul><li>Instead of the Dominion of Humanity, they exclusively practice the Dominion of Death</li> </ul><b><i>Gamallim</i></b><b>- </b>The Obscene, Beasts, Devourers<br /> <br /> See the <i>Cherubim</i>. However, when faced with the loss of what had been their foundation, their desperation and anger was not turned to the perceived threat, but to the thing that once acted as their rock instead. Rather than see something fall, they destroyed it. Rather than see something sullied, they desecrated it and claimed it as their own. Their gruesome revelation through Confirmation is that they could only rely on themselves. They learned, in fierce animalism, that they are not their physical form, and that all they claim as a possession is but an extension of their physical form. Changing the physical to suit a greater, internal Truth is their highest imperative. Anything physical is a tool, to be used, manipulated, and consumed like any other. They know their own mind, and they know their independence, and they will risk Exousia and Glory for the sake of proving or securing it. There is nothing they can\u2019t twist or contaminate, nothing they can\u2019t use as a simple tool. Deep inside, they fear that they destroyed the only thing that mattered in their lives, but outwardly they fight to prove that anything can be corrupted, anything can be torn down or twisted. There is a fierce primal wisdom to the actions of the Devourers, the knowledge that nothing lasts forever and order is an illusion. They are as, in many ways, the most honest of the Shedim in their willingness to admit inhumanity, to demonstrate that they are, in fact, holy beasts and nothing more. Devourers falter when they realize something has become important to them, they stumble when they realize they\u2019ve come to depend on it. When fallen to temptation they act much like the <i>Cherubim</i>, but they don\u2019t seek to protect. They still claim things as within their purview but they fight to twist them, to defile them, to tear them down. They burn Glory and Lux to make visceral demonstrations of the inconsistency of physical things, they charge forward stubbornly heedless of god, man, or any other being- desperate to prove that they need to depend on nothing but themselves.<ul><li>Instead of the Dominion of Flesh, they exclusively practice the Dominion of Metamorphosis</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-06T13:52:46-07:00","id":691687,"title":"","content":"And now, the first of the Announced Sects: The Host. Once again, feel free to comment. I am appreciative of the views and the likes, and especially of the handful of PMs I've gotten on the subject, but without further commentary I have no idea what's good, what's bad, and what just doesn't work.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><b>The Bureaucracy of Heaven</b><br /> <br /> The legends of the Announced speak of their unified societies in the past. The wonders of the Host under the command of the Grigori before the Deluge, the pioneering lawgivers of the Cult of Anu, the unity and co-existence of the days of the great fire temple at Ghagha-Shahr. Announced old enough to remember the Middle Ages pine for the days when the Catholic Church or the Caliphs of the Abbasid Dynasty were among the preeminent political entities of the day. Sadly, these days the Announced have decidedly more fractured societies in an age when the word of a divinity carries less weight than it once did. However, rare as the Announced are, their traditions, lore, cultural and religious trappings have survived with enough consistency that certain organizations- called Sects -have formed over the years. Some are more recent organizations in search of ancient roots, others are hollowed husks of ancient societies looking for modern glory, and others are mainstays that have persisted over the centuries.<br /> <br /> The main Sects still active today are the <b>Host</b>, <b>Sefer Raziel</b>, the <b>Children of the White Temple</b>, the <b>Kan Fravashi</b>, and the <b>Black Flame</b>.<br /> <br /> <i>The Host</i><br /> <br /> <u>History</u>: If one believes the legends, the Host has always existed. They claim to be modeled after the Celestial Hosts of the <i>qashmallim</i>, a system that was revealed to the Announced through the Grigori. The idea of an army, or at least an organization, of servile beings glorifying a particular divinity above all others is present across many cultures in many times and places. In the World of Darkness, physical examples of the idea are equally as evident. Spirits occasionally organize into Courts, bowing to the mightiest who themselves have overlords. True Fae Titles shaped as Wisps fit the bill, as do- to certain interpretations -the Arisen. However, the Announced are perhaps among the most ubiquitous in this regard.<br /> <br /> In ancient times, the Host was a fairly disparate organization of like-minded Announced gathering in service to a particular god. The Cult of Anu could be said to be the earliest recorded example, and the predecessors of <b>Kan Fravashi </b>also fit the description. There were Hosts dedicated to Baal, to El, to Inanna, to Tammuz, to Heru and Sekhmet- to any and every god who was described as, or seemed capable of, leading a divine army had a local Host dedicated to their name. Depending on whether they were Irin or Shedim they tended to dedicate themselves to a particular god, and Vocation played a roll, but Announced of any stripe could join so long as they submitted to the hierarchy and philosophy. The sense of camaraderie was comforting, almost military in style, and the Sect made sure that a threat against one of its members was met head-on as a community. Perhaps the most militant and dedicated of these early Hosts was that dedicated to Yahweh, which at the time was considered quite odd to the other devotees. Their faith and devotion the other Hosts could understand- they were no strangers to Henotheism, but Monotheism was an anomaly that the historical record shows only once as a precedent during the reign of Akhenaten several centuries before. At the time, no one could foresee how this one, relatively small, Host among many would come to dominate.<br /> <br /> The Second Temple Period saw the spread of a Host dedicated to the monotheistic God, influenced by the evolving religion of Judaism, contact with Zoroastrianism, and a wider spread of the associated concepts- and at this time it was effectively the <i>only</i> Host. With the destruction of the Second Temple, the Host lost the site and symbol of their united leadership, and began to focus on communities, and maintaining communication between different groups. The term \u2018angel\u2019 became something of an in-joke after a while, since some of the most prestigious member of the Host were those who would deliver messages to other communities and were thus, literally, an angelos. The rise of Christianity caused some division and infighting, though these issues resolved themselves more quickly- generally -among the Announced than among humans, as they saw no demonstrable difference between the abilities of those who professed different philosophical or religious beliefs. New sects and denominations of monotheistic religions brought on breaks within the Host. While those Announced who managed to insinuate themselves within the Catholic Church found a way to catapult their way to social and temporal power, they had their opponents in other parts of the world, in particular among the courts of the Abbasid Dynasty. Some would say the Crusades hid some of the nastiest Shadow Wars in history, where the god-like Announced went to war in force.<br /> <br /> With the fall of organized religion as a major political power, the Host lost the total dominance it held in Announced society- other Sects were capable of challenging it for the first time in centuries. Modern nights may have reshaped the Host, but the oldest of the traditionalists still remember the glory days, and they would prefer to maintain whatever advantages they still possess.<br /> <br /> <u>Modern Nights</u>: To this day, the Host remains one of the largest political and cultural entities among the Irin. They favor two of the largest religions in the world for their trappings, taking up a whopping 55.7% of the world\u2019s population. That is a lot of people to hide in and/or around. However, the days where they could discriminate based on the orthodoxy of their members\u2019 beliefs are past. While the oldest traditionalists and those at the top of the organization rail against \u2018heresy\u2019 and \u2018godlessness\u2019 those in the position to observe everyday life realize that in a world where dissenters are no longer burned at the stake, they need to swallow their pride and open their doors to those of differing beliefs- so long as they obey the hierarchy and follow the philosophy. The Host is split into ideological factions named after the most famous Archangels of the monotheistic religions- <b>Michaelites</b>, <b>Gabrielites</b>, <b>Raphaelites</b>, and <b>Urielites</b>.<br /> <br /> <u>Factions</u><br /> <br /> <b>Michaelites: </b>The followers of the Archangel Michael are protectors, warriors, and guardians. They set things to rights, they punish the unjust, and they work tirelessly to bring things back to wholeness and stability. Understandably, they tend to attract the <i>Erelim</i> and the <i>Cherubim</i> the most. Michaelites are typically unwavering in their adherence both to Exousia, and to their own moral codes. Whatever the case may be, every Michaelite generally has a strict code- actions they can condone, and things they cannot allow to slip by. Some of the more militant actively punish those who have done wrong according to their code, and while some will claim an area of a city as theirs to protect, most put themselves at the service of the Announced community at large. However, when they are most <i>able</i> to help and when they are <i>free</i> to help are two very different things. As a result, most Michaelites try to complete Missions as swiftly and efficiently as possible, then use whatever Glory they have left to benefit others- pursuing their own goals and acting on the behest of the Choir. Their Missions are a holy crusade; they fight a war against division, apathy, chaos, and destruction. A Michaelite falters when their Mission conflicts with their code, they stumble when they can see no good come of acting according to Exousia. For them, Chains are the marks of those who broke faith- and they had better have a good reason for it. In their opinion, the Shedim are their natural enemies, who pursue goals that are always the antithesis of theirs.<br /> <br /> <b>Gabrielites: </b>The followers of the Archangel Gabriel are prophets, mentors, and diplomats. They are the deliverers of good news, they are overseers of movements, and more often than not are more than content to help nudge a mere mortal along the path to restoration and wholeness. They typically attract the <i>Malakim</i> the most. Gabrielites are actually among the subtlest members of the Host, favoring mortal champions and mortal agents, much like the <i>qashmallim</i> themselves. When they <i>do</i> stretch the limits of their Exousia, or burn through Glory, they are usually taking a stand on behalf of humans or railing against the need to observe an event they deem unjust and unnecessary. Gabrielites feel the most camaraderie with ordinary mortals, and in their spare time typically act as silent backers for movements they support and invisible intercessors on their behalf to the supernatural world. For Gabrielites, Missions are a burden they need carry only for a little while before passing it along. A Gabrielite falters when they see the burden of a Mission as too much for a mortal to carry, they stumble when the Principle would punish mortals simply for being human. For them, Chains are the marks of a Announced that took a stand against the will of the Principle at personal cost- a sign of their connection to humanity and an indicator of how far removed they\u2019ve become. In their opinion, the Shedim are threats to the community- but it is not their place to end them. When they want to dispose of a \u2018demon\u2019 they usually push and empower a mortal to do it.<br /> <br /> <b>Raphaelites:</b> The followers of the Archangel Raphael are healers, counselors, and companions. They are a mortal companion\u2019s rock; they are deeply involved activists, they are the kind of person who can never do enough to help. They typically attract the <i>Ishim</i> the most. While the Gabrielites are the most vocal about maintaining their connection to humanity, it is the Raphaelites who spend the most time among them. They are the most likely to get close to those affected by the purview of a Mission to begin with, and cherish their mortal friendships and connections even as they are painfully aware how far afield their powers and responsibilities put them. They try to avoid risking Exousia even as they prepare those within the purview of a Mission for what is to come. When they <i>do</i> push their limits, it is usually to prevent, limit, or alleviate suffering. A Raphaelite falters when a Mission would condone, or prevent the reduction of, suffering. They stumble when close companions are worn out by the troubles of life, and when their own oaths call them to act. Chains are the marks either of a great betrayal or a great demonstration of loyalty- but either way means a broken oath somewhere. Most followers of the Archangel Raphael tend to use the Hippocratic Oath as a guideline for how they should live and when they should act against the dictates of the Principle. In their opinion, the Shedim are not inherently evil, but if they act against those the Raphaelite care for they will see little of their famed healer\u2019s mercy.<br /> <br /> <b>Urielites: </b>The followers of the Archangel Uriel are judges, rulers, and agents of punishment. They act as the chief authorities among the Host; they know the laws of the local Choir backwards and forwards, and they take it upon themselves to punish those who do not conform to their decrees with as little pity as any Shackled Shedim. They typically attract the <i>Elohim</i> the most. Urielites are the most blatant among the Host in terms of supernatural power, pushing themselves to the absolute limits. Fitting for a group dedicated to the ideals of a being called \u201cLord of Thunder and Terror\u201d. They see themselves as the true heirs of the Grigori, and the archons in service to Metatron- they alone reserve the right to act in official channels to punish and kill other Announced, and they alone reserve the right to try and push exceptional mortals to Confirmation; a sacrament that is patronized by Uriel. When a Urielite stretches the limits of their Exousia, or burns through Glory, it is typically to punish a troublemaker or eliminate an opponent. Of all the members of the Host, they are the most inclined to try and understand the Divine Plan, and they try to preemptively enact it as they understand it, with personal revisions taken into account, of course. A Urielite falters when a Mission would undermine their goals, they stumble when a Mission goes against what they believe is the Divine Plan. For them, any Chains are signs of weakness and of flawed philosophy. If they understood the Divine Plan, then they would be able to act freely within the limits of Exousia. In their opinion, the Shedim are as necessary to police as any other Announced, and make an excellent form of distraction for their underlings.<br /> <br /> <u>Favored Maxim</u>: <i>Nefesh </i>(Living Soul)<br /> <br /> <u>Dominions</u>: The Host practices the Dominion of Vitality and exclusively teaches the Dominion of Hymns<br /> <br /> <u>Philosophy</u><br /> <br /> <b>Belief is Strength:</b> It does not matter in what. Most members of the Host would say that belief in religion is the greatest strength, and identify their religion as the best example, the truth is this facet of the philosophy applies to any faith or any creed. Ultimately, believing in something gives an individual strength beyond anything else, and beliefs are to be fostered and honed like fine weapons.<br /> <br /> <b>Glory is Service:</b> The Announced had their moment of Glory in the Confirmation, and now the Principle calls them to divest that Glory on others. For the Irin, Glory should be in service to another. Many would apply this to lofty ideals- glory is in service to a cause, glory is in service to a nation, glory is in service to the law. However, perhaps some of the traditionalists who used to be monks have it best. Glory is helping those you can to the best of your ability.<br /> <br /> <b>Vocation is Purpose: </b>God intends the Announced for great things; they are Its agents in the natural order of things, and they are meant to obey a hierarchy. Therefore, it is only natural that some Vocations are higher than others. In this paradigm, <i>Ishim</i> are at the bottom and <i>Elohim</i> at the top, with the <i>Malakim</i> operating in a strange position of \u2018privileged but outside\u2019 the system and the <i>Erelim </i>as middle-class citizens.<br /> <br /> <b>The Fire is the Source:</b> Ultimately, the Host believes that everything in the world, mundane or supernatural, comes from the Principle and the Fire being filtered through. It is how they explain the strangeness of the world, and provides comfort in asserting that the Irin must- therefore -be at the top of the power spectrum, seeing as they have the most direct access to the Fire. It would certainly be a rude awakening, being proven wrong.<br /> <br /> <u>The Prodigal Demiurge</u><b>:</b> The cosmic being holding the Announced in Its grasp is nothing less than the name they give It; Metatron, the Voice of God. It may have divided from the Divine Fire in times past for reasons alien and unknowable, but It is a loyal servant of the Principle now and they would do well to abide by Its rules and serve Its purposes.<br /> <br /> <u>Stereotypes</u><ul><li><b>Sefer Raziel:</b> Knowledge may be power, but they seek to uncover things better left alone.</li> <li><b>Children of the White Temple: </b>The Principle will send another Deluge, and they will gaze upon the Grigori and wish for their fate.</li> <li><b>Kan Fravashi:</b> So close, and yet so far. Our philosophies are compatible, even if our politics are not.</li> <li><b>The Black Flame:</b> Arrogant anarchists who\u2019d spit in the eye of God. Oppose them however you can.</li> </ul><ul><li><b>The Shedim:</b> Our enemies by nature, if not by design. I would pity them, if they didn\u2019t disgust me so.</li> </ul><ul><li><b>Vampires:</b> You know; I wanted you to seek redemption. Now I\u2019m not too sure I can stomach how you\u2019re doing it.</li> <li><b>Werewolves:</b> You, my friend, are cursed. Now stay the hell away from me, and take your mad gods with you.</li> <li><b>Mages:</b> So close, and yet so far. I\u2019d help, if you weren\u2019t so set on upsetting the natural order of things.</li> <li><b>Sin-Eaters:</b> I can\u2019t tell if you\u2019re blessed or damned. Why does that scare me so much?</li> <li><b>Mortals:</b> Don\u2019t worry, I\u2019ll show you the way</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-06T23:35:27-07:00","id":692040,"title":"","content":"I'd like to say, so far, all of this is good, and I think the change to <b>Irin: the Announced</b> has made the splat more complete. I'm enjoying reading about them. Question, how much of the mechanics has changed from the last iteration of the Irin?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><u><b>My Homebrew</b></u><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1PLu_eTg03h4ogiTSGSkzEB7T_1YpB9_VkhHmgZN41-I/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Star Wars d10</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">T</a><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">he Fallen for Demon: the Descent</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1-TDr4NF_5cUn-In2vjI9P5LTKqsu9B4-OMxbnbaTgT8/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">Requiem for a Masquerade: VtR 2e Cainite Conversion</a> (<a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HpKi5giBNsQrZRLIRRjaCe7sHVm1Q13ravVYlE45Dbw/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Vampira Roma</a>)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1601-dreaminggod","name":"Dreaminggod","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"745"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-07T08:29:08-07:00","id":692506,"title":"","content":"Thank-you very much sir. Mechanically, they will be quite changed. Glory is now comparable to Sekhem, Exousia is similar to Memory and ideas of what Promethean Pilgrimage will eventually be like. Maxims are a combination of Pillars and Renown, and Dominions now share more in common with Gifts than they do with Arcana. Edicts will still be Utterance-like powers (even though I never got around to revealing the first version of Edicts) and the Celestial form will see some serious changes, becoming more freeform like the Apocalyptic Form and the Demonic Form, less tied to Dominions, and more tied to personal and local ideas about the Divine. Blessings now have much more in common with Affinities.<br /> <br /> What <i>pentientes </i>were going to be changed dramatically, Alchemists will be another branch of human antagonist, and the Announced will also have to deal with Pandorans- though with different themes and a different approach.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-07T09:32:33-07:00","id":692592,"title":"","content":"Mechanically that all sounds better than previously. Freeform Celestial forms is great. I eagerly await this, I've been trying to nail down my own personal WoD, and wanted a version f angels/demons I like, and this may be it. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><u><b>My Homebrew</b></u><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1PLu_eTg03h4ogiTSGSkzEB7T_1YpB9_VkhHmgZN41-I/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Star Wars d10</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">T</a><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">he Fallen for Demon: the Descent</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1-TDr4NF_5cUn-In2vjI9P5LTKqsu9B4-OMxbnbaTgT8/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">Requiem for a Masquerade: VtR 2e Cainite Conversion</a> (<a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HpKi5giBNsQrZRLIRRjaCe7sHVm1Q13ravVYlE45Dbw/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Vampira Roma</a>)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1601-dreaminggod","name":"Dreaminggod","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"745"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-07T10:37:08-07:00","id":692701,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dreaminggod</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/673743-irin-the-announced?p=692592#post692592\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mechanically that all sounds better than previously. Freeform Celestial forms is great. I eagerly await this, I've been trying to nail down my own personal WoD, and wanted a version f angels/demons I like, and this may be it. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>It looks like it wouldn't be too hard to hack this into something like Demon: The Fallen, too.<br /> <br /> Maybe an official Hack for this unofficial game?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://eoinsstuff.shoutwiki.com/wiki/Genius:_the_Transgression_(2nd_Edition)\" target=\"_blank\">Genius: the Transgression 2E is a thing that's being worked on.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4488-malonkey1","name":"malonkey1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4488_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-07T11:39:50-07:00","id":692763,"title":"","content":"Quick question since irin are connected to prometheans do you think they should still be affected by disquiet?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/893-reaperfrost8","name":"reaperfrost8","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar893_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-07T13:49:11-07:00","id":692882,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>malonkey1</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/673743-irin-the-announced?p=692701#post692701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It looks like it wouldn't be too hard to hack this into something like Demon: The Fallen, too.<br /> <br /> Maybe an official Hack for this unofficial game?</div> </div> </div> </div>In my NWoD Demon, my working canon so far is the Elohim were basically the precursor the the <i>Qashmallim</i> with more free-will and the Mission of 'Create the Universe' and when that was done (about the time humanity came about) then they were to rejoin with the Divine Fire and... well, the Fallen said fuck that. Fought the war in Heaven, Awakened humanity (and maybe other sentient species? The Time Before's history is muddled) and eventually won their ability to be separate, but got thrown in the Lower Depths as punishment. It's not perfect but it's a working framework.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><u><b>My Homebrew</b></u><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1PLu_eTg03h4ogiTSGSkzEB7T_1YpB9_VkhHmgZN41-I/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Star Wars d10</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">T</a><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">he Fallen for Demon: the Descent</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1-TDr4NF_5cUn-In2vjI9P5LTKqsu9B4-OMxbnbaTgT8/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">Requiem for a Masquerade: VtR 2e Cainite Conversion</a> (<a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HpKi5giBNsQrZRLIRRjaCe7sHVm1Q13ravVYlE45Dbw/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Vampira Roma</a>)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1601-dreaminggod","name":"Dreaminggod","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"745"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-07T19:09:02-07:00","id":693309,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>reaperfrost8</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/673743-irin-the-announced?p=692763#post692763\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Quick question since irin are connected to prometheans do you think they should still be affected by disquiet?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The Announced will not be affected by Disquiet in the same way as everyone else, and in fact will be virtually immune to it. They have their own version of it to contend with, called <i>Yara</i>, but no- there is no inherent tension between the children of the Divine Fire even if Prometheans are the favourites for reasons I'll get into later.<br /> <br /> Dreaminggod that backstory sounds quite a bit like what's going on with the Grigori, except with more concrete truths and less fuzzy edges. Ultimately, I'll cover that later when I write rules on playing one of the Grigori; mechanically they will be quite similar to Announced in Communion, but more importantly the Grigori serve a role in this game similar to that held by the Earthbound in Demon: The Fallen and the Deceived in Mummy: The Curse.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-10T06:44:10-07:00","id":696016,"title":"","content":"And now, the next Sect: Sefer Raziel. Thanks for all the comments guys- feedback is always appreciated.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><i>Sefer Raziel</i><br /> <br /> <u>History</u><b>:</b> For as long as humanity has believed in gods, they have believed in lonely gods of knowledge- the ones who dedicate their eternities to recording all there is to know. And these same gods also decide whom it is that gets to use it. Gods like Thoth, the Apkallu, Prometheus, they all share the their knowledge with mankind to various degrees, and mankind profits, flounders, or is corrupted by it. The serpent in the Garden of Eden story may see its predecessor in the Sumerian Ningishzida, and the Announced look to the Grigori as the source of these legends. Knowledge was given to, found, or stolen by mankind and to this day the trouble that stems from that knowledge continues to trouble the world.<br /> <br /> The Announced of Sefer Raziel believe that the Deluge was sent to wipe out the civilization created by the Grigori not because it was evil, as The Host and Kan Fravashi would believe, nor because of jealousy as The Children of the White Temple and The Black Flame would have it. The Announced of Sefer Raziel believe the Deluge was sent because the Grigori taught mankind Things It Was Not Meant To Know, because they created an alchemical empire that wouldn\u2019t be rivaled until the days of the Nameless Empire, because they showed mankind how to steal the Divine Fire. Perhaps starting in the days of the Cult of Anu as a group of like-minded scribes and priests, those Announced who served as the predecessors of Sefer Raziel were censors. They killed, they stole the knowledge back, and they tried to keep humans from discovering it again. But <i>qashmallim</i> still gave the humans knowledge time and again, their Exousia dwindled and they could feel Chains pulling tight. Eventually, these early seekers of truth came to the conclusion that they had it wrong. The sin was not in giving mankind the knowledge; it was doling it out indiscriminately, without thought for what the knowledge was for. Their duties shifted from concealing the wisdom of the Principle to finding those who would truly be worthy of it.<br /> <br /> In time, the process became more complex as this loose organization of scholars picked up philosophy and mysticism. They didn\u2019t just seek out the worthy, because first they needed to understand <i>why</i> they were worthy. And to do that, they would have to understand the mind of the Principle. They became, at one point, deeply invested in Kabbalah, which is where they get their name from- the legend of an Archangel who gathered up all the knowledge of the Grigori and compiled it into a single book. They have been interested in mystery religions and philosophers throughout history, and continue to have a soft spot for the truly honest and wise.<br /> <br /> <u>Modern Nights</u>: While Sefer Raziel still tends to hold the reputation of mostly fostering the more intellectual and reflective of the Announced (and not without reason) it is less the impenetrable and perplexing guild of philosophers and mystics it used to be. More modern Announced who join Sefer Raziel will try to use mathematics, quantum physics, and other scientific disciplines in an effort to understand. While the modernists have a better grasp on tracking patterns and finding the underlying structure of the Principle\u2019s metaphysics, the traditionalists are still the ones most likely to find a motivational or intellectual reason for the Principle\u2019s actions. Philosophy and theology are more comforting than physics in the face of an unknowable deity. Sefer Raziel is divided into factions based more on methodology than anything else. They are the <b>Sages</b>, <b>Pilgrims</b>, and <b>Antiquarians</b>.<br /> <br /> <br /> <u>Factions</u><br /> <br /> <b>Sages:</b> The faction most likely to harbor traditionalists, these are mystics with a tendency to believe that all the answers you seek can be found if you find the right <i>qashmal</i> or travel deep enough into the Pleroma. Sages are teachers, philosophers, and dedicated mentors. Due to the nature of their attitudes, which is dependent on both mastery of themselves and their abilities, as well as the recognition of that mastery, they tend to attract the <i>Elohim</i> the most. Because the Sages tend to see life as a lesson, they almost invariably have high Exousia ratings and generally rarely do anything to endanger them. There is no Mission these deeply insightful philosophers won\u2019t try to learn a lesson from. To them, Missions are a chance to learn something more about themselves, about the Divine Plan, and about the Principle. However, due to their attitude as teachers and mentors, one thing that will invariably move them to action is when their student is caught in a situation they can\u2019t get out of. Among the Sages the teacher is <i>always</i> responsible for their student, and to threaten the pupil of an experienced and powerful Sage is to invite the wrath of a small god. A Sage falters when they can\u2019t find a lesson in the Mission, they stumble when the actions of the Principle seem meaningless. To the Sages, a Chain is the sign of failed lesson and the mark of a bad student. In their opinion, the Shedim are fellow students on a similar, albeit much darker, path.<br /> <br /> <b>Pilgrims:</b> To these Announced the truth is clear- as angelic beings it is not their place to understand the Divine Plan or the purpose of their Missions they are just the messengers. They are the eyes, ears, and hands of the Principle, meant to interfere only when the Mission goes awry, but they are not privy to the Divine Plan. The Divine seeks a relationship with mortals, and it is to them the Announced must turn to discover meaning. Pilgrims are observers, witnesses, and aspirants. They shadow those their Missions bring them close to in an effort to uncover what the Principle sees in humanity. Ultimately, as deeply individual and protective seekers, they tend to attract the <i>Ishim</i> and the <i>Cherubim</i> the most. Pilgrims try extremely hard to maintain Exousia, to prevent themselves from intervening, but it rarely lasts. Eventually, something will come along the Announced will determine is too much for those they are observing to bear and they will step in to carry the load- even at the risk of losing the insight. To the Pilgrims, Missions are a chance to witness exactly what it is the Principle sees in humanity, to learn a lesson in creativity, resilience, passion, or fortitude. A Pilgrim falters when they can see nothing exceptional in a mortal\u2019s response, they stumble when they can\u2019t see whatever it is stirs the Principle to connect with humanity. To them, a Chain is an indication that you have stepped off the path; that you are failing to see things the way the Principle would. In their opinion, the Shedim are the agents of the Principle\u2019s displeasure- to study with them is to understand God\u2019s wrath.<br /> <br /> <b>Antiquarians:</b> A faction of adventurers, students of the past, and seekers of the truth. These Announced tend to believe that all the answers they seek have been known in the past, or that all the information they need to find the answers was known in the past. Uncovering relics and artifacts of the past steeped in this lore, the Announced can uncover their heritage and move towards a better future. Glory hounds and idealists always on a quest for revelations and resolutions, they tend to attract the <i>Erelim</i> and <i>Malakim</i> the most. Typically, there are varying attitudes on what Missions are, and what they are meant to show. There is a Divine Plan, of that the Antiquarians are sure, but they need to know where it\u2019s been and what it\u2019s done to figure out the shape of where it\u2019s going. At worst, Missions are diversion keeping them from their work or outright destruction of knowledge. At best, they are clues, leads to the truth. Any data is good data, especially when you are trying to comprehend the unfathomable mind of the Principle. These Announced fight to craft a cosmology they can understand, to fit everything together. They chase every myth, every legend; apply every scientific theorem and crackpot hypothesis they can find in an effort to understand. An Antiquarian falters when the pattern breaks, they stumble when their theories can be proven false. To them, Chains are marks of desperation- either ending in spectacular success or crushing failure, and they are willing to tell the story. In their opinion, the Shedim <i>could</i> work with them- but their tendency to destroy and conceal doesn\u2019t make them the best of friends. Most Antiquarians think the Shedim are the Principle\u2019s censors.<br /> <br /> <u>Favored Maxim</u>: Neshama (Seat of Understanding)<br /> <br /> <u>Dominions</u>: Sefer Raziel practices the Dominion of Realms and exclusively teaches the Dominion of Tongues<br /> <br /> <u>Philosophy</u><br /> <br /> <b>As Above, So Below:</b> When the Pleroma shifts, the world follows suit. When there is massive turbulence in the human world, you can bet the Pleroma will shift to match it. By examining the shape of history and the currents of the Pleroma, they can see where the hand of the Principle has been, and predict where it is going. In turn, by examining the human world they can observe what sort of events call the attention of the Divine Fire, and predict where they will need to be.<br /> <br /> <b>Glory is Understanding:</b> The more the Announced\u2019s blazing heart is filled to bursting with the Divine Fire, the more connected they are to the very fires of creation, the deeper they can safely travel into the Pleroma, and the more of the Divine Plan they can observe. The Confirmation was a moment of supreme understanding of oneself, the core of their being. It is what gave the Announced their first taste of Glory, and by seeking to understand the mind of the Principle they would find ways to extend their Glory.<br /> <br /> <b>Vocation is a Relationship:</b> In the Confirmation, the Announced made true contact with the Divine Fire to the point that they are no inseparable from it. Through their Vocations, the Announced can come to understand their personal relationship with the Principle- and from there, determine their place in the Divine Plan. In doing so, the Announced of Sefer Raziel hope to achieve Communion, by finally understanding the true invitation extended to them by the Principle and accepting it.<br /> <br /> <b>The Fire is United:</b> The Announced of Sefer Raziel know more clearly than any other Sect that Divine Fire is Divine Fire; separating it into Azoth and Flux is trivial. The Fire is both creative and destructive. It calls humanity to come to it, and burns those who are not ready to join it. To try and outline metaphysical distinctions between different Sects, different Vocations, or even the Irin and the Shedim is to lie to yourself. Everything touched by the Fire is one.<br /> <br /> <u>The Prodigal Demiurge</u>: The Announced of Sefer Raziel believe that Metatron\u2019s sin was a failure to understand. The core concept of sin is <i>hamartia</i>, a Greek term that means, \u201cto miss the mark\u201d. In not just disagreeing, but entering into conflict with the Principle, It separated Itself from the totality of the Divine. The Announced will learn from It\u2019s mistake by understanding the Divine Plan. However, they still respect It as Metatron remains, above all, the chief scribe of the Pleroma. If any are close enough to the Principle to know the shape of the Divine Plan- it\u2019s the Prodigal Demiurge.<br /> <br /> <u>Stereotypes</u><ul><li><b>The Host:</b> In blindly obeying Metatron they recommit Its sin. Action without understanding is vacant.</li> <li><b>Kan Fravashi:</b> They are so close to understanding, but their faith blinds them</li> <li><b>Children of the White Temple:</b> Not even the Demiurge is so foolish. You aren\u2019t biting the hand that feeds you; you\u2019re planning to cut it off.</li> <li><b>The Black Flame:</b> Our other half, they look in the shadows we won\u2019t</li> </ul><ul><li><b>The Shedim:</b> Yours is a dark path and a hard lesson. I can respect those that manage not to lose themselves.</li> </ul><ul><li><b>Vampires:</b> If they can remember where the world has been, maybe you can see where it\u2019s going.</li> <li><b>Werewolves:</b> The echoes of the Principle\u2019s actions still sound in the <i>Hisil</i>, and these bundles of rage are your best insurance policy there.</li> <li><b>Mages:</b> They have a tendency to make and break the plans of gods, trade notes but watch them carefully.</li> <li><b>Hunters:</b> Quite handy to have around, but beware of giving up too much</li> <li><b>Mortals:</b> What is it that makes you worthy?</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-12T12:29:23-07:00","id":698875,"title":"","content":"Sect number three, the Children of the White Temple. Once again, feel free to comment- feedback is always great and it gives quite the sense of accomplishment to have people comment on the thread or even send a PM.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><i>The Children of the White Temple</i><br /> <br /> <u>History</u>: Very, very young by the count of the Irin- the Children of the White Temple were born as a Sect, admittedly a very small Sect, during the days following the decipherment of Sumerian and Ancient Egyptian. Caught up in a tide of Egyptomania, and the obsessive studying of newly deciphered Sumerian texts in an effort to verify the accuracy of the Bible- which before these translations was one of the earliest pieces of literature known -the Announced were no different than other fascinated people around the world. The difference was, the implications of stories of angel-like beings, global floods, and other such things well predating the Bible caused quite a sensation in its day, as it does in a more muted scale now. And the Announced became caught up by it. It was an electrifying idea; that they were more than just servants of a monotheistic god, but were small gods unto themselves in a polytheistic universe.<br /> <br /> They came to a heretical conclusion; that the Principle is not their lord and master, but is instead their jailer. An upstart enslaving the true gods of the world. For a long time, the Sect only drew upon ancient legends of Sumeria and Egypt, reveling in the knowledge that they were simply one of many gods in the universe, and that each of them had a right to rule and be worshipped. They prefer to call the Grigori the Annunaki; gods that descended to create an empire to guide mankind into the future. Supposedly, many of the ways the World of Darkness operates is thanks to them. They charged <i>Urfarah</i> with lordship of the Border Marches, the light of the Pleroma is what ignites the Supernal and leads mortals to Awakening, they ransomed mortals back from death and commanded legions of Spirits. However, one of their creations, an effort to create a guiding intelligence to their source of limitless mystic energy, backfired and became the Prodigal Demiurge. It broke away from Its old masters and cast them down, in modern days it enslaves their heirs and denies them true divinity.<br /> <br /> With the onset of Ancient Alien beliefs, especially the work of Zecharia Stichin in the 1970s and 80s, the already enormous egos and confidence of the Children of the White Temple rose even higher. They alone are the true gods- all stories, all mystic framework other beings have built themselves up with, are relics of their ancestors- the Annunaki.<br /> <br /> <u>Modern Nights</u>: The mythos surrounding Ancient Aliens has developed at an unprecedented rate, leaving no end of stories for these Announced to draw upon. They are paradoxically a uniquely modern Sect, and one that tries to take hold of ancient roots. As they unearth ancient Edicts and Dominions, as well as the writings of ancient Announced, they move farther away from their modern incarnations as scions of a race of multi-dimensional space travelers and closer towards a pantheon of bloody and capricious Bronze Age gods. These days, the Children of the White Temple are separated mostly into two factions- <b>Claimants</b> and <b>Pneumatics</b>.<br /> <br /> <u>Factions</u><br /> <br /> <b>Claimants: </b>These are the true believers, those who genuinely believe that they are gods and that the Prodigal Demiurge is their jailer. They are cult leaders, mob bosses, and motivational speakers that are utterly assured of their divinity. Due to the strongly Bronze Age attitudes of the group, they tend to attract <i>Erelim</i>, <i>Elohim</i>, and <i>Cherubim</i> the most. Almost without fail, members of the Claimants have rocky Exousia and are always a step away from becoming Shackled- they only manage to hold on by fixating strongly on their Title. In their opinion, the cycle of missions and burnout is artificially imposed- as is Exousia. Missions and the supposed \u2018Divine Plan\u2019 in general are the call of those gods who ascended to the limitless power union with the Divine Fire, but twisted by the petty and cruel Prodigal Demiurge. The call to return home through Communion is twisted by Metatron. They tend to try and eschew all Missions, except those that they <i>must</i> take to preserve their free will and those that forward their goals, though the second practice is frowned upon. In their opinion, Chains are a sign of oppression, and there is nothing particularly malevolent or noteworthy about the Shedim. There are gods of the underworld as well as gods of the heavens.<br /> <br /> <b>Pneumatics:</b> Less so true believers in the ancient gods angle of the Claimants, this faction is a group of Gnostics who take the \u2018Demiurge\u2019 part of \u2018Prodigal Demiurge\u2019 quite literally. They are visionaries, true mystics, supposed gurus of the Announced obsessed with transforming mortals into Announced themselves so they may eventually ascend to the true Pleroma, beyond the sad structure of forms the Demiurge built and into the endless totality of the Divine Fire. The Pneumatics believe that the Confirmation was the awakening of the Divine Spark within their soul, that they achieved some kind of transcendental knowledge, and that they must try to help others to see as they have seen. In their opinion, Metatron is nothing more than Yaldabaoth and the Grigori were rebellious Archons. The <i>qashmallim</i> and <i>lilithim</i> are usually seen as servants of the Demiurge, but those of high Rank- especially those reaching into truly divine territory -are occasionally seen as messengers from the Principle Itself, which they identify with Sophia or even greater Aeons. Exousia is a false construct created by the Demiurge, and missions are to be avoided as fulfilling Its work. However, in spite of this belief, most Pneumatics can\u2019t help but exploit missions in an effort to help people. In their opinion Chains is itself a flawed term, as they see them as connections to the Aeons. The Shackled are not to be pitied, but seen as having achieved union with the Divine. In their opinion, the Shedim are nothing but Archons still enslaved to the Demiurge.<br /> <br /> <u>Favored Maxim</u>: Chaya (Life Divine)<br /> <br /> <u>Dominions</u>: The Children of the White Temple practice the Dominion of Ephemera and exclusively teach the Dominion of <i>Mes</i><br /> <br /> <u>Philosophy</u><br /> <br /> <b>The Announced are Divine:</b> Not through the will of the Demiurge, but in spite of it, the Announced are Divine. Not mere servants to a higher power, but independent gods unto themselves. Cast from the heavens by a creation that got away from them, the Announced are bound by the limits imposed upon them artificially by the Demiurge and are limited by the dictates of Exousia.<br /> <br /> <b>Glory is Owed:</b> As formerly boundless divinities, Glory is not a measure of loyalty, favor by God, or some other such dross. Glory is merely a measure by how far you can stretch your tethers- how much of your divine nature you have wrested from the Demiurge. Ultimately, Glory is not something you are gifted with, but something you are owed by virtue of nature.<br /> <br /> <b>Vocation is Purview:</b> All gods must be god of something, but it just so happens that for the Announced their purviews fall into predictable patterns. By pursuing mastery of their purviews, the Announced will find their way to Communion. They will be welcomed home to the Pleroma and their enemies will tremble before a true god.<br /> <br /> <b>The Fire belongs to the Announced:</b> Ultimately, the Divine Fire is their right. There is no battle between good and evil, the Demiurge is an enemy to be swept aside once enough Announced have ascended to combat it properly, and the Divine Fire is their own personal property. The very furnace of creation in the hands of a pantheon of true gods- what could possibly go wrong?<br /> <br /> <u>The Prodigal Demiurge</u>: It is just that- the Demiurge. It limits, It opposes, and It tries to keep those who would be true gods of the universe from their thrones. It is their jailer, a traitor to Its true masters, and an upstart responsible for all that is wrong with the world. Things will be set to rights once the gods command the heavens again.<br /> <br /> <u>Stereotypes</u><ul><li><b>The Host:</b> Lapdogs of our jailer, we should oppose them at every opportunity</li> <li><b>Sefer Raziel:</b> They seek out ancient knowledge. For that reason alone, it would be wise to keep them close.</li> <li><b>Kan Fravashi:</b> Traitors- they were the first to bow to the Demiurge.</li> <li><b>The Black Flame:</b> They deny the Demiurge\u2019s right to rule, but not It\u2019s power. Watch them carefully; they know things we do not.</li> </ul><ul><li><b>The Shedim:</b> A god is a god whether of the underworld or the heavens.</li> </ul><ul><li><b>Vampires:</b> There was once a day when you worshipped us, and that day shall come again.</li> <li><b>Werewolves:</b> Once your Father and Mother would come when we called. Perhaps you will do the same.</li> <li><b>Mages:</b> You use the analogy of Plato\u2019s Cave, yes? We are the sun.</li> <li><b>Mummies:</b> Perhaps the only ones who can help us find what we seek, but for the love of Enki our ancestor, <i>don\u2019t touch anything</i>.</li> <li><b>Mortals:</b> You are, all of you, beneath me</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-13T13:05:42-07:00","id":700054,"title":"","content":"Sect number four, Kan Fravashi. I think I changed these guys up the most. The backstory and modern information is much less theology heavy- though I may throw in some sidebars to add a bit of flavour and a bit of depth -but they are still probably the most isolated conceptually of the rest of the Sects. The others can all co-exist to a certain degree, though the Children of the White Temple would prefer to dominate an area, but Kan Fravashi prefers to either utterly dominate the region, or isolate itself when it is not the most significant group.<br /> <br /> Let me know what you guys think- they're quite a bit different from the rest so feedback here will be vital.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><i>Kan Fravashi</i><br /> <br /> <u>History</u>: One of the great religions of the ancient world, and one of the oldest surviving today, is Zoroastrianism. An inclusive, dualistic religion, it had an undeniable and lasting impact on world religions such a Judaism, Christianity, and Islam. During the days of Cyrus the Great the Announced of the Persian Empire found solace in their faith, and were the first since the days of the Cult of Anu to seek out other Announced in different cultures across the empire. From the beginning, the Announced were involved in Zoroastrianism. The dualism was quite appealing to them, and much of the philosophy spoke directly to the Announced experience. Some even believe that someone touched by the Fire wrote some of the scriptures.<br /> <br /> A united group such as the world of the Announced would never see again, when the Persian Empire collapsed in the wake of Alexander\u2019s conquests, they spread out and shared their knowledge with other groups- most of it collected by the Host and the predecessors of Sefer Raziel even as Kan Fravashi before them collected the knowledge of the Cult of Anu when it collapsed. The group managed to limp on, coming together under the Sassanid Empire and then being splintered again under the Umayyad Caliphate. The group survived with the adherents of the religion, protecting and watching over it as they were spread in a diaspora and persecuted by mortals and Announced of other faiths. They continued to have the strongest hold among the Parsi of India and the Yazadi of the Zargos Mountains.<br /> <br /> As following with their beliefs, they believe in two divinities, Ahura Mazda- the good God, lord of <i>Asha</i> which is unity, justice, truth, and right action - and Angra Mainyu- his evil twin brother, lord of <i>Druj</i> which is decay, deceit, destruction, and the erosion of good works. Naturally the former is the Principle and the latter is the Prodigal Demiurge, the former with Azoth and the latter with Flux. The Grigori were the Amesha Spentas, the great \u2018Divine Sparks\u2019 that were lured off the path of Truth by the Prodigal Demiurge. The Irin themselves are Yazatas, those worthy of worship, who are lucky enough to- in life -be united with the Guardian Spirit of their soul, called a Fravahar. The Shedim, on the other hand, are <i>druj</i>, servants of Angra Mainyu who exist to tempt people off the path. Generally, Zoroastrianism does not accept converts, and neither do Kan Fravashi- however, they tend to declare any Announced who are Confirmed in regions of their control members of the Sect.<br /> <br /> <u>Modern Nights</u>: More than any other Sect, the Announced of Kan Fravashi have their own internal culture and terminology. They are Announced, but they are also Yazatas. The Grigori are fallen Amesha Spentas, Exousia is <i>Daena</i>- the laws by which all duty and right conduct is judged -and Missions are clues from the Principle to defeat the workings of the Prodigal Demiurge. In the same way, the Missions of those Shedim who follow the beliefs of Kan Fravashi are considered acts of Angra Mainyu- and they resist them with all their hearts. More than any other Sect, the Announced of Kan Fravashi look to find a way to create more of their kind, for they can only accept those \u2018born\u2019 into the Sect. They are separated into three factions, <b>Aesthetics</b>, <b>Materialists</b>, and <b>Fatalists</b>.<br /> <br /> <u>Factions</u><br /> <br /> <b>Aesthetics:</b> These Announced are bodhisattvas, shepherds, and champions of life. To them, the Divine Fire is the <i>prima materia</i>, the source of all matter, the building blocks of creation. It is Time and Fate in one, and when influenced by desire, it becomes both reason and blazing passion. The Divine Fire, touched by desire, can become <i>anything</i>. Individuals of immense drive and deep passion, this faction tends to attract the <i>Ishim</i> and <i>Erelim </i>the most. To their view, <i>Asha</i>- and therefore Azoth -is existence itself, and to work against <i>Asha</i> is more than a mere sin against morality, or some god, it is a violation against Creation itself, it is a step to unmake existence. They try to follow Exousia- which they call <i>Daena</i> -rigorously and without failing, but refuse to deny themselves anything. To not enjoy life, to not experience it, is to deny their duty to support <i>Asha</i> and inaction does nothing except work for <i>Druj</i>. They see Missions as a chance to do the good work of <i>Asha</i> against the machinations of the Prodigal Demiurge. In their opinion, Chains are not punishments but spiritual scars that remind them of what is at stake- they could lose their soul and their Favrahar would be bereft of the lessons they have to offer.<br /> <br /> <b>Materialists:</b> These Announced are saints of passion, vivacious heretics, and students of human nature. They reject the idea of totally spiritual existences- something can\u2019t come out of nothing, and even if science can\u2019t measure something like Essence, Vitae, or Pyros (while it <i>can</i> measure Sekhem) it is still matter. Even the Pleroma and Sheol are material places. While they are quite aware ghosts, spirits, and other beings exist, they place no spiritual or metaphysical stock in them. Even the Underworld is just a place where the echoes of human memory live on, and souls are little more than a source of power. They believe the Divine Fire is undifferentiated matter, pure potential energy, and nothing else. Perhaps it is tied to some grand consciousness like a Boltzmann Brain, but more likely the <i>qashmallim</i> are deceptively \u2018self-aware\u2019 natural processes. As such, the only morality and order in this world, all enjoyment in life, must come from the sentient and aware. Humans and Announced have a duty to enjoy life, to make the world a better place, to stave off entropy as represented by Flux. As they are so focused on the material and the pursuit of personal causes, they tend to attract the <i>Cherubim</i> and <i>Malakim</i> the most. In their opinion, Exousia is a measure of their own soul\u2019s integrity, and Missions are a chance to act against entropy. Chains are the sign of a fraying soul. In their opinion, while the Shedim are naturally agents of entropy, they are not inherently evil and if they can resist the urge to tear down they are welcome in the community.<br /> <br /> <b>Fatalists:</b> These Announced are soothsayers, priests, and hands of fate. To them the Divine Fire is nothing less than the master of the universe, who has allotted preordained destinies to all within it and given the universe itself an expiration date. Believing so utterly in a transcendental power, they tend to attract the <i>Elohim</i> the most. In their belief, those who do good have good things occur to them, and those who do evil have evil things occur to them. In their belief, <i>Asha</i> is ultimately the only thing that will survive, as it is in line with the Principle, and thus it is the duty of humanity and the Announced to try and maintain the path of <i>Daena</i>- however while the Principle has decreed nothing but happiness for humanity, those who follow <i>Druj</i> have through their actions caused chaos, despair, and unhappiness. In their opinion, Missions are a means to bring people back to the path of <i>Asha</i>, a race against the clock to save those affected. Chains are the marks of broken oaths and a tumble off the path, while the Shedim are the source of all that is wrong with the world.<br /> <br /> <u>Favored Maxim</u>: Yechida (Oneness)<br /> <br /> <u>Dominions</u>: The Announced of Kan Fravashi study the Dominion of Souls, and exclusively practice the Dominion of <i>Daena</i>.<br /> <br /> <u>Philosophy</u><br /> <br /> <b>Good Thoughts, Good Words, Good Deeds:</b> By these things you shall know those who are on the path of <i>Asha</i>. Through these things you will obey <i>Daena</i>, explore your Maxims, and find the path to Communion. Increase the power of <i>Asha</i> in the world, for it is the right thing to do. All good rewards shall follow suit.<br /> <br /> <b>Glory is a Gift:</b> The Principle is the giver of all true gifts. You have the great privilege of being united with your oversoul, the Favrahar, in this life instead of your soul returning to it in death to impart the lessons it has learned and strengthen it through your good deeds. The strength of your soul is not metaphorical, but literal, and by taking steps to fulfill Missions and obey the laws of <i>Daena</i> you are employing your gift in a manner pleasing to the Principle.<br /> <br /> <b>Vocation is the Path to Truth:</b> You were Confirmed in a specific manner for a reason. Staying true to your Maxim and your Vocation will be the way to maintain <i>Daena</i>, follow the path of <i>Asha</i>, and find a way towards Communion.<br /> <br /> <b>The Fire is Eternal:</b> Before the universe proper existed and when everything else decays to dust, the Divine Fire will still be there. It will mourn the loss of creation, but it will persist. And those Announced who have reached Communion and become one with the Divine Fire? They will be more than immortals, but true eternals.<br /> <br /> <u>The Prodigal Demiurge</u>: It is what It is described as and more. The Announced of Kan Fravashi call it Angra Mainyu, and it is the lord of <i>Druj</i> and Flux. It tears away at Exousia in an effort to destroy those who oppose It, and Its servants hide in every dark shadow and every dark deed.<br /> <br /> <u>Stereotypes</u><ul><li><b>The Host:</b> Our ignorant successors- they should be reminded from whence their knowledge came.</li> <li><b>Sefer Raziel:</b> Old allies. They understand us, but they look constantly for answers and secrets where there are none.</li> <li><b>Children of the White Temple:</b> We are the heirs of Sumer, not you.</li> <li><b>The Black Flame:</b> They walk the path of the lie, and are proud of it. Oppose them when you must, guide them to right action if you can.</li> </ul><ul><li><b>The Shedim:</b> The vilest servants of <i>Druj</i>- even if they do not wish it</li> </ul><ul><li><b>Vampires:</b> <i>Druj</i> is in their blood- let them learn of the fires of <i>Asha</i></li> <li><b>Werewolves:</b> You are outside my understanding.</li> <li><b>Mages:</b> Where did you think the word \u2018Mage\u2019 came from anyways?</li> <li><b>Prometheans:</b> We walk the same road; just don\u2019t come so close please.</li> <li><b>Mortals:</b> We are enlightened, but if that is true why does God love you more?</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-15T18:46:12-07:00","id":702813,"title":"","content":"The last Sect- the Black Flame. These guys also got a neat and tidy overhaul. Normally, they would likely be an Antagonist faction- as would the Children of the White Temple -but the opportunity to play a character like this is far too tempting to pass up. Picture a blend of the Refinement of Cobalt and Disquietism Transmutations. Imagine an Arisen, or even, say, a Leviathan capable of actually controlling and shaping Sybaris/the Wake mixed with the power of a Daeva's Awe. That is what the Dominion of Rapture does; it manipulates <i>Yara</i> directly. In the same way, the Dominion of Corruption does much of what the Contamination Transmutation class does. These Announced can be devils from your worst nightmares, or some of the hardest but most rewarding teachers.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><i>The Black Flame</i><br /> <br /> <u>History</u>: The group as it exists now is without a doubt the youngest of the Sects, even compared to the Children of the White Temple- but like the Children, Announced of similar thought and purpose have existed throughout history. There are always the heretics, and there are always the Shedim- in modern nights, the Sect known as the Black Flame dedicates its time to understanding the darker side of the Principle, of understanding Flux, of embracing the dark passion of it, and understandably their ranks are filled to bursting with Shedim on the run from far less accepting Sects like the Host and Kan Fravashi. Before they were Confirmed they were outcasts and exiles that pursued their passions in secret. They recognize that by their own desire, by their own will, they called the Divine Fire down- them and no one else. No one would take that from them, no one <i>could</i> take that from them; they would never have to serve again.<br /> <br /> Except they would serve again, this time under the Principle, and they would sit under the scrutiny of judgment again. They looked upon their peers and saw hypocrites and cowards unwilling to admit their all-too-human urges. In the company of those who should have lauded them, should have accepted them, they were still rejected. Eventually, they decided that if they <i>must</i> serve, if they <i>must</i> spend time in the company of holier-than-thou fools, and upholders of the status quo who deeply misunderstand the very nature of the Divine, then they would enjoy it. People forget that the first role of Satan was as Heaven\u2019s prosecutor.<br /> <br /> The Black Flame study the passion and madness of the Divine, why it affects some and not others. They indulge their own passions with a kind of iron-willed self-control that (usually) prevents them from going over the deep end, even as they tempt those without the strength to resist. They will push at the cracks in mind, body, and soul until their target either bends, breaks, or confesses in a cathartic release- and if they leave broken husks in their wake what do they care? They will serve as the barrier between the mundane and the Divine; they will assess the worth of those who seek the Divine Fire, and they will explore and acknowledge the facets of divinity that the rest of the Announced won\u2019t.<br /> <br /> <u>Modern Nights</u>: Perhaps it says something about the world that after the Host, the Black Flame are the most common Sect in the world- and not just because they unanimously accept the Shedim. They are too individualistic and disorganized a Sect to possess factions, but they do have overarching trends. Their philosophy is ultimately one of pragmatism- the universe is amoral and what is right and wrong can only be decided by the individual. Vice is a simple subset of human nature, and should be indulged. One should exploit their ability to affect change. However, none of that precludes taking steps to ensure their freedom, or taking action that could benefit them. Members of the Black Flame tend to have surprisingly high Exousia, though they are constantly flirting at the edge of it. Every moment they aren\u2019t \u2018on the clock\u2019 and a lot of the times they are, they move with their own utterly irreverent and potentially blasphemous flare for passion, madness, and indulgence.<br /> <br /> <u>Favored Maxim</u>: Ruach (Spirit of Vigor)<br /> <br /> <u>Dominions</u>: The Announced of the Black Flame are skilled in the Dominion of Rapture and exclusively teach the Dominion of Corruption.<br /> <br /> <u>Philosophy</u><br /> <br /> <b>Desire is the Whole of the Law:</b> Nothing short of Metatron, or the force of another Announced, can stop one of the Announced when they want something. Human morality, human laws, no longer apply to them save for when they wish it. As such, the responsible Announced must take all things in moderation.<br /> <br /> <b>Humanity is Animal:</b> Mankind is an animal, no matter how nicely they dress themselves up. Sometimes, we are better than the rest, but usually we are far worse. Through knowledge and spirituality, we have become the most vicious animal of all. The Announced of the Black Flame learn to use this insight to tempt and torment.<br /> <br /> <b>Glory is the Self:</b> When someone strikes your cheek, make sure they know whom they did that to. Vengeance rather than forgiveness, results rather than ideals, self-knowledge rather than delusional hypocrisy. Through their own excellence, their own self-worship, their own understanding, the Announced drew down the Fire and made themselves divine. They saw the order of things and tore it down. Not gods. Not kings. Only men.<br /> <br /> <b>The Fire is Change:</b> It simply doesn\u2019t care what you want, or what anyone wants. It cares not for morality, or choice. It makes Its efforts, It asserts Its will, and It knows only entropy or enthalpy. It only knows evolution or stagnation. Sublime, perfect, It is God and Satan both- but simply doesn\u2019t care for either title, for those are the things of mortals. Emulate It in all things.<br /> <br /> <u>The Prodigal Demiurge</u>: It is a god, more than that, It\u2019s a god\u2019s god, and It still desires nothing more than to reunite with the Principle. The Divine Fire may be a cosmic force of nature that deserves respect, study, and admiration, but to lose your individuality to it is a shame. The Announced of the Black Flame look to Metatron as an example of what not to do with their power.<br /> <br /> <u>Stereotypes</u><ul><li><b>The Host:</b> Sanctimonious hypocrites- you are everything I hate in the world</li> <li><b>Sefer Raziel:</b> You fight for what is accepted rather than what is new.</li> <li><b>Children of the White Temple:</b> Sad, delusional bastards.</li> <li><b>Kan Fravashi:</b> They are close to understanding, but they hold themselves back.</li> </ul><ul><li><b>The Shedim:</b> They know the lessons implicitly. For them, the abyss blinked.</li> </ul><ul><li><b>Vampires:</b> Oh, I <i>like</i> you.</li> <li><b>Werewolves:</b> Well, I knew there was a beast in everyone, but this is ridiculous.</li> <li><b>Mages:</b> You and I could do so much.</li> <li><b>Demons:</b> Sorry guys, but I\u2019m the real deal.</li> <li><b>Mortals:</b> I remember being like you- it was awful.</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-16T19:30:39-07:00","id":704147,"title":"","content":"And now, the new rules on character generation. This has changed quite a bit from the original, but it will be quite a bit better. Big thank-you to Ben Quo for letting me lift parts of his Promethean homebrew, specifically ideas about Beacons and Burdens, in order to apply those concepts here- even if they aren't explained in full at this point in time, I figured it would be a good idea to acknowledge the help.<br /> <br /> <b>Chapter 2: Divine Emanations</b><br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">This chapter introduces the Announced from the events leading to their Confirmation to the spectacular powers they now wield. Character creation rules are presented first, followed by the new Advantages of Glory, Maxims, Lux, and Exousia. New merits specially designed for these angelic characters and the details of the powers known as Dominions and Edicts will cap off the chapter.<br /> <br /> <u>Character Creation</u><br /> <br /> Quite a few of those called by the <i>qashmallim</i> never really make it to the ranks of the Announced. For some reason or another, they never push themselves to the point of drawing down the Fire with the sheer force of their obsession, passion, prowess, etc. Those who go on to become Announced are fairly rare.<br /> <br /> In building your character, there are several aspects you need to remember. There is the human; consumed by an obsession, possessed by a passion, enamored with a cause, exalting in their skill, or exerting their authority. This is the person who achieved Glory, and no one else. There is the restrained and frustrated divinity, the angelic identity assumed by the human. Most would love to cut loose and revel in their divine prowess, but they are restrained by the dictates and whims of the Principle. How they obey, when they obey, and <i>if</i> they obey can define their angelic identity. And then there\u2019s their identity within Announced society- how they seek out the limits of their freedom and push the boundaries of their Exousia. But more importantly, how they seek Communion with their own divine nature and the Pleroma as a whole. A strong <b>Irin</b> character should combine all of these traits.<br /> <br /> <b>Step One: Concept</b><br /> <br /> Were the Announced pushed or lead to divinity? What pushed them past the limits of what was merely human? Their focus as a human was ultimately singular, as defined above. It was their core, their purpose in life. However, the Confirmation is more or less a liberating, traumatic experience because it forces them to recognize that this isn\u2019t everything. You should recognize the same; a concept is a core, not the whole. The concept should be short, more than a sentence or two should be reduced to its bare essence. Most only comprise a few words: \u201cConscientious soldier\u201d, \u201cImpassioned activist\u201d, \u201cKing of the heavens\u201d. However, don\u2019t be afraid to push it beyond the point of glory and into terror- there\u2019s a reason angels need to say \u2018do not be afraid\u2019. \u201cUnholy foot soldier\u201d, \u201cDivine retribution\u201d, and \u201cJealous god\u201d work just fine. And while a concept that inherently breaches Obedience can be interesting to work with, make sure to not make your character virtually impossible to play.<br /> <br /> <b>Step Two: Select Attributes</b><br /> <br /> Before the Confirmation, the Announced excelled. What they excelled at is a question with virtually infinite answers, but ultimately they excelled due to raw, natural talent. These are things that can\u2019t really be learned, but rather express innate traits. The massively strong weight lifter might not be a skilled combatant and vice-versa. It is possible to improve these, but it takes significant effort. With regard to rules, these are split into three categories- Physical, Mental, and Social. Physical Attributes are Strength, Dexterity, and Stamina. Mental Attributes are Intelligence, Wits, and Resolve. Social Attributes are Presence, Manipulation, and Composure. In making your character, you\u2019ll need to prioritize these traits according to your vision of the character. You get 5 dots to distribute to the highest category, 4 to the middle category, and 3 to the lowest. Remember that every Attribute starts at *, and can generally never be reduced to zero.<br /> <br /> In prioritizing Attributes, remember your concept, as well as a Vocation and Sect if you have one in mind. Don\u2019t feel obligated to play to type, however. There are <i>Erelim</i> who are intellectual glory hounds, and <i>Malakim</i> who fight for \u201cThe Cause\u201d with divine fire and an AK-47. But more than that, they should have changed a little since the Confirmation.<br /> <br /> <b>Step 3: Select Skills</b><br /> <br /> Attributes are raw talent, and Skills are hard-earned accomplishment. These are easier to gain, but also narrower in scope. The Announced might be divine, but they can\u2019t expect to be good at <i>everything</i>. When creating an Announced character, consider the skills that enabled them to pursue the thing that led them to Confirmation and have come in handy since then. If an <i>Ishim</i> has no skill in Weaponry or Brawl, why do they think that they won\u2019t need to fight? Are they a master of Stealth, shadowing the targets of their mission unseen? Are they masters of Empathy, only arriving after the destruction has been wreaked and cleaning up the pieces? Also bear in mind the Skills one of the Announced needs to survive in their day-to-day life, and the potential hostilities of their own people and of the World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> As with Attributes, Skills fall into three categories- Mental, Physical, and Social. These also must be prioritized. You receive 11 dots for the primary category, 7 for the secondary, 4 for the tertiary. Unlike Attributes, Skills don\u2019t start with an automatic dot.<br /> <br /> <b>Step 4: Select Skill Specialties</b><br /> <br /> A Skill Specialty defines a narrow application of a Skill. They can be summed up in one or two words, and lend an extra die to any roll made with the parent Skill in which the Specialty applies. A specific Specialty can only be bought once, but multiple Specialties can be bought for one Skill and some Specialties apply to multiple skills. At least one dot is needed in the Specialty\u2019s Skill. An <b>Irin</b> character starts with four Specialties, one of which <i>must</i> apply to their method of Confirmation.<br /> <br /> <b>Step 5: Apply Announced Template</b><br /> <br /> <b>Vocation: </b>Each Confirmation is unique, but they fall into specific patterns in which they can be grouped together by similarity. These categories, called Vocations, define the means by which one of the Announced draws down the Fire into Confirmation and becomes one of the Irin.<ul><li><b><i>Ishim </i></b><b>(Torches):</b> Masters of a chosen field, consumed by an obsession that won\u2019t let them go. The <i>Ishim</i> are scrutinizers of detail, perfectionists, individuals focused on the successes and failures of other individuals.<ul><li>They are naturally adept at the Dominion of Conflagration and the Dominion of Portents.</li> </ul></li> <li><b><i>Erelim</i></b><b> (Visionaries):</b> Ambitious lords of spectacle, full of a need for the spotlight and a passion for the attentions of others. The <i>Erelim</i> are proud, seemingly limitless, and adore the spotlight.<ul><li>They are naturally adept at the Dominion of the Firmament and the Dominion of the Crucible.</li> </ul></li> <li><b><i>Elohim</i></b><b> (Powers): </b>Modern-day regional monarchs, with the need to rule and exert control. The <i>Elohim</i> are entitled lords of men, natural-born leaders, who dominate with God-given power and political skill.<ul><li>They are naturally adept at the Dominion of the Depths, and the Dominion of Divinity.</li> </ul></li> <li><b><i>Malakim </i></b><b>(Princes):</b> Prophetic activists armed with the fires of heaven, and a cause to empower. The <i>Malakim</i> are charismatic, inscrutable, and impassioned, willing to fight for the greater good regardless of cost.<ul><li>They are naturally adept at the Dominion of Humanity and the Dominion of the Luminaries.</li> </ul></li> <li><b><i>Cherubim</i></b><b> (Wardens):</b> Obsessive protectors and guardians with a tendency to throw themselves in harm\u2019s way. The <i>Cherubim</i> are sympathetic companions and fiercely loyal allies, for whom no cost is too high to protect a loved one.<ul><li>They are naturally adept at the Dominion of Creation, and the Dominion of Flesh</li> </ul></li> <li>Refer to the end of the Vocations section in Chapter 1 for Shedim Vocations and the Dominions associated with them.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Sect: </b>Like-minded groups of Announced united by philosophy, religion, or ideals- the Sects have been the way that the Announced attempt to unite themselves since time immemorial. While it is ideal for a character at creation to be affiliated with at least one Sect- to the point of membership -some may wish to hold off their decision until after a chronicle has started and they have a better grasp of the different factions.<ul><li><b>The Host (Choristers, Altar Boys):</b> Sincere faith mixed with corrupt power brokers. The Church of the Announced, the Host has been the status quo for well over a thousand years. They believe in a natural order to things, which includes subservience to the Principle. Known for drawing strength from their beliefs and service to others.<ul><li>They most effectively teach the lore of the Dominion of Vitality and exclusively teach the Dominion of Hymns.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Sefer Raziel (Philosophers, Bluestockings): </b>Ancient mysticism and quantum physics joined as one. The members of Sefer Raziel seek out ancient knowledge, and new secrets that can be teased from the Pleroma. In understanding the heavens, they can control its powers and the earth.<ul><li>They most effectively teach the lore of the Dominion of Realms and exclusively teach the Dominion of Tongues.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Children of the White Temple (Igigi, Idols):</b> Well-intentioned rebellion and god-like confidence. The members of the Children of the White Temple seek escape from the Principle, and the restoration of a divine empire of the Announced. In searching out ancient relics, they shall find the way back into heaven to reclaim godhood.<ul><li>They most effectively teach the lore of the Dominion of Ephemera, and exclusively teach the Dominion of <i>Mes</i>.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Kan Fravashi (Magi, Nostalgics): </b>A philosophy of altruism with the pride of legacy. The members of Kan Fravashi seek to improve themselves and others through their actions, and see it as their mission to guide mortals toward right action. Naturally, order shall be restored when they once again command united Announced society.<ul><li>They most effectively teach the lore of the Dominion of Souls, and exclusively teach the Dominion of <i>Daena</i>.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>The Black Flame (Daemons, Goths):</b> Extreme individualists and pragmatists. The members of the Black Flame seek an end to hypocritical creeds, where every individual can pursue their agendas as they please. If they aren\u2019t skilled enough to keep up, that\u2019s their problem.<ul><li>They most effectively teach the lore of the Dominion of Rapture, and exclusively teach the Dominion of Corruption.</li> </ul></li> </ul><br /> <b>Maxims:</b> Ways to explore the Announced soul and increase its glorious radiance, these paths to understanding and Communion are badges of accomplishment, milestones on a pilgrimage, and an observable measure of divine power. You begin play with four dots of Maxims, plus a free dot in your Vocation and Sect Maxim- if they are one and the same, double up. At character generation, only a single Maxim may remain without any dots, and you can only purchase the fifth dot of a Maxim provided you have at least one dot in every other Maxim. At character creation they may buy additional Maxims for three merit points per dot.<br /> <br /> <b>Dominions and Edicts:</b> Tied implicitly to the soul-expanding power of Maxims, you begin play with six dots of Maxims to divide up between one of their Vocation Dominions, a Common Dominion, or their Sect Dominion. Each Maxim dedicated into the Aspect of one of the Dominions. A dot of any Maxim that lines up with that favored by their Vocation or the Sect freely unlocks a new Aspect of that Dominion. If the Announced\u2019s Sect and Vocation Maxim line up, they will have to choose whether to unlock new Aspects of one of their Vocation Dominions, or their Sect Dominion. They begin play with a single Edict, but if they have one Maxim at five, they gain another for free at character generation.<br /> <br /> <b>Celestial Form: </b>The Announced may only possess a number of Blessings with a certain Maxim as a prerequisite as they have dots in that Maxim. They can have a number of Sect Blessings equal to their Sect Status +1, and gain one freely upon joining the Sect that doesn\u2019t count towards the tally. The Announced begins play with a number of Regalia equal to their Exousia, and may never have more Regalia than their Exousia. They begin play with one Visage, though Visages are limited more by Glory than by anything else.<br /> <br /> <b>Passion and Purpose:</b> No longer mere mortals, the Announced are not tied to their former Virtue or Vice, but instead to new sources of Willpower selected in part by their means of Confirmation and in part by the will of the Principle. This reflects the new dichotomy of their identity. Their Purpose is what the Principle desires them to be, what their Exousia pushes them to be in order to preserve it. In contrast, their Passion is what is left of their human life, the thing that pushed them to Confirmation in the first place. The Purpose is the angel they have become; the Passion is the person who earned it.<br /> <br /> <b>Name and Title:</b> Much like the Touchstones of Kindred and Uratha, the Announced also have ways of maintaining their identity. Their Celestial Name was gained during the Confirmation, thundered into their soul by the \u2018voice\u2019 of the Prodigal Demiurge. It represents their Purpose, and therefore their position as a servant of the Divine. Their Title represents something tied to their Passion, maybe a person place or thing, that reminds them of where they\u2019ve come from, that they have <i>earned</i> their power and freedom, and that while they serve a greater divinity, they are themselves divine.<br /> <br /> <b>Step 6: Select Merits</b><ul><li>The Announced have 10 dots to spend on Merits at character creation. They can also be used to buy Glory at the rate of three Merit dots a point. For each Merit the player buys, consider whether it applies to the character\u2019s pre-Confirmation human life, or their post-Confirmation angelic life. Sanctity of Merits applies. The Announced may not possess the following Merits: Iron Skin, Iron Stamina, Iron Stomach, Natural Immunity, and Toxin Resistance. All of these are made irrelevant by some of the innate abilities of the Announced. Naturally, any Supernatural Merits, or supernatural benefits granted by Mystery Cult Initiation, do not apply. For a list of Announced-specific Merits, refer to later in this chapter. For a list of other Merits, consult the <b>God-Machine Chronicles</b>.</li> <li><b>Special Considerations:</b> You begin play with five free dots to divide between Magisterium and (potentially) Cult.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Step 7: Determine Advantages</b><ul><li><b>Beacons: </b>Replacing simple Aspirations are Beacons, the means by which the Announced pursue the self-exploration that leads to the production of Ichor and their ability to enhance their Maxims. Their first Beacon is tied to their current Mission, and more loosely their Purpose- through it they may discover what it is the Principle intends for them. Their second Beacon is tied to their Passion, the means through which they achieved Confirmation in the first place. The third is for them to decide, but it is likely related to a personal goal beyond their Passion. If they possess a Cult, the Cult\u2019s goals serve as their third Beacon. Whenever the Announced resolves a Beacon, they are free to replace it with one of their own.</li> <li><b>Other Advantages:</b> Calculate as you would for a mortal character.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Step 8: Age and Experience</b><ul><li>Irin characters don\u2019t begin play with Experience by default. However, a Storyteller may grant a more seasoned character bonus Experiences, as follows:<ul><li>New Wings: 10 Experiences</li> <li>Established Chorister: 15 Experiences</li> <li>Guardian: 20 Experiences</li> <li>Archangel: 30 Experiences</li> <li>Divine Presence: 45 Experiences</li> </ul></li> </ul><br /> <b>Step 9: Confirmation</b><ul><li>At this point, the mechanics of character creation have been handled, and according to the rules the character is finished. However, your character is still probably missing some spark of life that makes them unique and vibrant. What do the Attributes and Skills say? What was his home life like? How was his childhood? Do they know they were Announced? Would they have believed it before the Confirmation? Do they see humans as equals, companions, and potential lovers? Do they see humans as beneath their notice, unable to understand what they\u2019re privileged with? Do they have dots in Intimidation? Why? Do they use cool intellect to shake those they\u2019re opposed to, brute force, or the sheer power of their presence? How and why did they pick up Skills? Are they still of use to them after the Confirmation? What\u2019s changed about the way they use their Skills? What did they do <i>before</i> the Confirmation?</li> <li>As the game is typically social, the <b>faith</b> (a group of Announced) is the core of it. Obsessions, ambitions, frustrations, powers, and limitations all simmer to the boiling point. It\u2019s best; therefore, to make sure the group is established before the chronicle begins, so they don\u2019t find themselves at each other\u2019s throats. However, once everything\u2019s been sorted out the chronicle can begin days, weeks, months, years, decades or even centuries later.<ul><li><b>Hot Seat:</b> The players each take turn being asked questions about their character by the other players. Remember that you\u2019re answering as an author, not a character. Note that acknowledging you don\u2019t know how to answer a certain question is perfectly valid. Getting to know the characters means that the players will be able to act as though each character already knows the others well.</li> <li><b>Q&amp;A:</b> Each player writes two questions they would like answered, and passes them to the Storyteller, who then asks each player to answer them. Remember to make sure all questions are suitable for the group, and bear in mind that some of the answers may inspire Aspirations- or engender future plot points from the Storyteller.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Anecdotes:</b> Each player picks another player\u2019s character and makes up an anecdote about something that character was involved in. Try to keep it down to one anecdote per character. If something is grossly out of character, or highly disagreeable to the player, they retain final verdict on the anecdote. But if it is only slightly out of character, try to work it in.</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T07:22:35-07:00","id":708724,"title":"","content":"And now, new information on Glory. These days, it's looking less like Azoth and more like Sekhem, though it shares more in common metaphysically with the former than the latter.<br /> <br /> <b>New Advantage: Glory</b><br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Limitless and unknowable, the Divine Fire flares throughout all creation. Pyros permeates the entire physical world in small quantities, wherever humans (and other beings) plan, act on impulse, build, tear down, create, and destroy. Demiurges steal the Divine Fire to create Prometheans. Alchemists bottle it in their experiments. Pandorans feast upon it. The Created burn it into Azoth like messy reactors. Far from simple magical fuel, though, Pyros <i>reacts</i>. It forms its own agenda- its own <i>Principle</i> -and coalesces into its own agents and advocates.<br /> <br /> Unlike the <i>qashmallim</i>, born from the alien thoughts and needs of an unknowable intelligence, the Announced are quite understandable. With their towering passions, rages, obsessions and ambitions they serve as excellent conduits with which to usher Pyros into the world. The Azoth of the Created builds like a furnace, sublimating ideas, experiences, and other esoteric ingredients into a human soul- an exceptionally \u2018pure\u2019 one at that, immune to the maddening touch of the Divine. But the Glory of the Announced is not a furnace, but an inferno. It roars to terrifying and magnificent heights with specific intent, and disperses with time and use as the need that called it vanishes like burning fuel. Unless the Announced decide to risk their freedom of will and try to steal the Divine Fire, they generally reach the heights of their power only when they\u2019re \u201con the clock\u201d.<br /> <br /> <b>Effects</b><ul><li><b>Transhuman Potential: </b>While the Announced can indeed cultivate superhuman Attributes and Skills, they can only enjoy the benefits while they possess Glory 6 and above- and the current Glory rating caps the Attribute or Skill rating. Unusable dots become useable again when Glory increases. Glory also limits what level of an Edict they can use, as well as the amount they can boost their Attributes by spending Lux.</li> <li><b>Effortless Might: </b>The Announced are among the most powerful beings in the World of Darkness. Filled with a maelstrom of Divine Fire, they don\u2019t begin as supernatural weaklings, but instead begin with a Glory rating of 10 and watch as the Fire dwindles over time.</li> <li><b>Burnout: </b>After a certain period of time, the Announced uses their Glory as a dice pool during Burnout rolls.</li> <li><b>Glorious Radiance:</b> When the Announced is full of power and purpose, the towering mystic might of their Glory casts off them in waves, enabling them to sense other sources of Pyros nearby. See Chapter 3 for full rules.</li> <li><b>Possession Immunity:</b> So long as the Announced is in accord with the Principle, and still possesses a shred of Glory, they are untouchable. The Divine Fire is hot enough to melt ideas, to scorch power. Attempts to possess them fail, and the possessing entity suffers a number of points of Aggravated Damage equal to the Announced\u2019s current Glory rating.</li> <li><b>Crucible of the Will: </b>In desperate times, the Announced may sacrifice power for fortitude. As a reflexive action once per turn, the Announced may sacrifice a dot of Glory to refill their entire Willpower pool.</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div><br /> And now, the brand-new advantage of Maxims. Part Renown, part Pillars, mixed with pilgrimage and self-discovery flavour that permeates the Divine Fire as a whole, hopefully you guys appreciate the new development.<br /> <br /> <b>New Advantage: Maxims</b><br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">The Divine Fire is transformative. Perhaps it destroys, perhaps it brings together, but it is always change. As such, the Announced cannot be exposed to it time and again without learning and growing. Unlike most things in the World of Darkness, Maxims are measureable indicators of the understanding of the Divine and Its relationship to themselves and the mortal world. When the Announced learns, grows, and completes goals of their own in response to lessons learned, the Divine Fire blossoms and grows in their soul, expanding into their Maxims. This process of learning about oneself and the Divine is called Affirmation, and much like Renown, each act should be a step up from the last in some way. Maxims are used as prerequisites and dice pools for various power- including Blessings and Regalia -and unlock Aspects of Dominions.<br /> <br /> Every time you buy a new dot in a Maxim, you immediately unlock a new Aspect of a Dominion, \u2018dedicating\u2019 that Maxim dot to the Dominion. Ultimately, as Vocation Dominions <i>both</i> require the Dominion Maxim, you will have to make choices based on specialization, as you can only have five dots of one Maxim.<br /> <br /> Maxims are collectively separated into five categories representing different kinds of understanding and dedication to the Divine. Maxims are measured in dots and have a maximum of five.<br /> <br /> <b><i>Nefesh</i></b><b> (</b>Living Soul<b>): </b>Awareness of the physical world is a gift that is rarely appreciated, since most people observe life passively. Human senses are actually astonishingly sharp, and merely lack the total focus and ability to screen sensory information that comes as second nature to animals. With focus, a perfectly ordinary human can hear a watch ticking twenty feet away, they can smell one drop of perfume in a six-room house, they can taste a teaspoon of sugar in a gallon of water, they can feel the brush of a fly\u2019s wing, and see a single candle alight around 30 miles away. An Announced who dedicates themselves to studying Nefesh can do all that and more. By exploring the animating force of their body- their souls -and the Pyros they\u2019re made of, the Announced encourages their union, melding Divine and Phenomenal together to push themselves above and beyond human limits. Those who make a study of Nefesh are constantly pushing the limits of their physical experience, trying to become even more aware of their body and how it connects to their soul. In mentality, the student of Nefesh is like a rock- implacable, immovable. Their greatest happiness is a relentless pursuit of their goals, throwing all their efforts into what they believe. In the pursuit of Communion, action becomes the primary means of understanding their own soul and those of others.<ul><li><b>Example Affirmations: </b>Fight to protect your Magesterium, lead another to a revelation, reach the point of total exhaustion, best a Pandoran, protecting someone else- physically or otherwise.</li> </ul><br /> <br /> <b><i>Ruach </i></b><b>(</b>Spirit of Vigor<b>): </b>The Divine Fire is always drawn to obsession, desperation, and the mad vortex of emotions that leads to spectacular acts of creation. However, the students of Ruach feel the connection between emotion and Pyros keenly. Perhaps if they can understand <i>why </i>the two are connected, they can understand the Principle, Metatron and Their motivations. Perhaps. You will not find so loyal a friend or an enemy so spiteful. Their emotions roil like the inferno of Glory within them- their love burns and their hatred freezes. Though the students of Nefesh define themselves by action, it is the passionate disciples of Ruach that are known for being impulsive and reacting on instinct. To explore this Maxim, those who seek it push themselves to be unwavering in their convictions and beliefs, and to explore the extremes of emotion and sensation in ways that have to do more with how they feel than the actual experience. In the pursuit of Communion, they define themselves and others by their emotions and whether others suppress or act upon their feelings.<ul><li><b>Example Affirmations:</b> Succumb to Temptation, bring the perpetrator of an inhumane act to justice without resorting to mortal law, engaging in a intimate relationship (in any sense), earn recognition for an accomplishment, maintain your honor or integrity even if your enemies do not.</li> </ul><br /> <b><i>Neshama</i></b><b> (</b>Seat of Understanding<b>): </b>The Divine is unknowable and ineffable, but that certainly doesn\u2019t stop people from trying- least of all the Announced. Neshama is an effort to understand beyond the self-exploration of Nefesh and Ruach, but instead an exploration of the Divine directly at an intellectual level. Instead of structured, quantified existence, they look at origins, at coming into being from nothingness. The Irin following Neshama chase after Azoth to understand it; examine its continuous creation, the sustenance of life and bringing things to wholeness. The Shedim, naturally, pursue Flux and seek to contemplate the ultimate dissolution of things. There is indeed overlap between the two groups studying the other aspect of the Fire, but it is not nearly as fulfilling as examining the function of the Divine Fire that is tied to their soul. The students of Neshama require a greater understanding of \u201cwhy\u201d than most, but are also in the best position to have an answer to that question. They possess both unshakeable confidence and endless questioning in equal measure. In the pursuit of Communion, they define themselves and others by their understanding- by their willingness to question or their simple acceptance.<ul><li><b>Example Affirmations:</b> Survive a Firestorm, master an Edict or Dominion (Be able to access the powers of every Edict Tier or Dominion Aspect), teach another of the Announced to use Lux more effectively, make a dramatic insight.</li> </ul><br /> <b><i>Chaya</i></b><b> (</b>Life Divine<b>): </b>Perhaps, if one cannot try to define Divinity by what it <i>is</i> you can define it by what it is <i>not</i>. Those who study this Maxim seek not to understand the Divine Fire in its functions and processes, but instead by its limits. What, exactly, can Pyros do when pushed to the limit? Frankly, there isn\u2019t a lot it can\u2019t. These Announced gaze into the endless flames of the Divine, and the Divine gazes back. They can <i>feel</i> Its limitless power in a way others of the Announced can\u2019t, they push at the limits of their power because they know they don\u2019t actually exist. There is no limit they cannot surpass with time and effort. A dedicated student of Chaya would say that the Divine has consumed their ego, and now they exist only in union with it- but many who think that way haven\u2019t yet reached Communion, and likely never will. Their identity rests upon their self-assurance; they judge themselves and others by how they exercise their divinity. Only the truly bold, the truly transcendent, are worthy of their respect.<ul><li><b>Example Affirmations: </b>Set an \u201cimpossible\u201d goal and achieve it, construct a Magisterium, defeating a superior foe, fighting against overwhelming odds, creating an exceptional vision, spending Glory on a Dominion, Edict, or Celestial Form power.</li> </ul><br /> <b><i>Yechida</i></b><b> (</b>Oneness<b>): </b>Those enraptured by Yechida follow a simple creed- faith without action is dead, and so is divinity. What is the point of studying the Divine Fire, or pushing the limits of your power, of merging yourself harmoniously with the Pyros in your soul and in your heart, if it is not <i>directed</i> to something? They pursue their own causes and goals with as much- if not <i>even more</i> -fervor than the missions of the Principle, and if the two come into alignment the results are spectacular. Though emotions, thoughts, or even facts will not be interpreted the same way by the Principle or the Announced, results don\u2019t lie. Disciples of Yechida are always pushing themselves above and beyond the call of duty, often at great personal risk, in an effort to earn Communion through demonstrations of loyalty, dedication, and understanding. They judge themselves and others through their actions, and their dedication to a cause.<ul><li><b>Example Affirmations: </b>Charitable works, participation in a religious service, construct and maintain a list of wrongdoers and appropriate punishments, incite a group of mortals to action through words alone (Lux bolstered words count)</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T08:43:21-07:00","id":710655,"title":"","content":"Here's the new data on Lux, with a rather lovely table including Glory, Attribute and Skill limits, Edict limits, Visage limits, and the number of Attribute dots Exaltation has to work with. It also includes a new way to look at <i>fanes</i>- less like loci for the Divine Fire, and much more like ways the Divine Fire can be summoned into the world in a limited manner. I tied them rather implicitly to the Maxims, and I spent a lot of time reading over what creates <i>seba</i> in Book of the Deceived to get the feel right.<br /> <br /> As always, any feedback would be greatly appreciated.<br /> <br /> <b>New Advantage: Lux</b><br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">The Pleroma is described as a realm of \u201climitless light\u201d so it stands to reason that the Announced would call the power of the heavens <b>Lux</b> or \u201clight\u201d. Of the same origin of the Pyros used by Prometheans, it coalesces in similar ways. If Glory is a measure of the intensity of an Announced\u2019s access to the Pleroma, burning like a small star beneath their skin, it stands to reason that Lux is like plasma- unbound energy that radiates off of Glory like solar wind coming off of a star. Measured in points rather than dots, Lux is used to fuel the powers known as Dominions and Edicts, as well as facilitate the change into Celestial Form, and travel to the Pleroma. You are limited in how much Lux you can store by your Maxims and your Exousia, and how much you can spend per turn by your Glory- only being able to spend a number of points equal to their current Glory.<ul><li><b>Powers: </b>For Dominions and Edicts, spend Lux as written for the powers. It costs no Lux to assume Celestial Form, but at least one to change back, and potentially more to activate different abilities. If one is left without Lux while transformed, they are stuck in Celestial Form until they can get themselves more Lux. However, it can also be used to enter the Pleroma, something that will be discussed in the Appendix on that subject.</li> <li><b>Miraculous Healing:</b> The Divine Fire is more than the stuff of life; It is its author, its origin. Glory means it can be extraordinarily hard to kill one of the Announced, and depending on the situation, it just gets even more difficult. An Announced, by default, heals a number of points of Bashing damage equal to their Glory each turn. They may spend a point of Lux to heal Lethal damage, but this is obviously supernatural and inflicts Fear <i>Yara</i>. When the Announced heals Lethal damage, their injuries seem to light up from within, they are wreathed in spectral flame, and let off a smoke smelling heavily of incense. As an additional bonus, they cannot become sick through merely mortal means. See Chapter 3 for the other ways the Announced use their regenerative abilities.</li> <li><b>Exaltation:</b> Limitless in its potential and power, the Divine Fire can allow the Announced to push aside even their superhuman limitations for a brief period of time. By spending a point of Lux, the Announced using this ability gains a number of temporary Attribute dots in each trait depending on their current Glory for the rest of the scene and can add a number of Attribute dots <i>past</i> their current limits equal to their Exousia rating.</li> </ul><div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Glory</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Attribute and Skill Limits</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Edict Level</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Stam; Str, Dex</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Max Visages</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">10</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">10</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">6/5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">9</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">9</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5/4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">8</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">8</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4/4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">7</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">7</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4/3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">6</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">6</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3/3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3/2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2/2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2/1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1/1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1/0</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">0</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">See Exousia</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">See Exousia</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">See Exousia</td> </tr> </table></div> <b>Regaining Lux</b><br /> <br /> While effectively unlimited in the Pleroma- depending on the spot you can more or less pull Lux from the air or drink it like water -it is understandably much harder to come by in the physical world, but there are still some ways that the Announced can regain their power. What use would their powers be if they couldn\u2019t replenish the means to use them?<ul><li><i>Daily Allotment</i>: The easiest means of regaining Lux is simply to wait. Like radiation coming off a star, the Announced regains one point of Lux each sunrise- even if they cannot see the sun themselves.</li> </ul><ul><li><i>Other Announced</i>: Naturally, the Announced can trade Lux amongst themselves on a one-to-one basis. In human form, this requires physical contact and an act of will. In their Celestial Form, it is significantly more dramatic- requiring the Announced to breathe out a tongue of twisting flame that must be breathed in by the Announced receiving the Lux.</li> <li><i>Meditation</i>: By meditating one their tie to the Pleroma and to the Divine Fire, the Announced may regain points of Lux using their Exousia as a dice pool. When in their Magisterium, add their dots in Acoustics to their Exousia and roll it as a dice pool. If the Magisterium possesses Fane Resonance, follow the rules listed there.</li> </ul><ul><li><i>Supplication</i>: Described in full in Chapter 3, by submitting fully to the Principle they find their pool of Lux filled to bursting- and more is quite easy to come by.</li> </ul><br /> <i>Fanes</i><br /> <br /> The Divine Fire does not occur naturally in the mundane world. You can\u2019t extract Pyros from a forest fire, or a thunderstorm (Unless you\u2019re one of the Created, that is). Obsession, passion, and desperation don\u2019t <i>make</i> Pyros; they draw it down. In a similar way, there are places in the world of sublime beauty, of perfected symmetry, that constantly evoke awe, passion, obsession and desperation. These are called Fanes. Sometimes, they\u2019re locations or objects, other times they\u2019re occurrences. Whatever the case may be it draws down the Divine in a way that aligns with a particular Maxim. A fane created by a desperately beautiful performance only exists as long as the performance does, but in that period of time the door to the Pleroma is open and Lux is there for the Announced to take. The Announced may take a number of points of Lux equal to the dot rating of the Fane or their dots in that Maxim, whichever is lower, in a single day. The Storyteller should determine the degree of the Fane beforehand- some guidelines will be outlined below. A Shedim must pay Willpower equal to the degree if they wish to use a Fane.<ul><li><b><i>Nefesh</i></b>: A Fane tied to the power of the animating force of the body are driven by <i>revelations</i>: knowledge with an impact unimpeded by the organized self. By their very nature, it is difficult to find an enduring Fane attuned to Nefesh, but they also have a tendency towards the higher degrees. They are difficult to find, except for those who really know how to look, since they are mostly impossible to articulate in spoken words. When a revelation is captured, the individual it came from loses memory of the insight, which immediately subsumes to strengthen the inner self. Announced who prefer to gather revelations are insightful self-examiners, watching bemused from the sidelines, unable to express themselves.</li> <li><b><i>Ruach</i></b>: The human spirit is rarely expressed so profoundly as through art, and some would argue that it is <i>performances</i> that are art\u2019s highest form. A performance is expression made evident for the entire world to see. They look for the excellent-beyond-excellent. Repetition is preferred, but the singularly excellent, the kind of performance that will <i>never</i> be repeated, are always much higher in degree. Those who are, in fact, performing when the Announced harvests Lux tend to feel disconnect between their efforts and the audience. They feel exposed, embarrassed, or unappreciated; on some level they know the <i>performance</i> was flawless, but they are not satisfied\u2026 and it will drive them to perform again, with increasing success and less fulfillment.</li> <li><b><i>Neshama</i></b>: The tireless search for understanding is made brighter by <i>insights</i>. These are not found within dead texts, and empty words; they are found within the minds of humans. And while an insight can only be harvested once from the mind of an individual, when it is spread to another it can be harvested again. In the modern world, true insights are almost impossible to find amidst fields of limitless, disorganized information and so those Announced who uncovers a true insight will hold onto it tightly and spread it judiciously. Insights change degree from use to use, depending on how impressive it is to the one receiving it. When harvested, the insight seems less impressive, or the memory of it becomes hazy.</li> <li><b><i>Chaya</i></b><b>:</b> The aspirants to true divinity want to erode barriers and gaze upon testaments to an enduring power. They seek out <i>trances</i> and <i>visions</i>; physical actions that offer up their self in favor of subservience to the divine, and works of art- statues, paintings, or other physical pieces -that somehow embody it. When harvesting a trance, the physical action seems inconsequential, it no longer draws attention; reverence becomes disinterest. Visions seem like hollowed out hulks, empty pieces of art, no longer quite as impactful as they had been. Either way, ecstatic worship or phenomenal works of art that no longer impact the performer as before- encouraging them to perform again. Visions are the most likely fanes to be permanent and possess a static degree- unless they\u2019re defaced.</li> <li><b><i>Yechida</i></b>: Fighting endlessly in pursuit of their causes, the Announced beholden to this Maxim bask reverently in the light of <i>orations</i> and <i>inspirations</i>. Fierce words and fiercer passions pull at them, spontaneous explosions of artistic genius calls to them. The performance is not what calls to them, but the essential spark of delivery, the passion that precedes it. When harvested, orators feel the speeches turn to ashes in their mouth, feel their audiences fall away. Artists lose focus; lose their drive to create. Both are driven to seek it again; a good orator <i>constantly</i> speaks, a true artist <i>constantly </i>creates.</li> </ul><div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Degree</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Examples</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">A personal revelation, a great individual performance, fine art</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">A persuasive speech, unique use of an idea, a leap of logic connecting concepts</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Life-changing declaration, flawless performance, an oeuvre d\u2019art</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">A movement-starting speech, a truly original idea, an unexpected breakthrough</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Cosmic revelation, an incomparable masterpiece of art or performance</td> </tr> </table></div> <i>Wastes</i><br /> <br /> Much like Fanes channel Azoth, Wastes channel Flux, making it significantly easier for the Shedim to collect the power they need to help support their supernatural nature. There are generally two ways Wastes form- one is as a result from Flux Taint due to the presence of Pandorans or <i>lilithim</i>, and the other from the Announced choosing to mitigate the effects of <i>Yara</i> by choosing to mark the land instead. The exact mechanics of Wastes will be discussed alongside <i>Yara</i> in Chapter 3, and the implications of it will be discussed in the Storytelling section of Chapter 4.</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T07:28:25-07:00","id":712379,"title":"","content":"Their new Integrity analogue, Exousia. It's a bit more fidgety than before, with new ideas about what Chains are and what they do. More importantly, it also gives you options to get out of becoming Shackled. A big thank-you to Ben Quo for letting me use the idea of Burdens.<br /> <br /> <b>Modified Advantage: <i>Exousia </i>(Integrity)</b><br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> <i>\u201cDid you ever notice, how in the Bible, whenever God needed to punish someone, or make an example, or when God needed a killing, he sent an angel? Did you ever wonder what a creature like that must be like? A whole existence spent praising your God, but always with one wing dipped in blood. Would you ever really want to see an angel?\u201d</i><br /> - Thomas Dagget, \u2018The Prophecy\u2019<br /> <br /> Something should be made extremely clear here- angels are not moral beings, and neither are the Announced. They are authoritative, acting with license granted to them by the Almighty- they are punished not for doing things that are reprehensible to mortals, but for going against the will that commands them, and acting against union with the Divine. When an Assyrian army surrounded Jerusalem, and the king prayed for aid, God sent an angel that slaughtered the entire army in a night without a single man making a sound. The Assyrian general woke to find all his men butchered in the night, silently. When God wanted Gomorrah judged, he sent a pair of angels. They didn\u2019t balk at the potential rape, or even a father abandoning his daughters to a crowd of potential rapists as substitutes for his guests, they balked at the refusal to abide by sacred hospitality.<br /> <br /> The Announced are. Not. Moral.<br /> <br /> They can <i>be</i> moral, but by their nature, they obey the will of the Principle, enforced by the being they call Metatron. If God says turn a woman mourning the loss of her city to salt for gazing on its destruction, you do it. If God says kill every firstborn child in a country, then hang around to hear the screams of their parents, you go \u201cYes sir, when do I start smiting?\u201d That is the difference between a simple angel, and the Announced- the Announced, being formerly human, and ultimately separate from the Divine whole can <i>choose</i> what to do. They are not bound wholly to the will of God, they have free will- but the Principle certainly makes it worth their while to obey. To rebel, to be disobedient, is to lose some freedoms to Chains. Rebel often enough, and you\u2019re Shackled- completely under the control of the Principle. Conscious, perhaps protesting, but as bound to act out Its will as any <i>qashmal</i> or <i>lilitu</i>.<br /> <br /> Unlike regular Integrity, which starts at seven points out of ten, Exousia starts at three points out of ten. Instead of hitting a Breaking Point at a threshold equivalent to or below where they stand, they suffer a Breaking Point for actions undertaken equal to or <i>above</i> their current level of Exousia. Exousia, translated from Greek, comes across rather flatly as \u2018authority\u2019. However, a direct translation misses out on some of the nuances. Exousia isn\u2019t <i>just</i> \u2018authority\u2019. It is \u2018license to act\u2019, authority that has been given down from on high, the ability or strength with which one is imbued that is either merely possessed or- more appropriately -exercised. It can sometimes even be read as \u2018weight\u2019.<br /> <br /> Exousia, then, is the metaphysical union to the Principle the Announced feels- how in line the will of the Principle your own actions are. More cynically, it\u2019s a measure of how far you can push yourself before you break. How much slack you have and weight you can throw before Metatron panics and slaps another Chain on you. Oddly enough, Exousia often has little to do with direct <i>obedience</i> to the Principle, and significantly more to do with maintaining a union with the Divine. Abuse of Glory, use of Dominions and Edicts to fulfill acts that go against what the Divine Fire stands for, and many other things can result in a loss of Exousia that pushes the Announced closer to becoming one of the Shackled.<br /> <br /> However, unlike most Integrity meters, Exousia fluctuates wildly similarly to Clarity. To succeed at a Mission immediately awards the Announced with a dot of Exousia, while failing a Mission results in a universal Breaking Point as a Chance Roll. Beyond that, an Irin may artificially raise their Exousia by dedicating enough time to working on their relationship with the Divine Fire in order to be able to pay two experience points to increase it by a dot. As a starting character, you will need to be concerned with everything listed at three and above- because that will result in Breaking Points.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Exousia</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Breaking Points</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Number of Chains</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Entering the Pleroma, Concealing your identity, Spending more than one Lux a day (Five dice)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Interfering with another\u2019s Mission, Failing to meditate upon the Divine daily, altering another\u2019s behavior with a Dominion (Five dice)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Using Dominions to affect the outcome of a Mission, taking full Celestial Form on earth, Becoming emotionally connected with the subject of a mission (Five dice)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Revealing one\u2019s nature to a mortal, interfering in the lives of those in the purview of a mission while it is ongoing (Four dice)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Destroying a relic or artifact, defiling a fane, ie. Removing traces of the Divine Fire from the earth (Four dice)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">6</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">When the Principle\u2019s plans result in your Mission failing whether by intent or miscommunication, destroying a relic aligned with your Vocation or Sect Maxim (Three Dice)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">7</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Directly opposing one of the <i>qashmallim</i>, Turning a mortal into a <i>penitente</i> intentionally, altering the outcome of a Mission (Three dice)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">8</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Going against the command of a Mission (Two dice)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">9</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Attempting to wrest control away from the Principle during Supplication (Two dice)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">10</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Killing another Irin to make a relic, consuming a human soul (One dice)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> </tr> </table></div> Exousia does possess a number of universal Breaking Points that apply regardless of the number of dots you have dedicated to Exousia. On top of these, every player should have some personal Breaking Points that they will discuss proper application of with the Storyteller, in order to personalize their character and their relationship with the Divine- as you can see, a great deal of what would phase a mortal character is fair game for the Irin, and a whole lot of stuff that mortals wouldn\u2019t understand, or- worse yet -actually be able to do are on there as well. As you can see, rebellion is technically possible, but no one said it would be easy.<ul><li>Depriving an individual of their free will with a Dominion or Edict</li> <li>Burning through their Glory to get \u2018off the clock\u2019</li> <li>Attempting to preserve Glory by pursuing other projects of the Principle</li> <li>Forming or expanding a Cult dedicated to yourself</li> </ul><br /> When making a roll for an Exousia Breaking Point, use the number of dice listed beside the dots at which the Breaking Point is suggested. Universal breaking points use five dice. If the threshold is <i>below</i> the character\u2019s current Exousia rating (so at character creation that would be two or less) then there is no need to roll.<br /> <br /> <i>Suggested Modifiers</i><ul><li>Character is protecting themselves: +1</li> <li>Character is protecting loved ones: +2</li> <li>Character is acting in accordance with their Passion: -1</li> <li>Exousia Breaking Point is 5 or more dots above their current rating: -3</li> <li>Character is acting in accordance with their Purpose: +1</li> <li>Exousia 8-10: +2</li> <li>Exousia 6-7: +1</li> <li>Character has meditated on the Divine in the past day: +2</li> <li>Character has meditated in their Magisterium recently: +1 per dot of Acoustics</li> </ul><br /> <i>Roll Results</i><ul><li><b>Dramatic Failure: </b>The Announced can feel the gap between themselves and the Principle widening, edging their identity towards the endless furnace that is the Divine Fire. They immediately lose a dot of Exousia, and gain a permanent Chain. They also gain the Cuffed, Madness, or Delusions of Divinity Condition.</li> <li><b>Failure:</b> The Announced begins to feel their Chains pull tight, and sorrow as the blazing light of their Glory flickers. They lose a dot of Exousia and gain the Detached, Chastened, or Haunted Condition. Roll to resist Chains with a +2.</li> <li><b>Success:</b> The Announced manages to skate by with their relationship to the Divine intact. They keep their Exousia rating, though their opinion about what they have done may vary. Apply the Guilty or Inspired Condition. Roll to resist Chains normally. Take an Ichor Beat.</li> <li><b>Exceptional Success:</b> Not only have they pulled through the Breaking Point, but they have done so magnificently, reaffirming their connection to the Divine and their own sense of self. Roll to resist Chains with a -2. Take an Ichor Beat and regain a point of Lux.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Chains</b><br /> <br /> The Announced call them Chains, but to look at them with purely philosophical and metaphysical trappings, they are direct connections to the Principle. The Principle Itself- if It can truly think -sees nothing but good in this. Another piece of the Fire is coming home; It is one step closer to being whole. The Announced, however, have a very different opinion. Further union to the will of the Principle aligns them with it, prevents them from acting against it, and restricts their personal freedom. Metatron- though as far from the Announced in thought as the Principle is from It -knows that the Announced fear connection to the Divine for the same reason It craves nothing more in the universe; loss of identity, loss of separateness of persons. The Fire is one, and after untold millennia of loneliness, Metatron wants to never be lonely again. The Announced, on the other hand, have always been individuals. Loneliness to them is quite different than Metatron\u2019s loneliness, and as an extension of the natural fear of death all living things possess; they fear the loss of their own identity, their own ability to recognize that they are, in fact, alive.<br /> <br /> Chains generally come in three variations- Bans, Revelations, and Emanations. Bans are behavioral in nature, either much like the bans of ghosts, God-Machine Angels, and <i>Hisil</i> Spirits, or more general psychological or social tics. Revelations are changes- sometimes dramatic, to the Announced\u2019s body that reveal their own inhuman nature. Emanations are environmental changes caused by the Irin\u2019s direct presence, and as such often herald their arrival. The Announced roll Resolve + Composure to resist gaining a Chain, and apply any bonuses or penalties listed above for Exousia Breaking Points as the opposite for this purpose. The Announced cannot spend Willpower on this roll.<br /> <br /> The sort of Chain they gain is more thematic and connected to whatever led to the situation than something that should be governed by game mechanics. Chains are always deeply personal; otherwise they wouldn\u2019t hurt nearly as much. Some aren\u2019t so much punishing certain behaviors, as they are encouraging others; it is entirely feasible for Chains (usually Bans) to serve the advancement of the Announced\u2019s mission. They also last a certain period of time dependent on their Glory rating at the time of affliction unless it is a permanent Chain, and have a tendency to repeat and increase in severity.<br /> <br /> Chains can be gained permanently due to an extreme decrease or increase in Exousia, as noted in the table above. If they have gained a particular Chain (even in varying severities) a number of times more than their lowest Maxim, make a note on the character sheet at the level of Exousia they gained it at. They may suppress the Chain with a Burden, but if they ever drop to that level of Exousia again the Chain comes back.<br /> <br /> <i>Roll Results</i><ul><li><b>Dramatic Failure: </b>No Chain occurs, make a note to add two dice to the next roll to resist Chains the Announced has to endure.</li> <li><b>Failure:</b> The Announced gains a minor Chain</li> <li><b>Success: </b>The Announced gains a major Chain</li> <li><b>Exceptional Success:</b> The Announced gains a catastrophic Chain</li> </ul><br /> <i>Examples of Chains</i><ul><li><b>Bans- </b>The Announced applies a -3 penalty to any roll that would break the Ban. If the Ban is not a direct behavior but instead a taboo, apply a -2 penalty to all actions until the Announced corrects their mistake. What level of Ban the Announced gains also alters the severity of the penalty, which stacks with the penalties listed here.<ul><li><b>Minor (</b>-1<b>):</b> A simple social or physical tic, or a basic taboo that is easily avoided or explained.<ul><li><b>Ex. </b>Must make a sign of respect when entering sacred ground. Cannot imbibe alcohol. Loses a point of Lux for every day without human contact.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Major (</b>-2<b>): </b>Defining taboos and personality traits that are not only noticeable, but also exploitable.<ul><li><b>Ex. </b>Cannot accept anything by hand. Loses all Willpower for more than a day spent avoiding a mission. Forbidden to enter an area with significant Flux rating.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Catastrophic (</b>-3<b>): </b>Obvious, extreme eccentricities that cannot be avoided at all.<ul><li><b>Ex. </b>Must obey an individual carrying a seal with their Name and Title. Must punish adulterers. Must inspire a mortal artist once a month or lose all Lux.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> <li><b>Revelations-</b> The Divine Fire is transformative, able to shape life forms in unexpected and sometimes unusual ways. Revelations are expressions of how the Divine Fire has altered the soul of the Announced manifested on their body. Depending on the nature of the changes, they add a +2 to Social rolls if they are visible to mortals and the Announced is specifically using them as a marker of religious or supernatural power. However, some horrific changes make a -2 penalty, unless they\u2019re used for Intimidation.<ul><li><b>Minor (</b>+/- 1<b>):</b> Easily concealed markings, minor changes in diet or physique.<ul><li><b>Ex. </b>Faint scent of incense or olive oil. Hair changes to a different, natural, color. Scar tissue develops resembling ritual flagellation.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Major</b> <b>(</b>+/- 2<b>): </b>Visible changes, inconvenient alterations to body chemistry.<ul><li><b>Ex. </b>Figure and face become androgynous. Tattoo-like glyphs of angelic script cover sections of their body. Hair falls out or becomes extremely coarse.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Catastrophic (</b>+/- 3<b>):</b> Obviously supernatural, impossible to hide or explain.<ul><li><b>Ex. </b>Eyes turn to flame. Manifestations of the Celestial Form. Blood becomes silver or gold. Body becomes luminescent.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> <li><b>Emanations- </b>The Glory of the Announced cannot always be held back. <i>Yara</i> pours off them in waves, and overt displays of the Fire only worsen it. Add a -2 penalty to a mortal\u2019s ability to resist <i>Yara</i>. Emanations never manifest in Announced with less than Glory 6 at the time of receiving their Chain.<ul><li><b>Minor (</b>-1<b>):</b> Slight changes to the environment that aren\u2019t immediately traceable to the Announced.<ul><li><b>Ex. </b>Subtle changes in temperature. Tricks of the light evocative of appropriate symbology.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Major (</b>-2<b>): </b>Obvious but not supernatural alterations to reality.<ul><li><b>Ex. </b>Distortions of sound to resemble music or hymns. Rising water levels. Animals flee or become docile.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Catastrophic (</b>-3<b>):</b> Overtly supernatural, dramatic warping of physics that are obviously following the Announced.<ul><li><b>Ex. </b>Objects ignite but do not burn. Statues weep tears of blood. Nearby ghosts become visible and begin begging for mercy.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> </ul><br /> <i>Hotline to God</i><br /> <br /> Chains are, in essence, direct connections to the Principle, so shouldn\u2019t there be <i>some</i> advantages to having them? Well, that\u2019s up to you. Maybe clues to the mission of the Announced are written in the angelic script branded in their skin. Maybe another serves as a countdown to some foretold point. Ultimately, regardless of any inconvenience they lead to for the Announced, they are <i>still</i> direct connections to something that is almost definitely the source of souls, and could possibly be the capital-G God if your Storyteller so desires. It certainly fits the part. The connection might be applied and manipulated by Metatron, but It cannot take away <i>everything</i> positive about it. Chains suck, but only if you intend to follow your own agenda instead of the Principle\u2019s mission. Keep that in mind when designing Chains. They might impede your own goals, but that same Chain will almost certainly push you towards acting in accord with the Principle.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Glory</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Transient Duration (whichever is less)</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">10</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Once Chronicle/month</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">9</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Three Stories/weeks</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">8</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Two Stories/weeks</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">7</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">One Story/week</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">6</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Three Chapters/days</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Two Chapters/days</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">One Chapter/day</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Three Scenes/hours</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Two Scenes/hours</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">One Scene/hour</td> </tr> </table></div> <b>Advantages &amp; Disadvantages of Exousia</b><br /> It is difficult to flawlessly follow the ways of Exousia. It is almost always challenging, and occasionally nearly impossible, to obey the will of the Principle and rigorously maintain a code enabling the responsible use of power. Power calls to be used, power is a means to an end; power is easy to abuse, easy to rely upon. And while there is interplay between storing \u2018credit\u2019 with the Principle to take action later, and constantly striving against It, there are both benefits and drawbacks to high and low Exousia.<ul><li><b>Advantages</b><ul><li>For every dot of Exousia over five, they gain +1 to Breaking Points if they have meditated on their Name. For every dot of Exousia below five, they gain +1 to Breaking Points if they have meditated on their Title.</li> <li>+1 for every dot above seven for Social rolls made against <i>qashmallim</i> and other inhabitants of the Pleroma.</li> <li>+1 for every dot above seven for rolls to navigate the Pleroma.</li> <li>Dots of Exousia allow for the storage of extra Lux.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Disadvantages</b><ul><li>-1 to -3 to enter the Pleroma (-1 for Obedience of 4 or 5, -3 for Obedience of 3 or lower). You\u2019re out of favor with the Principle- why should you be let into the heavens?</li> <li>- 1 for each dot below 4 to rolls made to navigate the Pleroma. You are no longer welcome in this place, and it resists you at every turn.</li> <li>-1 per dot below 4 on all Social dice pools involving interacting with <i>qashmallim</i> and other inhabitants of the Pleroma. They can tell how disagreeable you are to their purpose.</li> </ul></li> </ul><br /> <b>The Shackled</b><br /> <br /> Some unlucky Announced are so closely tied to the Principle, be it by constant testing the borders of Exousia or simple bad luck, that they lose all free will entirely. When an Announced gains a number of Chains more than their Exousia +1, they become Shackled. They are entirely bound to the will of the Principle; they exist to fulfill its Missions. They cannot deviate, they do not avoid them, and often they have no recollection of time spent \u2018on the clock\u2019. They simply regain consciousness <i>after</i> their Mission has been completed, and try to discern what just happened along with handling any social issues that may have arisen as a result. Of course, having completed their Mission, they are also hemorrhaging Glory and that could leave them in a very bad spot very quickly. While it is not <i>impossible</i> to play one of the Shackled, it does call for a very specific kind of play- one where you\u2019re chasing after actions you can\u2019t remember performing, and dealing with social problems you can\u2019t remember instigating. Whether you\u2019d prefer for a Shackled character to become a Storyteller character or to carry on bravely and keep playing is entirely up to the group.<br /> <br /> <i>There but for the Grace of God\u2026</i><br /> <br /> The attentive reader will notice that above in the section describing Glory, it mentions that a Glory of zero is technically possible. While the full rules for that will be described in Chapter 3, it will be mentioned here briefly that when an Announced runs out of the power of Glory, Exousia substitutes. Dots of Exousia stand in for dots of Glory for the purposes of determining regeneration, Exaltation, and the resistance of various supernatural powers.<br /> <br /> <b>Breaking Chains</b><br /> <br /> It is possible for the Shackled to shed their Chains, or for one of the Announced terrified of becoming Shackled to avoid that fate- however it is neither easy, nor is it always preferable. Either by sacrificing a dot of Glory, or an encounter with a <i>qashmal</i>, the Announced may shed a Chain in favor of taking up a Burden. If taking up a Burden is suggested by the <i>qashmal</i>, the Announced may refuse- gaining a Willpower point (and the <i>qashmal</i> gains its Rank in Pyros). However, if they accept the <i>qashmal\u2019s</i> Burden, or offer it up themselves, they gain 1-3 new Breaking Points somewhere on the Exousia scale from the most grievous offense they can make (the lowest is usually Exousia 8) and will never give a Breaking Point <i>below</i> what phases the Announced, but beyond that there\u2019s no limit to how the Divine may impart this metaphysical weight. The Announced, invigorated by the charge, refills their entire Willpower pool, and the <i>qashmal</i> fills its Pyros pool. In the event of an Exousia Breaking Point, they may give up one of the Breaking Points taken up by the Burden in lieu of losing a dot and gaining a Chain, though they still suffer the appropriate Conditions. Shedding the last Burdened Breaking Point means immediately losing a dot of Exousia, but no Chain is gained as a result.<br /> <br /> The Announced may use the taking up of Burdens to suppress permanent Chains until such a time as they lose Exousia again, or Chains gained by very low or very high Exousia, but they may never possess more Burdens collectively than their dots in their Vocation\u2019s favored Maxim.</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-23T06:55:09-07:00","id":718858,"title":"","content":"And now, have some Merits exclusive to the Announced and their mortal companions, the <i>penitentes</i>. Feedback on these, and the above, would be greatly appreciated- even if it's criticism. Every bit of commentary helps.<br /> <br /> Also- a question for those of you reading along. Blessings are already equivalent to Affinities, the Celestial Form is extremely powerful, Edicts will be equivalent to Utterances. I was thinking of Dominions being a parallel to Promethean Transmutations, but would they be too much alongside all these other factors?<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>New Merits</b><br /> <br /> Here are several Merits that apply directly to the Announced and take advantage of their unique nature. These Merits apply exclusively to the Announced unless marked.<br /> <br /> <b>Heavenly Sleep (</b>*-*****)<br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Announced or <i>Penitente </i>Wits ***, Occult **<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The Announced receive visions from the Pleroma, which is how they are tasked with missions. Irin possessing this Merit are so in tune with the Pleroma that even after the initial vision through a trance, sleep, or meditation they can get a hint of what is to come. Once per game session, the player can have their character spend an hour (one scene) engaged in sleep, trance, or meditation to alter their state of mind. This works just as well for the Shedim, but substitute all Pleroma imagery with imagery from Sheol. The Storyteller will then secretly roll their Wits + Composure. These are the results:<ul><li><b>Dramatic Failure: </b>Terrifying nightmares, unveiled visions of the Pleroma. Endless fields of fire, <i>qashmallim</i> and other inhabitants of the heavens in all their mind-numbing glory. The dreams are in fact so disturbing, that upon waking the character loses a Willpower point. Any hints that can be teased from the dream may do more harm than good.</li> <li><b>Failure: </b>Meaningless visions of shifting auroras and angels passing by.</li> <li><b>Success:</b> One or more clue (per dot in this Merit) that may aid the character in understanding or progressing in the mission at hand, or something which may be useful to them in the future. They must be interpreted.</li> <li><b>Exceptional Success:</b> One or more clue (per dot in this Merit) that may aid the character in understanding or progressing in the mission at hand, or something which may be useful to them in the future. The Storyteller also provides an interpretation for the players to think over.</li> </ul><b>Drawbacks:</b> An open mind is one susceptible to temptations. For each dot in this Merit the Irin in question receives -1 on rolls to resist Persuasion attempts or rolls to resist falling to Temptation.<br /> <br /> <b>Alchemical Object (</b>*-*****<b>)</b><br /> <b>Effect:</b> When the Announced was Confirmed, the first magnificent flare of their Glory reached out and touched an object nearby, perhaps even the object that helped push them to Confirmation in the first place. Whatever the object is, the lash of the very fires of creation- like a solar flare or electrical arc -reached out and refined something nearby. This object is attuned to the Announced alone, and not only do others attempting to use it not benefit from equipment bonuses, they suffer the dots dedicated to this Merit as a penalty to using the object. A <i>Malakim</i> that was Confirmed in the middle of a powerful speech now possesses a manifesto that truly touches the souls of the masses, a <i>Ghogilim </i>that gazed into the dark places of the world until their soul snapped has a tome of maddening lore only they can comprehend. The Merit\u2019s cost depends on what benefits they want to give to the object.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Property</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Merit Cost</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Increased Durability</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1 dot per +2 Durability</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Increased Structure</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1 dot per +2 Structure</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Equipment Bonus</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1 dot per +1 Equipment bonus</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">9-Again</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3 dots</td> </tr> </table></div> <b>Drawbacks:</b> The Alchemical Object is tied to the Announced\u2019s very soul, if they ever lose the object they will need to make a Resolve + Composure roll every time they sleep. Failure means nightmares about the object, they gain no Willpower from rest, and for 12 hours upon waking suffer a -1 penalty to all actions (-2 on a dramatic failure). This ends when the object is recovered. If it is ever <i>destroyed</i> they suffer Lethal damage equal to their dots in the Merit.<br /> <br /> <b>Repute (*-***)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Announced<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Stories are passed among the Announced, telling tales of spectacular Missions, god-like feats, acts of great loyalty, passion, or defiance. Whatever the case, they are famous (or infamous) among others of their kind. When another Announced encounters a character with this Merit, roll their Intelligence + the other\u2019s Repute. If it is successful, the Announced is recognized, and they gain a bonus to Social rolls equal to their dots in Repute for all those who know of them. They may also use those that are aware of them as a one-time Ally equivalent to the Merit with a number of dots equal to the Repute level of the Announced. At one dot, they are moderately famous, maybe not recognizable on sight, but definitely through conversation. At two dots, they are recognizable on sight to others of their kind. At three dots, they are so famous that the very touch of their Glorious Radiance is enough to pronounce who they are. In Social Maneuvering, your dots of Repute add to your Impression once other Announced recognize you.<br /> <b>Drawbacks: </b>Obviously, due to their fame, they will be petitioned by other Announced to assist them- certainly they are capable. If they refuse too many times, they may lose Repute due to rumor of their selfishness being spread. If the Announced who identifies them is repulsed or disgusted by their reputation, apply their dots of Repute as a penalty to all Social rolls except for Intimidation.<br /> <br /> <b>Advanced Repute (*-***)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Repute *-***<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Urban legends of angelic visitations and divine intervention have a way of getting around in human society- especially the religious or those interested in the Divine Fire. Whenever they inflict Fear <i>Yara</i> on a mortal (which causes hallucinatory visions of the Celestial Form) the mortal makes a second roll of Intelligence + Advanced Repute to see if they recognize the appearance of the divinity before them. If they succeed, the Announced may apply Advanced Repute to a mortal as they would to another Announced, and instead of using them as a one-time Ally they may use them as a one-time Retainer. In Social Maneuvering, automatically open a number of Doors equal to the Announced\u2019s dots in Advanced Repute<br /> <b>Drawbacks: </b>Because the Announced\u2019s divine self is so famous (apply regional knowledge of it on the same level described under Fame) uncovering their Celestial Name is a snap. Add the Announced\u2019s dots of Repute <i>and </i>Advanced Repute to Intelligence + Academics or Intelligence + Occult rolls to find their Celestial Name. Additionally, every time you take advantage of Advanced Repute, the Storyteller should make a note. At the beginning of the next session, they roll a number of dice equal to the number of times you took advantage of Advanced Repute. A single success applies the Admirer Condition (Persistent) but any others apply the Rogue Cult Condition (Persistent), either way, showcasing your divinity will cause problems.<br /> <br /> <b>Unpalatable Aura (**)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Irin<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Pandorans instinctively dislike the Announced\u2019s Glorious radiance, and while it has the same effect on them- that is, awakening them from Dormancy -they\u2019d prefer to choose a different target from which to take their Pyros. If given a choice, they will likely attack other bearers of the Divine Fire first. If they have no choice, it can\u2019t prevent them from attacking, but it will reduce their Initiative by two.<br /> <br /> <b>Divine Intellect (***)</b><br /> <b>Effect: </b>The Divine Fire was particularly generous in their Confirmation, and their mind works with a speed and dexterity others envy. Even if they have no formal training, their raw talent and divinely refined mind can see them through. The Announced ignores all of the -3 unskilled penalties for trying to use Mental Skills the character does not possess. <i>Available at character generation only</i><br /> <b>Drawbacks: </b>When the Announced inflicts Awe <i>Yara</i>, they do so with more obvious intensity. The resulting degree of <i>Yara</i> worsens by one.<br /> <br /> <b>Perfected Form (**)</b><br /> <b>Effect: </b>The Divine Fire changes, purifies, <i>perfects</i>. Their physicality is something to be envied and celebrated in equal measure. They don\u2019t make any wrong steps, they move like a panther, and they don\u2019t walk- they stalk. The Announced ignores the -1 penalty for trying to use Physical Skills the character does not possess.<br /> <i>Available at character generation only</i><br /> <b>Drawbacks: </b>When the Announced inflicts Awe <i>Yara</i>, they do so with more obvious intensity. The resulting degree of <i>Yara</i> worsens by one.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Rarefied Grace (**)</b><br /> <b>Effect: </b>The character looks and sounds like an angel. Even if their appearance isn\u2019t all that exceptional, or their ability to empathize or maneuver socially particularly refined, it is very hard for them to say or do anything wrong- they get by on sheer force of personality. The Announced ignores the -1 penalty for trying to use Social Skills the character does not possess. <i>Available at character generation only</i><br /> <b>Drawbacks: </b>When the Announced inflicts Awe <i>Yara</i>, they do so with more obvious intensity. The resulting degree of <i>Yara</i> worsens by one.<br /> <br /> <b>Enigma (*-*****)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Announced, No Fame<br /> <b>Effect: </b>While the Announced tend to have incredibly dramatic, showy abilities and they take up the mantles of divine figures the world over, they remain secret, shadowy figures on the fringe of the supernatural world. Rumor and conjecture to creatures that consider their own existence a well kept secret. However, even though the Announced may garner attention, an unusual series of improbable coincidences lead investigators to frustrating dead ends. Evidently the Principle or the Prodigal Demiurge do not want their free willed agents found. Whenever someone takes an action to learn about, track, or locate the Announced they suffer their dots of Enigma as a penalty. This does not inhibit regular perception or stealth checks. It makes the Announced unremarkable, not invisible. If the Announced has Cultists, they use the Enigma rating as a <i>bonus</i> to track their own personal demigod without revealing them to the uninitiated. Of course, nothing is stopping unscrupulous Alchemists from kidnapping Cultists and using them as \u2018native trackers\u2019 as it were.<br /> <br /> <b>Icon (*-*****)</b><br /> <b>Effect: </b>The residual effects of something channeling the Divine Fire, and the artifacts of passion and obsession, the main purpose of Icons is to help attune the Announced to the Pleroma within their Magisterium, to guide them along their Affirmations to new Beacons, and as emergency reserves of Lux. The last is dangerous, however, as draining a point of Lux from an Icon permanently reduces its rating by one for every point drained. Normally, experience points need not be paid, but those spent on this Merit ensure the player begins the game with Icons in their possession. The remnants of a Beacon may become an Icon. The Storyteller is to provide backstories for Icons, but players are suggested to come up with some as well.<ul><li>See the Relic Merit below for dot rating considerations</li> </ul><br /> <b><i>Penitente</i></b><b> (***)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Mortal<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Some are able to look past the awe and horrors induced by the Divine Fire and see the Announced for what they really are. It is unknown what makes such a companion spontaneously arise, but for millennia they have served as the most loyal worshippers, allies, and friends of the Announced- as well as some of their most persistent enemies. They gain total immunity to <i>Yara</i>, but at a price- they feel an obsessive desire to be around the Announced. Whenever presented with an opportunity to accompany or learn about the Announced, the <i>penitente</i> must roll Wits + Composure to resist the urge to give in to their obsession. Success allows the <i>penitente </i>to act as they desire for the rest of the day. Apply a cumulative -1 penalty (up to -5) for each month they have gone without indulging this fascination. Such indulgence must dominate most of their waking hours for at least a week for the penalty to reset to zero. The obsession only flares up in the presence of the Announced or when they are presented with genuine information leading to the Announced. Finally, they gain a limited Unseen Sense, similar to the ability to read Glorious Radiance with a Glory of 1, which enables them to intuitively identify the Announced upon seeing them in person. They may not recognize what this means at first, but they will. Perhaps most important for the Announced is the fact that, due to their connection to the Divine Fire, <i>penitentes</i> can make Icons whenever they produce a form of art. When they work with passion they can potentially create a <i>fane</i>. The relationship between <i>penitente</i> and Announced is hardly simple, and will be discussed in full in Chapter 4.<br /> <br /> <b>Relic (*-*****)</b><br /> <b>Effect: </b>While possessing relics made from the bodies of dead Announced normally does not require experience points to be spent, those who spend dots in this Merit begin play with one or more relics or artifacts instead of needing to find one during play. The Storyteller will provide names, powers, and histories for the relics or artifacts, which could easily provide story hooks for future adventures<ul><li><b>* - </b>The Announced possesses a single one-dot relic</li> <li><b>**-</b> The Announced possesses a single two-dot relic or two one-dot relics</li> <li><b>***-</b> The Announced possesses a single three-dot relic or a two-dot relic and a one-dot relic.</li> <li><b>****-</b> The Announced possesses a three-dot relic and one-dot relic</li> <li><b>*****-</b> The Announced possesses a four-dot relic and a one-dot relic or a three-dot relic and a two-dot relic.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Sect Status (*-*****)</b><br /> <b>Effect: </b>While the above Repute Merits deal with the potential fame and recognition the Announced receive as divinities, both in their own communities and among mortals, this Merit solely represents their Status within social organizations exclusive to the Announced- the Sects. Sect Status is a way to gauge initiation into the mysteries of the Sect, and therefore serves as a cap on Sect Dominions, and a prerequisite for Sect Blessings and Edicts. Typically, due to the sheer rarity of the Announced, a given Sect will have only a single Sect Status 5 member in a particular city who serves as the leader of that Sect. In less stratified Sects like the Black Flame and Sefer Raziel, there may be a few more members of such rank as Status in those Sects is based more on seniority and learning than it is on political position. If they ever leave the Sect, they generally <i>keep</i> the Sect Dominion and any Sect Edicts learned, but lose the Sect Blessings. As usual, dots in Sect Status can act as modifiers in Social rolls with other Announced, positively with other members of your Sect or negatively if you encounter political rivals.<br /> <br /> <b>Fire Reader (*-*****)</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Announced or <i>Penitente</i>, Occult ***, Empathy **<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The radiance that cascades off of Glory and generates <i>Yara</i> carries quite a bit of information about the Announced it is bleeding off of- their power, their triumphs and their misdeeds, even the kind of god they are. Those skilled in understanding the subtleties of the Announced\u2019s Glory and <i>Yara</i> can find out that information and much more. By spending a point of Willpower and rolling Exousia (Integrity for <i>Penitentes</i>) + dots in Fire Reader, they can attempt to read the radiance of an Announced near enough for them to detect.<br /> <b>Dice Pool: </b>Exousia (Integrity) + Fire Reader<br /> <b>Action: </b>Instant<br /> <b>Roll Results:</b><ul><li><b>Dramatic Failure: </b>The Glory is too overwhelming, even for one touched by the Fire. They are knocked unconscious for a full minute.</li> <li><b>Failure: </b>They fail to read the Glorious Radiance. Each additional attempt suffers a cumulative -1 penalty for the rest of a 24-hour period.</li> <li><b>Success: </b>For each success they may determine one Trait- including Vocation, Sect, Purpose, Passion, preferred name, and Glory rating. They may spend one success to incur Judgment as though they were in the Anncounced\u2019s presence if they are one of the Announced.</li> <li><b>Exceptional Success: </b>Though they must spend the first four successes on one of the effects listed above, all those following may be spent on one of these effects. They may determine the Anncounced\u2019s Exousia, but only in general term- whether it is higher, lower, or equal to their own. They may determine any Conditions currently imposed on the Announced, and which Dominion they have unlocked the most Aspects of.</li> </ul><b>Suggested Modifiers: </b>Using this ability not in the presence of the Announced in question (-3), the target\u2019s Glory is greater than their own (-1/per dot), the target\u2019s Glory is less than their own (+1/per dot)<br /> <br /> <i>Devils in the Details</i>: Once again, the main assumption I\u2019m making in writing these Merits, like much of the rest of the book, is that players are going to default to the Azothic Irin. But for those who want to play the Shedim, all is not lost. The vast majority of these Merits still apply, but the flavor needs to be changed a bit. The Shedim usually have Repute that paints them as dark and terrible servants of the Principle, Advanced Repute usually depicts them as destroying angels and demons. People serve and aid them out of fear, not respect or worship. Alchemical Object is more or less unchanged, but the objects that are important and the reasoning behind why others are punished for using them are different. A maddening tome of occult lore only of use to the Shedim who holds it may actually impede the efforts of others to understand. The Icons and Relics of the Shedim typically have terrifying and tragic backstories. The Magisterium of a Shedim is enough to drive one mad- the acoustics send distorted whispers into your ears, your voice could echo endlessly or not at all; the constant void of sound or total reverberation could deprive you of one of your senses. With the Irin, there has to be at least a little sense of wonder and reverence. With the Shedim, don\u2019t hold back the horror.<br /> <br /> <b>Magisterium (Special)</b><br /> <b>Effect: </b>A Magisterium <i>shouldn\u2019t</i> be an Announced\u2019s home, but sadly more often than not it ends up being one. A Magisterium is meant to be a temple, a shrine, a place dedicated to the exploration of the relationship between the Announced and the Principle, a refuge within the Pleroma, a way to attune yourself to the Divine. An Announced without a Magisterium isn\u2019t homeless, they\u2019re godless; they have no way to reliably make contact with the Divine, no way to reliably harvest Lux. If they possess a Cult, to them it is a true temple; a place to connect with the Divine in a way most other mortals can barely comprehend. If they can\u2019t find the Announced, it\u2019s a convenient place to leave a message for them, though their enemies could always leave a calling card as well. Announced without a Magisterium lack all of these advantages, and must therefore be much more careful in when and where they choose to spend Lux and manage a Cult.<br /> <br /> Mechanically, the player can put up to ten Merit dots into Magisterium, dividing them between Acoustics, Pleromic Overlay, and Endowments. If you want to stretch your investment a bit (or like helping the Storyteller heap trouble on your head) you can take Drawbacks to reduce the Merit dots cost of Magisterium. Taking five dots in Drawbacks would enable for five dots to be dedicated to every advantage, making it a powerful Magisterium indeed. (The inherent Drawback to Memory Palace is to help facilitate a semblance of balance, and does not count as a discount on the Merit) Players <i>may</i> conflate their Magisterium with somewhere covered by the Safe Place Merit but this is not strictly necessary.<br /> <br /> <b>Acoustics (*-*****):</b> Perhaps better than anyone, the Announced understand the need to connect with the Principle. Until the Announced achieve Communion, using their Magisterium is the best (and only) way they have of communicating with It. Whether they actually manage to make the Principle <i>understand</i> or if the appeal merely fulfills the deal the Grigori made with Metatron and It is mollified by an uncomprehending attempt to intercede for It is unknown. But how does one communicate with an alien intelligence that has trouble comprehending separateness of persons? Energy, as a universal factor, is a constant. Music, song, mantras, whatever the Announced use to meditate on the Divine, they attempt to use the wavelength of the sound, and eventually the music within the Announced resonates with the music of the Principle- the Music of the Spheres.<br /> <br /> However, like any engine, the more you want out of it the larger it has to be. A one-dot Magisterium is little better than a broom closet, while a five-dot one is substantially larger and therefore more potent. If the Announced chooses to enhance this effect with Relics or Icons, the Magisterium could have strange effects on the surrounding area, enhancing <i>Yara</i> in strange ways.<ul><li><b>*:</b> Little more than a closet-sized shrine to meditate and pray, probably not much better than singing in the shower. Add one die to attempts to regain Lux.</li> <li><b>**:</b> Maybe about the size of a small office, it is likely something fairly similar to a home studio or a recording suite. Add two dice to attempts to regain Lux.</li> <li><b>***:</b> While it isn\u2019t much, it was designed with consideration taken for genuine performance or worship. In addition to being functional, it is also rather beautiful and well decorated. Perhaps prized possessions or works of art line the walls. Maybe something similar to a small church or a community theatre. Add three dice to attempts to regain Lux.</li> <li><b>****:</b> This place is somewhere a performer would be genuinely proud to perform. Maybe not the size of a concert hall or an Old World style church, but the place <i>definitely</i> has the acoustics for it. The connection to the Divine, or to the beauty of music or art in general, is evident in the d\u00e9cor; chances are the place is a little opulent or grandiose, but you can hardly deny that it\u2019s affecting. Add four dice to attempts to regain Lux.</li> <li><b>*****:</b> A true work of architectural art, this is a place people would <i>kill</i> to be able to sing or play in. A large-scale concert hall, a genuine cathedral, that sort of thing. They might not be <i>quite</i> that big, but they\u2019re close. Voices are hushed as channeled Lux and shaped <i>Yara</i> cause a palpable aura of true awe, and those that do raise their voices to sing would weep for the beauty of the music. Add five dice to attempts to regain Lux.</li> </ul><br /> <b> Pleromic Overlay (*-*****): </b>Ultimately, in many ways this is the true purpose of a Magisterium, to be a place where the mortal realm and the worlds touched by the Divine Fire can connect and mingle. However, the exact means in which this expresses itself is different from Magisterium to Magisterium. It could be that the sanctum of one Announced is more or less a tableau of their life, with a fridge stocked with their memories of the best things they\u2019ve ever eaten and drank. It could be another is so shrouded in the Divine Fire that it takes an effort of will for mere mortals to even enter the place. But whether it is a place that is untouchable due to the aura of sacred forbiddance that surrounds it, or a manor that twists and conforms to the will of its master, a Magisterium is always a place of beautiful madness- where the very stuff of heaven touches the earth.<ul><li><b>Memory Palace: </b>Crafted from the memories, ambitions, and desires of the Announced who is master of it as much as it is a building. By spending a point of Lux and rolling Wits + Memory Palace, the Announced may make any change they desire to the interior of the Magisterium, even if it normally would be physically impossible. This can\u2019t change the square footage of the Magisterium, merely how it is used. This could, theoretically, be used to remove any and all exits from the Magisterium, making it significantly more difficult to escape or break in. Depending on how stressful the situation has become, apply penalties as the Storyteller sees fit.<ul><li><b>Drawbacks: </b>Because of how deeply connected Magisterium and Announced become due to this particular alteration, should anything happen to the Magisterium it has dire consequences for the Announced. The destruction of any part of the Magisterium is grounds for the Amnesia Condition- regarding what probably pertains to what was destroyed, but ultimately it is up to the Storyteller.</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Sanctum Sanctorum: </b>The Magisterium should be a place the Announced can retreat to in order to be alone with their thoughts, and with the Principle, like the high priests of old. And for that to be true, it must be virtually impossible for others to approach. Every dot dedicated to Sanctum Sanctorum means that another 100 feet away from the Magisterium, those approaching begin to feel a profound yet sourceless distress bordering on outright terror. Those who take the hint and back off suffer no additional penalties. Those who try to approach must roll Resolve + Composure, <i>penitentes </i>are able to do so as a rote action, and those that succeed may approach by spending Willpower equal to the dots dedicated to Sanctum Sanctorum. Note that this works on <i>any</i> entity capable of using Willpower, not just humans. This particular defense for the Magisterium may be waved for those the Announced declares welcome.</li> </ul><br /> <b> Drawbacks: </b>These are issues or concerns associated with the Magisterium. In character they do little except cause potential headaches for the Announced (unless they can turn them to their advantage), but out of character each dot of Drawbacks allows for extra dots to be spent on the Magisterium Merit. Each Drawback may only be taken once, and each should serve as a Story Hook given appropriate circumstances.<ul><li><b>Famous (</b>minus <b>**): </b>For whatever reason, the Announced\u2019s Magisterium is noteworthy and well known to the general public. The acoustics and the feelings of genuine reverence and worship that permeate them are well recorded. Not only does this make it much easier for people to find you, but attempting to enter or leave without notice requires a successful Dexterity + Stealth roll. Attempting to enter the Pleroma or draw upon Lux without notice while the place is open is effectively impossible.</li> <li><b>Inhabited (</b>minus <b>***): </b>Something lives in the Magisterium, drawn to its power. Maybe it\u2019s an old ghost, or the new ghost of a <i>penitente</i>. Perhaps it\u2019s one of the <i>jinn</i>, or something far worse- like a Pandoran. Whatever the case, something lives there that sees the place as its own, and it won\u2019t be easily mollified. It is an Ephemeral Being, it is effectively a Rank 1 entity- with ten Attributes and three Numina to be chosen as the Storyteller sees fit. If it is a Pandoran, build it using the \u2018Minion\u2019 level template.</li> <li><b>Ravaged (</b>minus <b>*): </b>Due to some sort of defacement or spiritual defilement, the way the Magisterium attunes with the Pleroma is thrown off. Pick one Maxim. You cannot use anything using it as a prerequisite within the Magisterium. Ever.</li> <li><b>Stained History (</b>minus <b>*): </b>This can only be taken by one of the Announced who possesses at least one dot of the Cult Merit. Normally, the Announced\u2019s Magisterium is their Cult\u2019s holiest site, their true temple- they benefit from operating near it. Not this group- fear, shame, or remorse taint the site. A common Drawback associated with the Shedim.</li> <li><b>Under Surveillance (</b>minus <b>**): </b>A group of mortals has some deep vested interest in your Magisterium. The Storyteller has five dots to distribute between their Eminence and Ardor (Reach and Grasp). The player may have some suggestions as to who these people are and why they are interested in them, but ultimately the Storyteller must decide.</li> </ul><br /> <b> Endowments: </b>Sometimes, it\u2019s good to be a servant of the Divine. Occasionally, you catch a lucky break; the Principle smiles on you, and you can really enjoy life as a small god in a world full of mortal men. Each Endowment may only be taken once.<ul><li><b>Obscure (*):</b> Mortals have a blind spot for your Magisterium, most of them don\u2019t even know it exists; they must succeed a Wits + Occult roll to even notice it. (Unseen Sense or <i>Penitente</i> negates this) If the roll fails, they cannot notice it, and cannot roll again for another 24 hours. If the character is led there, they can see it, but if their guide abandons them, they are lost again. If you also possess the Famous Drawback, then the public parts of the Magisterium are known and celebrated, but the inner sanctum is virtually invisible. The Announced may travel to the Pleroma and meditate to gain Lux normally.</li> <li><b>Prime Location (*): </b>The Magisterium is located somewhere particularly desirable and notable. A Parisian Announced doesn\u2019t just have a Magisterium; they have a Magisterium within La Cathedral Saint-Chapelle.</li> <li><b>Food of Angels (***):</b> Somewhere in your Magisterium, strange food mysteriously coalesces, seemingly out of the light streaming through the windows. Whatever shape this takes (traditionally thin wafers tasting vaguely of milk and honey) the effect is distinct. For the Announced, eating this <i>manna</i> grants them a number of Willpower points equal to their Exousia. If this would exceed their normal limit, the excess points disappear at the end of the scene. For a mortal, it boosts their Resolve, Composure, and Stamina by one dot for a number of <i>days</i> equal to their Integrity and grants them the Rote Action quality to resist <i>Yara</i> and any powers fueled by the Divine Fire.</li> <li><b>Fane Resonance (****): </b>The Magisterium itself acts as a singularly magnificent <i>fane</i>. When taking this Endowment, pick a Maxim. When you meditate in your Magisterium, you automatically gain a number of points of Lux equal to your dots in that Maxim.</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-28T12:17:41-07:00","id":735463,"title":"","content":"So, after a lot of thinking (and a lot more editing) I have decided that giving the Announced Dominions on top of Edicts, Blessings, and the Celestial Form is just too much. When the final document goes up, all mention of Dominions will be missing; they're plenty powerful on their own.<br /> <br /> More feedback is always a good thing, even if it's criticism. Every bit helps guys, and a big thank-you to those of you who have been following along and commenting, you're a big help. And now, for the rules on Radiance and Burnout.<br /> <br /> <b>Divine Power</b><br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">In the Confirmation, Glory comes upon the Announced and the light of the Pleroma shines through them. This light, though dampened by mortal flesh and mundane life, cannot entirely disappear and confers certain abilities to the Announced- either the overt powers of Dominions and Edicts, the divine might of the Celestial Form, or other, innate abilities that arise as side effects of the Confirmation.<br /> <br /> <b>Glorious Radiance: </b>An aura of power that heralds the arrival of the Announced well before they actually get close to the perimeter of it, the Radiance that pours off the Glory of the Announced wakes Pandorans, inflicts <i>Yara</i>, calls to the minds of the <i>penitentes</i>, and informs other Announced of the approach of one of their fellows. Those Announced within the area of effect outlined in the chart below may roll Glory in order to sense whether there is a source of Divine Fire nearby, though they will need Fire Reader if they wish to determine anything more. They may spend Willpower on the roll. With Storyteller approval, this may also detect Promethean Azoth and vice versa. Whenever there is a sudden surge of Glory, such as when one of the Announced is called to a Mission, Glory is spent on a Edict or Supplication, or the Announced uses an Edict tier with the \u2018Epic\u2019 keyword, anyone within a radius equal to the Radiance of Glory 10 feels the surge of power and gain an immediate indication of which direction it is in. Be aware that this includes Pandorans seeking you out to devour.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Glory</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Radiance</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">10</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Entire city</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">9</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Entire city</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">8</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Entire city</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">7</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Entire city</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">6</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Half of city</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">City directional location or borough</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">City neighborhood</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Several city blocks</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">City block</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Same building</td> </tr> </table></div> <b>Long Lived: </b>The Great Edict of the Prodigal Demiurge said the days of the Announced shall number 500 years, and that much is generally true. Unless truly killed they live that long without exception- with or without Glory. The Announced age exceptionally slowly as well. Most are Confirmed somewhere between twenty and fifty, but the Divine Fire perfects and invigorates them to the prime of physical health and from then on they age roughly one year for every eight they have lived. Even then, they never really slow down, though towards the last fifty years or so of their lives they can begin to feel a little worn thin or \u2018burnt out\u2019.<br /> <br /> <b>Burnout: </b>When the Announced are called to a Mission they go from metaphysical lightweights, barely able to use the copious power and knowledge they have gathered, to some of the greatest powerhouses of the World of Darkness. Glory is unfathomable power; at its heights you can take on armies, level cities, play at being a true god. But it comes with strings, and those strings are following the Mission laid out for it. All Missions occur within a particular time frame, and while the Prodigal Demiurge isn\u2019t <i>always</i> cracking the whip, Burnout is Its way of keeping the Announced humble and reminding them that the clock is ticking down. Generally, a Mission is deemed \u2018finished\u2019 by the time the Announced hits Glory 5- from then on, the Announced is a free agent, and depending on their success can either be left to enjoy their power, or be scrambling for any scrap of occult might they can take hold of to solve a situation that has gone completely sideways.<br /> <br /> Certain powers and actions necessitate the spending of points of Glory, like Supplication, certain visages or parts of certain Edicts. If the Announced dies in the line of duty, they lose a number of points of Glory equal to (10-Exousia) in order to resurrect, meaning there\u2019s no coming back if you\u2019re off the clock. However, the most common way to lose Glory is through Burnout. As mentioned above, Missions are on a timeline, either one that is fairly quick, or one that is a bit more sedate. Whenever the time is up, however, roll your current Glory rating. Success means you lose a dot, failure means you manage to keep a hold on the power for a while longer. Spending a point of Willpower means <i>reducing</i> the roll by -3.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Glory</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>When you roll Burnout</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Time Added</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">10</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">After a scene (12 hours)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">One scene (12 hours)</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">9</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">After two scenes (A day)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">One scene (12 hours)</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">8</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">After three scenes (Two days)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Two scenes (A day)</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">7</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">After a Chapter (Five days)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Two scenes (A day)</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">6</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">After a Chapter (Ten days)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Three scenes (Two days)</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">After two Chapters (20 days)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Three scenes (Two days)</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">After three Chapters (40 days)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">One Chapter (Five days)</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">3</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">After a Story (60 days)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">One Chapter (Ten days)</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">After two Stories (100 days)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Two Chapters (20 Days)</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">After a Chronicle (160 days)</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">One Story (40 Days)</td> </tr> </table></div> Succeeding a Mission, or at least finishing it adequately, means being able to use this time scale normally, maybe hold onto your power for a while longer. Failing a Mission, in addition to a jarring loss of Exousia, means that every time a Burnout deadline pops up you succeed the roll, falling slowly but surely to helplessness. When you hit Glory \u201c0\u201d you treat Exousia as your Power Trait, and can only hold a number of points of Lux at once equal to your Exousia. It is important to note here that while Exousia ties you directly to the Principle, Glory and Burnout are controlled by the Prodigal Demiurge. While It normally wants what the Principle wants, occasionally the Announced do things that the Prodigal Demiurge may see as threatening the dialogue, whatever it looks like, between It and the Principle, or perhaps of supplanting It in the Principle\u2019s esteem. Such actions may trigger Burnout rolls early. Here are some examples:<ul><li>Allowing another Announced to commit Exousia Breaking Points</li> <li>Slowing the efforts of a Promethean or killing them</li> <li>Dramatic Failure on anything that uses Glory as a dice pool</li> <li>Increase Exousia by a dot</li> <li>Mocks or decries the Prodigal Demiurge</li> <li>Mar a Magisterium</li> <li>Stealing Glory from another Announced</li> <li>Verbally profess their highest love for a mortal, using their own name and their first language.</li> </ul>And that\u2019s it! Other than avoiding certain esoteric prohibitions or rebelling against a cosmic being with a grip on your soul anyways, the only thing that can really endanger the security of Glory is just being a fairly decent person. As mentioned under Exousia, the Announced do not need to be moral- they just have to do as they\u2019re told. In effect, they are <i>meant</i> to be powerless vessels of the Prodigal Demiurge\u2019s will in order to ingratiate it to the Principle, but the Divine Fire doesn\u2019t work like that. Conversely, there are also things you can do to earn a little more time from the Prodigal Demiurge- the sort of things It believes are the equivalent of roses and chocolate to the Principle. If you complete one of the actions below, you time extends. Say you come across a <i>Sublimatus</i>, and being a reasonable person, chop it up into many pieces. However long you have spent on this particular level of Glory, the timer resets back to the beginning, granting you more time.<ul><li>Destroy a powerful Pandoran or <i>Sublimatus</i></li> <li>Create a Fane or be the primary motivation behind the creation of one</li> <li>Exact horrifying revenge on those who steal the Divine Fire (Alchemists)</li> <li>Save the life of a fellow Announced</li> </ul>As you can see, the list of things you can do to give yourself a bit more time and appease the Powers That Be is shorter and significantly more difficult than the list of things that can get your power taken away. None of these are particularly moral reasons to be granted an extension on cosmic power, but the Announced are never all that moral to begin with. When you\u2019ve finally run out of Glory, you\u2019re left more or less powerless. You can only hold as much Lux as you have Exousia, Exousia serves as your Power Stat and calculates how swiftly you regenerate damage. Celestial Form is still accessible if you can pay the Lux for it, Edicts are off the table, and you treat your Maxims as though they only possess one dot.<br /> <br /> <i>Touching the Face of God</i><br /> <br /> What is it like to hold that much Divine Fire boiling under your skin? Generally speaking, it is exhilarating and excruciating. It feels like a furnace ignited in your soul- the world seems sharper, regular sensation fades to the background and all you can sense is Pyros; where it\u2019s strong, where it\u2019s weak, where it ebbs and flows. The Irin can feel unity, companionship, love, and other emotions analogous to Azoth with an intensity few can match. Pain, suffering, and discord- especially that of another -hits them like a truck and can cause almost unbearable pain. Basking in the power of a Fane is rapturous, almost addictive. You can hear the Music of the Spheres that is the voice of the Principle calling for you. As Glory drops, mortal sensations become more prominent; the color and the musicality of life seems to fade away.<br /> <br /> The Shedim, on the other hand, suffer an altogether more disheartening experience. At high Glory they can always see the cracks, the gaps, the places in the soul where passion and obsession turned dark and forced a rift open. Every piece of hypocrisy forces a gap open wider, every act of hatred and destruction creates gaping wounds in the world. False unity, false promises, false ideals; all these illusions fill them with a senseless and visceral disgust and rage. All they want to do is pump the cracks full of Flux-aligned Pyros and watch everything come down. To try and take Lux from a Fane is painful- they can hear the distant chime of the Principle but it\u2019s like hearing something from the bottom of a pool; they know it\u2019s not for them, and that makes them feel lonelier than ever. Basking in the power of a Waste is the only thing they can do to feel at peace. Then all the whispers, the rumble and cracking of unstable foundations all fade into the roaring emptiness of the Abyss. As Glory drops, color and beauty seem to fill the world again.<br /> <br /> <i>Draining Relics</i><br /> <br /> Sometimes, the Announced must do something that, for them, verges on blasphemy. They can take any relic or artifact, and so long as they desire so, may drain it of its Glory, taking back a dot of Glory for every dot the relic possesses. They must make a separate Exousia roll for each dot of a relic drained in this manner, but the end result is generally that they come out of it with greater metaphysical strength.<br /> <br /> <i>Stealing Glory</i><br /> <br /> It is possible to steal Glory but the process is brutal and disturbing. It is always an Exousia Breaking Point, and the target needs to be restrained, asleep, unconscious, held down by allies, whatever is necessary- point being they can\u2019t struggle to escape. The reaper rolls Strength + Occult contested by the victim\u2019s Stamina + Resolve. Every success steals a dot of Glory or, if they inflict Aggravated damage in excess of the target\u2019s Stamina, divide it between dots of Glory and points of Ichor. A dramatic failure on the part of the reaper inflicts a -2 penalty to all their actions for the rest of the scene.</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-29T08:34:07-07:00","id":738407,"title":"","content":"And now, more inherent template powers. Any feedback is good feedback- but thanks for reading along.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><b>Miraculous Healing: </b>The Divine Fire is more than the stuff of life; It is its author, its origin. Glory means it can be extraordinarily hard to kill one of the Announced, and depending on the situation, it just gets even more difficult. An Announced, by default, heals a number of points of Bashing damage equal to their Glory each turn. They may spend a point of Lux to heal Lethal damage, but this is obviously supernatural and inflicts Fear <i>Yara</i>. When the Announced heals Lethal damage, their injuries seem to light up from within, they are wreathed in spectral flame, and let off a smoke smelling heavily of incense. As an additional bonus, they cannot become sick through merely mortal means.<br /> <br /> When the Announced are in Celestial Form they no longer apply wound penalties, and cannot be knocked unconscious. So long as they manifest a single Regalia, they cannot become exhausted or stunned. Damage-based Tilts and Conditions heal with the turn after they are inflicted, unless they are caused by Aggravated damage, which the Announced normally heal like mere mortals, at which point the Tilt or Condition heals with the injury. Most importantly, however, is the fact that the Announced may spend a point of Lux to heal Aggravated damage equal to their Glory for a number of turns equal to their Exousia + Vocation Maxim. No mundane mortal weapon can destroy their body while they heal in this manner, and they can continue to fight past the point of death so long as they still have a few turns of healing left. They can do this as many times in a scene as they have dots in the higher of their Vocation or Sect Maxim.<br /> <br /> What does constitute as Aggravated damage for the Announced? Good question. Certain supernatural powers always inflict Aggravated damage, relic or artifact weapons deal Aggravated damage, as would certain Alchemical Objects used as weapons or an Alchemical Object used as an Improvised Weapon.<br /> <br /> <b>Resurrection: </b>Sometimes, it can be virtually impossible to keep a good god down. If the Announced dies in the line of duty, they lose a number of dots of Glory equal to (10-Exousia) and return from death in a period of time dependent on that trait. If they <i>aren\u2019t</i> \u2018on the clock\u2019 as it were, and currently have no Glory to shield their souls, then they sacrifice a dot of Exousia to come back and reduce their maximum Exousia by one. They may lose a dot of Willpower if they need to reconstruct their body from virtually nothing. Certain Regalia can make it easier to cheat death, but these are generally the rules. If the Announced wishes to return more quickly, then their body must be cared for by their allies and returned to their Magisterium, where they will resurrect in 30 minutes regardless of the chart below. They return from death with Lethal damage healed, and Aggravated damage equal to their Exousia removed.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>10-Exousia</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Time to Resurrection</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">9-6</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Days equal to Exousia</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5-1</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Hours equal to Exousia</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">0</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">Around 30 minutes</td> </tr> </table></div> <b>Rapture: </b>When the Announced first receive their Mission, they typically have no idea what is going on. Chances are, <i>someone</i> in the Announced community felt the burst of Glorious Radiance that comes with Confirmation, or had their eye on a potential candidate to begin with. However, until such a time as they are inducted into the society of the Announced, one newly Confirmed is utterly clueless in regards to their abilities, and can feel the niggling sensation that they are supposed to <i>do</i> something but have no idea what it is they\u2019re meant to do. Eventually, they\u2019ll start running into people that trigger that feeling until they get a flash of insight and their first hint at what the Mission really is. By that point in time, they\u2019re probably racing against the clock, but at least they\u2019ve gotten the hang of it, or had someone show them the ropes. Or, maybe the other Announced have left them to flounder and when they <i>do</i> fail the Mission things will have gotten a whole lot weirder for them.<br /> <br /> The sensation that helps the Announced find their way towards their Mission is called Rapture. They know the subtle touch of the Divine when they sense it- powerful scents, bursts of heat or cold, strange music, or vivid daydreams are all common responses to the sensation of Rapture. It is what enables them to recognize Fanes, Wastes, Icons, Relics, and other Announced on sight. It is both a passive and active ability that uses Glory as a dice pool. The Storyteller may roll it whenever they see fit, but the player can only choose to activate it in certain circumstances such as those below:<ul><li>Once per Chapter (Game session)</li> <li>Once after each Burnout roll, no matter the result</li> <li>Once after contact with a proxy (see below)</li> </ul>Announced with the Heavenly Sleep Merit gain extra hints about how to fulfill their Mission every time they sleep, but those who do not possess that ability must go off the first impression they had of what they must seek and do, and try to find a direct or indirect connection to the Mission in an effort to get closer. As time ticks down, the Announced gain more opportunities to use Rapture, and may spend Willpower to add dice to the roll. For every step closer to the subject of their Mission the proxy they\u2019ve found or the information they possess is, the easier it becomes to find out more about what it is they must do. The <i>Ishim</i> are especially adept at completing Missions in record time due to certain Blessings they have an affinity for. <i>Penitentes</i> occasionally serve as agents for Announced on Missions because they are treated as possessing a Glory of 1 for sensing (not projecting) Radiance, and therefore can also use Rapture provided they are given a description of what they must seek. It also helps that they may also possess the Heavenly Sleep Merit. Both Announced and <i>penitentes</i> occasionally go fishing for objects, people, or places touched by the Divine Fire they have encountered along their travels, electing to use Rapture actively to seek them out again.<br /> <br /> <i>Proxies</i><b>:</b> A proxy is a person or place with a connection between one and four steps away from what it is your Announced seeks. It could be your subject\u2019s brother, the caretaker of a church, that sort of thing. You generally don\u2019t know the <i>exact</i> relationship without further observation, but it is always a close one. A proxy then allows you to improve your own ability to use Rapture to look for a target- as you now possess the level of connection your proxy does, albeit one step down. Working from a proxy you can\u2019t go any farther back than Catalogued. Generally speaking, the Shedim tend to find proxies with powerful negative connections to their subject- reinforcing the desire to fulfill their Mission -while the Irin find proxies with powerful positive connections to their subject, ensuring more or less the same thing.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Dice Penalty</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Relationship</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">-2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><i>Owned or Nearby</i><b>:</b> The Announced once had the subject in their Magisterium, or the place or direct subject of the Missions lies within 5 yards per dot of Glory. <i>Penitentes</i> who have owned it and those with direct relationships to the subject also possess this rating.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">-4</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><i>Held or Seen</i>: The Announced or their proxy had seen the subject of their search in person once. They may still benefit from this connection long after the person, place, or thing is gone.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">-6</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><i>Described</i>: The Announced or their proxy has a detailed description of the person, place, or thing they\u2019re seeking.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">-8</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><i>Catalogued</i>: The Announced or their proxy is working off of a rough description or a vague recollection.</td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">-10</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><i>Rumored or Unknown</i>: The Announced is fishing. They have no idea what it is they\u2019re looking for.</td> </tr> </table></div> <br /> <b>Roll Results</b><ul><li><b>Dramatic Failure: </b>The Announced cannot distinguish the touches of Divine Fire leading to their quarry. If they were actively using Rapture the Storyteller rolls Glory again. If <i>that</i> roll fails, then the Announced does make a connection- just to a totally unrelated subject of interest to the Divine Fire that will likely be both strange and dangerous.</li> <li><b>Failure:</b> They can\u2019t find a trail of Pyros to hold onto.</li> <li><b>Success:</b> Through visions, omens, and intuition the Announced learn of the current broad area (city, country, etc) of their target, and a vision of someone two steps closer in relation to their target than they are. If their target is a proxy, they also discover their appearance, as well as the exact name and location of something they care about.</li> <li><b>Exceptional Success: </b>The Announced has a vision of their target and their surroundings out to a city block, as well as a hazy image of those who possess the closest relationship to it. They constantly know their distance to the target \u2018as the crow flies\u2019. If their target is a proxy, they learn the same information, as well as the identity of the person they possess the strongest emotional connection to.</li> </ul><br /> <i>Acting on Principle</i>: The Announced inherently know when they are in the presence of Relics, artifacts, or Icons. As mentioned above, they know instantly when they are in the presence of a source of Divine Fire. However, if they pick up a Relic or Icon, and roll Intelligence + Occult, they gain an inherent understanding of its power and potential dangers. If the Relic is <i>not</i> aligned with their Vocation or Sect Maxim, they only know the relative power, not the potential dangers.</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-02T11:58:52-08:00","id":749661,"title":"","content":"And now, the Announced version of Disquiet, <i>Yara</i>. Part Sybaris, part Disquiet, it takes notes from how Werewolf 2e handled Lunacy. As always, feedback would be much appreciated, especially with things that actually start to get into the crunch for big parts of the game.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><b><i>Yara</i></b><b>:</b> Everything above is the result of the relationship between the Announced and the Divine Fire; everything from Radiance to Rapture is a subset of the same phenomenon, the holy radiation boiling off of Glory. <i>Yara</i> is also derived from the power radiating off of the Divine Fire, but it focuses on how it affects mortals. Much like Promethean Disquiet, <i>Yara</i> is the conscious mind\u2019s response to the sheer stress of encountering the level of metaphysical might the Divine Fire possesses in such concentrated levels.<br /> <br /> Referred to variously as \u201cThe Awe\u201d or \u201cThe Fear\u201d depending on the circumstances, it is more properly translated as \u201cThe Outpouring\u201d. Disquiet is a subconscious response to the blasphemy that is stealing the Divine Fire to make a Promethean, but <i>Yara</i> is the other side of the coin. The basic response to stress is fight or flight, but that is oversimplifying it. Looking into this response in people with PTSD, it has been more accurately expanded to fight, flight, freeze, or fawn. Promethean Disquiet triggers the fight response, but <i>Yara</i> triggers the rest. Their response to the Divine in the Announced isn\u2019t to be repulsed by a blasphemy, but kneel or flee from something beyond their understanding. Generally speaking, <i>Yara</i> comes in two strengths- Awe and Fear. It is technically possible to forego the effects of <i>Yara</i>, but only by Marking the land- see Chapter 3.<br /> <br /> <b>Fear <i>Yara</i>: </b>When the Announced\u2019s Glory flares up, people respond with the abject terror you would expect from seeing a divinity unveiled. Shadows burn onto their surfaces; voices speak in babbling tongues, flames dance wildly and change color. The desire to bow down, to flee, combines with the maddening touch of the Divine Fire to create the Announced\u2019s Revelatory Form: it is ultimately based on their Celestial Form, but mixes with the target\u2019s own fears, anxieties, and ideas of the Divine. One Announced may look like a traditional seraph, but to one person they may look like a multi-limbed blind judge, wielding a flaming sword, while to another they may look like a mind-numbing tangle of lashing flames. Whatever the specifics, the point is the mortal reacts in abject terror.<br /> Fear <i>Yara</i> occurs when:<ul><li>The Announced undergoes resurrection</li> <li>The Announced <i>spends</i> a dot of Glory</li> <li>The Announced reveals their Celestial Form</li> <li>The Announced uses an Edict or Blessing that inflicts it</li> </ul>The impact of Fear <i>Yara</i> is damaging to a mortal\u2019s Integrity- a true experience with the divine isn\u2019t healthy for the solidity of the soul. They roll an Integrity Breaking Point with a penalty as appropriate for the severity of the supernatural occurrence. If the character succeeds the Breaking Point, they gain the Guilty, Shaken, or Spooked Condition. They act with a -2 penalty for the rest of the scene as the metaphysical pressure of the Divine Fire hammers at the back of their mind. Afterwards, they will rationalize away what they\u2019ve seen and will be loath to speak of it- requiring incredible leverage to even mention the occurrence.<br /> <br /> With an Exceptional Success, they gain no Condition and do not suffer any penalty. The mortal regains all Willpower as a way to deal with the divine monstrosity before them and will remember the scene with total clarity.<br /> <br /> If the mortal fails, they lose a dot of Integrity and gain a <i>Yara</i> Condition- Delusion, Uneasy, or Awestruck. At a Dramatic Failure, they <i>may</i> become a <i>penitente</i> and gain the Madness, Fugue, or Disturbed Condition.<br /> <br /> Working together in a group can help mortal witnesses comprehend what they\u2019ve seen. If two people are focused on the same goal when they experience the Breaking Point, they each gain a +1 to the roll. When a small group (five or less) works together they each gain a +2 to the roll. Large groups (ten or fewer) gain a +3 and any larger mobs gain a +4 to their Breaking Point. It is not advised for the Announced to face a mob except at the height of their power or if the mob is already under the influence of Awe <i>Yara</i>. Any Conditions inflicted persist for a number of days equal to the Glory of the Announced when they caused Fear <i>Yara</i> and then fade without granting a Beat.<br /> <br /> <i>Clash of Wills</i><br /> <br /> Supernatural creatures are immune to Fear <i>Yara</i>, but <i>not</i> to Awe <i>Yara</i>. There <i>is</i> an exception, however. If the Announced knows they are the target of a supernatural power that <i>is not </i>a physical assault, they may spend a point of Lux and unleash Fear <i>Yara</i> in a Clash of Wills and rolls Presence + Glory. Success means the targeting magic is totally obliterated and has no effect. They may only do this once per scene, but may resist other powers as written, including using Clash of Wills. The limit is only in regards to active use of the ability.</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-05T19:50:32-08:00","id":757773,"title":"","content":"Here's some more details on <i>Yara </i>for you. Awe <i>Yara</i> this time, the passive effect more like Unease Sybaris and regular Disquiet. As always, feedback would be much appreciated, especially with things that actually start to get into the crunch for big parts of the game. I eagerly await the release of Promethean 2e, if only because it means I'll have more goodies to work with and another overhaul to do.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><b>Awe <i>Yara</i>: </b>Over time and with proximity, the Glory of the Announced and the touch of the Divine Fire seeps into the people around them. They become affected by the Radiance of the Announced and suffer <i>Yara</i> in the same way mortals suffer Disquiet when they spend too much time around Prometheans. Unlike Disquiet, however, mortals don\u2019t <i>resist </i>Awe <i>Yara</i> so much as they <i>withstand</i> it. Either it affects them, or it doesn\u2019t. In order to calculate the degree of Awe the mortal experiences, subtract the Announced\u2019s (Glory-Exousia) from the mortal\u2019s Resolve + Composure. Each degree of Awe inflicts a specific Condition that persists in a cycle. Certain Blessings, Edicts, or Regalia can worsen Awe by a few degrees. However, whatever the specifics, Awe reflects the crawling spread of mortal awareness of the Divine. Where multiple Announced tread, or where a mortal encounters multiple Announced, use the <i>highest</i> Glory and the <i>lowest</i> Exousia between them to calculate Awe. Awe is inflicted under these conditions:<ul><li>Direct contact with the Announced</li> <li>Entering a Magisterium</li> <li>Touching a relic or artifact</li> <li>Spending 24 hours within 1 mile per dot of Glory of a Magisterium</li> </ul>Awe <i>Yara</i> persists as long as the situation that inflicts it continues to be problematic or for one week per dot of Glory the Announced possessed when they first inflicted it. One of the Announced may be dealing with aftereffects of the heights of their power <i>long</i> after their might has drained away.<ul><li><b>Awe 1 or Higher:</b> The mortal gains the Awestruck (Persistent) Condition</li> <li><b>Awe 0 to -2: </b>The mortal gains the Uneasy Condition</li> <li><b>Awe -3 to -5:</b> The mortal gains the Disturbed Condition</li> <li><b>Awe -6 or Lower:</b> The mortal gains the Zealous Condition</li> </ul>Modify this degree of Awe in steps up (worse) or down (better) based on some of the following cumulative circumstances. Use the total list of modifier to calculate the final degree of Awe, by numbers and not by categories. The Storyteller may apply more modifiers as they see fit. If the person affected by Awe <i>Yara </i>encounters the Announced again, apply all cumulative modifiers as appropriate to worsen their condition.<ul><li>Has experienced Fear <i>Yara</i> (from any Announced): one step up</li> <li>Unseen Sense Merit geared towards angels, magic, Pyros, etc: one step up</li> <li>The Announced is manifesting a Regalia: one step up</li> <li>The Announced has channeled their Purpose/Passion for willpower: one step up</li> <li>Has not come into direct contact with the Announced before: one step down</li> <li>Has not come into contact with relics, icons, artifacts, Magisteria, or cults associated with this Announced before: one step down</li> </ul><br /> <i>Shaping <b>Yara</b></i><b>:</b> One Sect in particular, the Black Flame, is all about understanding the passion and madness of the Divine Fire- their Blessings and Edicts are crafted in such a way that they can shape, twist, turn back, and inflict <i>Yara</i> in varying degrees and potencies; maybe even turn it into a weapon. In much the same way every Promethean has an opinion about Disquiet, every Announced has their view of <i>Yara</i>. Some welcome being followed around by a mob of crazed worshippers, while others would rather keep a low profile. However, one thing they all know for sure is that they can manipulate it to their own ends. The acoustics of the Magisteria of the Announced allow them to access and connect with the Pleroma. The Divine Fire cannot be controlled or reliably harnessed, but it <i>may</i> be coaxed into the world through reliable and pleasing channels such as the shifting grid of Pyros surrounding a Magisterium that most Announced call the Flower of Life or Metatron\u2019s Cube. A relic or artifact makes an excellent centering piece for this pattern of Glory, and Icons work to enhance and shape the Flower of Life and its purpose.<br /> <br /> The Announced may only fetter a number of Icons equal to the dots of the central relic + the higher of their Exousia or Sect Status. To do so, they must share the same Passion that defines the Icon and expend a Willpower point while placing the Icon in the appropriate location within the Magisterium. In order to determine the strength of the effect, add up the combined ratings of all fettered Icons (possible range is 0-75) in order to determine the strength of the Flower of Life. Every fettered Icon rating dot is an additional die to add to the Magisterium\u2019s Acoustics for the purpose of gathering Lux. More importantly, the Announced may turn the Flower of Life to manipulate <i>Yara</i> or perform any number of potential effects, from doubling all Burnout timelines within a city or making the Magisterium absorb Glory to completely subduing Awe <i>Yara</i> within a certain area or transforming Acoustics into an effective Glory rating that inflicts Awe <i>Yara</i> all on its own.<br /> <br /> Most effects have a target number of successes between 5 and 15, with lower end effects being equivalent to extremely long-range or persistent Tier 1 Edicts, and the higher end effects comparing to Tier 3 Edicts. When attempting to manipulate the Flower of Life, roll Exousia + Occult with each roll taking an hour of meditation and connection to the Magisterium.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> And now, the Announced version of Lashing Out, Judgment. I took the idea behind the Measure in Promethean and gave it some angel-themed mechanics.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><b>Judgment: </b>When the Announced first meet, their Radiance meets and mixes, causing them to immediately become aware of the nature of the other. They know that they have met another of divinity, and they want to know where they stand. This will influence first impressions among the Choir. When one Announced meets another roll their Glory + an appropriate Power trait. A seductive or charismatic Prince might use Presence while the impressively muscled Warden uses Strength. Whatever the case, they must also choose the image they want to project, which influences the Condition they can inflict if they succeed in overwhelming the other Announced.<ul><li><b>Avenging Angel:</b> This is the Divine Fire as an engine of passion, of action, of human desperation. They fulfill their goals, they punish the unworthy, and they cast down those who have done wrong. If successful, the other Announced feels petty and small, perhaps selfish and guilty, in the face of this paragon. It imposes the Chastened Condition</li> <li><b>The Almighty: </b>This is the Divine Fire as limitless power. There is nothing that, given proper circumstances, it cannot do. They are a dynamo unbound, a walking inferno of mystic might that cannot be equaled. If successful, the other Announced feels incredibly insignificant and even fearful in the face of such an overwhelming occult force; this is the closest a god will normally get to worshipping something else. It imposes the Cowed Condition</li> <li><b>The Messenger:</b> This is the Divine Fire as a force in service to a higher power. The strange and occasionally esoteric \u2018plans\u2019 of the Principle can be deeply vexing, but subservience to a higher power- especially one that brings people together and supports unity -is calming and reassuring. If successful, the other Announced feels empty and arrogant, jealous and envious of the peace their better possesses. It imposes the Guilty Condition. The Shedim don\u2019t impose this version of Judgment normally, but instead emphasize the hypocrisy and damage the Announced has experienced and been the cause of. The end result is the same, but it is a revelation of wrongdoing rather than a presentation of purity in principle.</li> </ul>If one Announced is successful, they gain a general sense of the power of the Announced before them (a combination of both Glory and Maxims) as well as a vision of the last time their fellow divinity fell to Temptation.</div></div> </div> </div><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-06T07:32:17-08:00","id":758636,"title":"","content":"Alright, so, I'm starting to become dissatisfied with the way this is going so far, and I have realized that I need some help. Those of you who have been following along, I am asking for your assistance. Anyone who has ideas, concepts, things they want to see done, send me a PM. Hopefully collaboration will give a more cohesive piece. It will almost certainly change again once Promethean 2e comes out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-06T08:24:07-08:00","id":758682,"title":"","content":"Well, I can tell you I am engrossed by your idea of angels, and think it's turning out well so far. I like the angelic aspects that cover the 'types' of angel.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><u><b>My Homebrew</b></u><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1PLu_eTg03h4ogiTSGSkzEB7T_1YpB9_VkhHmgZN41-I/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Star Wars d10</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">T</a><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1rIXZorT7CR5SREkdT3m0zbB3_gwf1484-gUVlG1JnhQ/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">he Fallen for Demon: the Descent</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1-TDr4NF_5cUn-In2vjI9P5LTKqsu9B4-OMxbnbaTgT8/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">Requiem for a Masquerade: VtR 2e Cainite Conversion</a> (<a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HpKi5giBNsQrZRLIRRjaCe7sHVm1Q13ravVYlE45Dbw/edit\" target=\"_blank\">Vampira Roma</a>)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1601-dreaminggod","name":"Dreaminggod","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"745"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-06T09:04:58-08:00","id":758839,"title":"","content":"Thank-you very much. Any comments or feedback on particularly standout mechanics or Merits? I'm trying to keep them nice and balanced. Rereading some of the stuff, I'm starting to get a bit <i>too </i>much of a Mummy vibe. When Promethean 2e comes out I'll probably change the Sects to be philosophical groups that pursue their Purpose and Missions in a way similar to the way Prometheans pursue Refinements. Athanors will probably replace relics/artifacts, Fanes will likely be unchanged. <i>Yara </i>will almost certainly change in the face of the new version of Disquiet, and I'll have to wait and see what Transmutations are like before seeing what I'll do with Dominions- which at the moment have been totally replaced by Edicts, Blessings, and Regalia.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-23T12:05:59-08:00","id":772441,"title":"","content":"It's interesting to see how you've remolded the Irin since Watchful, Arc. I'm liking the concept behind the Maxims, and the Shedim are gloriously hellish as always. I'll keep an eye on things and let you know what I'm thinking,","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6393-chuckles","name":"Chuckles","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6393_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-23T16:24:20-08:00","id":772609,"title":"","content":"Thank-you. It's been a while, but I've mostly been puttering around with some different concepts and theorizing on how things may change once Promethean 2e comes out. In the meantime, have some information about Blessings.<br /> <br /> <b>Blessings</b><br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">The Announced emerge from the Confirmation with preternatural competence, and unfathomably good luck- this is no accident, as the touch of the Divine Fire imbues them with the tools and talents they need to fulfill their purpose. Unlike most supernatural powers, they do not require training, cannot be taught, and aren\u2019t really learned in a conventional sense. Instead, they manifest immediately when they are purchased. The Announced do not know they have a new Blessing until it activates for the first time, at which point they intuit their new capabilities. Most Announced are as surprised by their Blessings as the rest of the world is.<br /> <br /> Most Blessings have the appropriate prerequisites for use listed in their description, and while it is possible for an Announced to possess a Blessing they <i>don\u2019t</i> have all the prerequisites for, but they remain dormant until the prerequisites are fulfilled. The type of prerequisite determines what sort of Blessing you have.<ul><li><b>Soul Blessings: </b>Using the Maxims as prerequisites, the higher the prerequisite, the stronger the Blessing. Typically, Soul Blessings are subtle with no overt magic, and can often be mistaken for exceptional luck or skill. Those that are more overt typically work only when they aren\u2019t seen. The Announced may only possess a number of Soul Blessings with a particular Maxim as a prerequisite as they have dots in that Maxim.</li> <li><b>Sect Blessings:</b> These use the Sect Status Merit as a prerequisite, and draw upon the metaphysical connection between Announced and Sect. Each Sect has a specific Blessing that manifests for free upon joining the Sect, and the Announced may possess a maximum number of Sect Blessings equal to their Sect Status +1 though rumor has it that certain leaders among the Sects have ways to circumvent the limit. If they lose status, they lose the ability to use the Blessing.</li> <li><b>Blasphemies:</b> Tied to the Dread Powers of the Pandorans and the horrifying corruption and decay inherent to Flux, the Shedim may purchase these sick parodies of Blessings naturally, but the Irin can only manifest them after coming into contact with one in action. These powers are always dramatic, vulgar, and horrifying- magnifying the inherent ineffable terror of the Announced. A character may possess a number of Blasphemies equal to 10-Exousia with a minimum of 2 for the Shedim.</li> <li><b>Miscellaneous Blessings: </b>Using various ratings like dots in Magisterium, Cult, Repute, or Exousia as prerequisite, Miscellaneous Blessings can range from the incredibly subtle to the overt and wondrous. The character may manifest these Blessings <i>in place of</i> Soul or Sect Blessings, not <i>in addition to</i>.</li> </ul><i>Liquidation</i>: Whenever the Announced finishes a Mission, either succeeding or failing, they may \u2018liquidate\u2019 one or more Blessings in order to use the experiences that went into them to purchase new ones, either at Storyteller discretion or your own, to represent the mercurial and ever-changing nature of the Principle. Some never liquidate their Blessings and others; when they lose the appropriate prerequisite, liquidate them in order to purchase other things.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Using Blessings</i>: Most Blessings have a persistent effect that is constantly supporting them, and enables them to go through life untroubled by the problems that afflict mere mortal men. Others list effects that are powered by the expenditure of Willpower or Lux- generally, use the abilities as written.<br /> <br /> <u>Sect Blessings</u><br /> <br /> <b>Eye for the Sacred</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Sect Status (Any) *+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Announced of any Sect may purchase this Blessing, as it is technically five different versions of itself with identical powers linking each Sect with relics and artifacts of the appropriate Maxims<ul><li>The Announced applies the 9-again rule on all Rapture rolls</li> <li>The Announced may use the active version of Rapture in unison with other members of their Sect. The roll uses the highest Glory amongst the group, adding +2 for all additional participants beyond the first. They apply the best target number and \u201cX-again\u201d abilities they possess amongst themselves, including the primary ability of this Blessing.</li> <li>While touching a relic or artifact of the appropriate Maxim, the Announced gains an automatic success for the purposes of tracking proxies who have held it before. In this way, they can backtrack the means by which it came into their possession.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Consecrated Font</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Sect Status (Any) *+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Announced of any Sect may purchase this Blessing, as it is technically five different versions of itself with identical powers linking each Sect with relics and artifacts of the appropriate Maxims<ul><li>The Announced gains a pool of (Sect Status) points they may use in place of Lux to activate relics or artifacts of the appropriate Maxim, or as Willpower points to subdue their individual Flux-based taints. They cannot use these points for any other reason. This pool automatically refills itself by one point every sunrise, using the nimbus of Radiance that interacts between the Announced and their Sect</li> <li>The Announced applies the 9-again rule to all rolls to uncover or study a relic or artifact of the appropriate Maxim, whether that means uncovering information in a library or interrogating witnesses. This <i>does not</i> assist Rapture rolls or other magical means of searching them out.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Silenced Edict Vessel</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Sect Status (Any) *+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Announced of any Sect may purchase this Blessing, as it is technically five different versions of itself with identical powers linking each Sect with relics and artifacts with the appropriate Maxims<ul><li>By touching an Icon that shares the same Passion as the Announced, and shares thematic similarities with the Sect Maxim, they may contain a tier of an Edict with a Maxim requirement equal to or less than their Sect Status. Additionally, the object the power is being sealed in must be an Icon with a dot rating equal to the Maxim dots needed to unleash the tier. The Announced pays all appropriate Lux in order to do so. If the object is <i>not</i> an Icon, then it must be an object with a Resources rating equivalent to the Maxim dots, and they must spend a point of Willpower as well as the required Lux.</li> <li>They may only empower a single vessel for each Maxim, meaning they may only have five vessels prepared at once, unless they are made in the Announced\u2019s Magisterium, at which point they may prepare one per dot of Sect Status <i>before</i> tying up Maxims. Vessels prepared in the Magisterium lose all power if they are removed. Those attempting to use a vessel must spend a Willpower point to unleash the Edict tier, and use the dice pools of the Announced at the time they prepared the vessel. Even a mortal may use such a vessel, as payment in Lux and the appropriate dice pools have already been invested.</li> <li>Once an Edict tier in a vessel is expended, the object may only be \u2018recharged\u2019. Azothic memory prevents anything other than the tier enchanted within the vessel originally, and cannot be used to contain another Edict tier.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Loyal Servant\u2019s Repute</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Sect Status (Any) **+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>Announced of any Sect may purchase this Blessing, as it is technically five different versions of itself with identical powers linking each Sect with relics and artifacts with the appropriate Maxims<ul><li>The Announced gains the 9-again rule regarding rolls that directly carry out the duties of business of the Sect, or to convince others to assist with such endeavors. By spending a Willpower point of a valid roll, they gain the 8-again quality. This does allow them to sense when their actions <i>don\u2019t</i> line up with their Sect\u2019s goals, indicating their orders are corrupt and invalid, though it doesn\u2019t tell them the how or why of it.</li> <li>The Announced adds +2 dice to their Social interactions with other Announced of lower Sect Status then themselves. This is in addition to the bonus already provided with the Sect Status Merit.</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-26T12:12:39-08:00","id":774952,"title":"","content":"And here's the Sect Blessings associated with the Host. I know most of these are more or less reskinned Mummy Affinities, but until Promethean 2e comes out, I'm working with the best inspiration I've got. Let me know if everything is still too much in the Mummy aesthetic or isn't differentiated enough<br /> <br /> <b>Host Sect Blessings</b><br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><b>Faithful Soul</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Sect Status (Host) *+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The Announced are all beings connected to the Divine, but the simple and powerful belief those of the Host espouse grants them all the power they need to persevere in the face of adversity and triumph.<ul><li>Add a +2 bonus to each of these Skills: Medicine, Athletics, and Expression. If a Willpower point is spent, they may use their Vocation Maxim instead as a bonus for a single roll.</li> <li>The Announced are naturally fearless, but those of the Host are especially so. Any attempts to cause fear in the Announced immediately fail.</li> <li>Add a +1 bonus to their Supernatural Power Trait for the purpose of resisting hostile powers; there is no cost to this effect. If they are resisting powers that would affect their thoughts or emotions, they may spend a Willpower point to use the higher of their Exousia or Vocation Maxim instead of their Glory.</li> </ul><br /> <b>True <i>Angelos</i></b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Sect Status (Host) **+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The Host would like to see themselves as the true powers of the Announced, the mediators and judges of their peoples, truly lords of the Celestial Hosts. Technically, this is four separate Blessings, with a unique power for each Sect, and thus can be taken multiple times, but that is ill advised except for those deeply invested in diplomacy and politics among the Announced. Taking an hour on an Intelligence + Crafts roll, the Chorister makes a small artistic gift to give to their companion. They can maintain a number of these amulets equal to their Sect Status, meaning a Chorister with all four versions of this Blessing can have up to 20 amulets. So long as the amulet is worn by an Announced of the appropriate Sect, whenever they are empowered for a Mission, the Chorister is as well. Additionally, the amulets grant the Chorister additional power over their bearers, though such abilities are only suspected at this time.<ul><li><b>Defy the False God: </b>The Chorister adds a +2 bonus to their Social rolls against any Announced from the Children of the White Temple. The Igigi suffers a -3 penalty for their Social rolls against the Chorister.</li> <li><b>Cutting the Reed: </b>The Chorister may choose to take up the injuries of their Philosopher companion, or may let the Sefer Raziel scribe take the blow for them. If the Chorister or the Philosopher suffers a Lethal or Aggravated wound, the Chorister may choose to transfer the injury to the other person in the connection.</li> <li><b>Power over the Unclean: </b>The Chorister benefits from a +2 bonus to their Social rolls against one or more Daemons who invite them into their conversation- one of which must bear the amulet. They may also reflexively spend a Willpower point to silence any Daemon in their line of sight, so long as the bearer of the amulet is still present. The player of the Daemon may spend a Willpower point to speak again and prevent being silenced again. Destroying the amulet doesn\u2019t prematurely end silencing.</li> <li><b>Shepherd the Pious: </b>Should the Chorister and one or more Magi associates- one of whom must bear the amulet -enter conflict together, whether physically or socially and as allies or enemies, they have three options. Firstly, they may allow everything to proceed normally. Secondly, they may levy a -3 penalty to any Magus in their line of sight either to Initiative or to Social rolls in order to give themselves a +3 bonus to either of those two traits. Thirdly, they may apply a -3 penalty to their own Initiative or Social rolls in order to grant a +3 bonus to an ally. They may do this to as many targets as are in their line of sight so long as the bearer of their amulet is still present and alive, and so long as they or their allies still have dice to give up.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Crusader</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Sect Status (Host) ***+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>An immensely popular Blessing among the devotees of St. Michael and the <i>Cherubim </i>alike, enabling them to protect and more effectively command those who fight alongside them and sit under their protection.<ul><li>By paying a point of Willpower as a Reflexive action, and touching a target the Chorister brands them with a symbol unique to the Announced who did so (typically a sigil representing their Name) that is visible to anyone who can see or otherwise sense auras. They may need to make a successful Dexterity + Brawl roll in a stressful or combat-oriented situation. Thereafter, when the target is in a situation that causes physical harm, the Announced that placed it may roll Wits + Composure to gain a clearer understanding of the target\u2019s condition, location, and current situation. They may brand as many targets as they please. One target may have multiple brands. The Chorister who placed it may seek their charge using Rapture at a -4 penalty.</li> <li>By spending a point of Willpower and touching a target as a reflexive action, the Chorister may grant their companion some protection for the next 24-hours. By rolling Stamina + Composure, the Announced applies successes as temporary Armor points up to a maximum of the higher of their Stamina or Sect Status. These Armor points stack with worn Armor, but not with other magical Armors that directly affect the body.</li> <li>Whenever the Chorister benefits from a Teamwork action as the primary actor, and forcefully commands their subordinates- as though they were some kind of military officer -their efforts are enhanced. They gain a +1 bonus to their action per companion even if their subordinates roll no successes on the teamwork action; their command of others for a righteous cause empowers them.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Ever-Pertinent Authority</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Sect Status (Host) ****+<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The truly influential members of the Host can bring the authority of their divine office to bear among mortals, as well as making their presence in the lives of their charges well and truly felt.<ul><li>If the Chorister presents other characters with an inanimate object as though it were a badge of office, government or private identification document, or other such mark of identity/authority, the player may spend a Willpower point to initiate a contested (Manipulation + Subterfuge + Sect Status) roll. Use the highest Resolve + Composure of observers present. Announced and <i>penitentes</i> are immune to this trick. Using something marked with their Celestial Name and/or Title confers the 8-again quality to the roll. Repute does not apply, but Advanced Repute does. If the Announced is successful, all witnesses perceive the object as what they say it is and treats the Chorister as though they are who their \u201cidentification\u201d says they are. Obvious evidence they are not who they claim to be causes the magic to fail, so additional props may be necessary. Implausible disguises are successful, impossible ones collapse under disbelief.</li> <li>If the Chorister has branded someone according to the rules in Crusader (see above) they may pay an additional point of Willpower when administering the brand. From then on, they inflict Awe <i>Yara</i> as though they were the Announced, and may use the degree of Awe as a bonus on a Social roll that would benefit from people wanting to avoid displeasing the branded character\u2019s patron. Awe 1 or higher confers no bonus besides that of the Awestruck Condition, and worsening Awe targets the Announced, not the branded. Use the highest Glory and the lowest Exousia for multiple empowered brands. The branded character also gives off Radiance as though they had Glory 1. While this may empower a branded <i>penitente</i>, it also makes them easier for other Announced, <i>penitentes</i>, and Pandorans to track.</li> </ul><br /> <b>Lord Of Hosts</b><br /> <b>Prerequisite: </b>Sect Status (Host) *****<br /> <b>Effect: </b>The local Archangel is high priest, general, and king to the Choir. The Host moves on their word, and those among the other Sects would do well to bow to them- serving a true God-appointed king comes with benefits.<ul><li>The Archangel synergizes the magic of up to four other Announced with Sect Status 5. So long as they are within a mile of the Chorister, and consent to have their magic merge with the character\u2019s, then the Archangel becomes the center point for all effects that use a mile radius. Additionally, this bubble of synchronized magic extends up to a number of miles equal to the number of Sect Status 5 Announced, expanding all of their reach. If consent is ever revoked, the relevant magic ceases and the diameter shrinks.</li> <li>By spending a point of Willpower, the Archangel creates a ward that protects an area up to their Sect Status in square miles for a number of days equal to their Exousia. Anyone trying to cross the border must bear a brand or have a Presence score greater than the Archangel\u2019s Sect Status.</li> </ul></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-30T04:30:55-08:00","id":796773,"title":"","content":"Would I be correct in saying that Edicts are similar to Utterances from Mummy? If so, I have a few ideas for them. They're not the most original, but I think they fit with the theme:<br /> <br /> Fire from the Skies: Sodom-and-Gamorrah-esque rain of fire at Tier 3. Tier 1, immunity to fire? <br /> <br /> Angel of Death: Tier 1, can see, speak to and interact physically with Twilight beings. Tier 2, can enter Twilight and become incorporeal. Tier 3 can kill with a touch.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/forum/core/<img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=423\" data-tempid=\"temp_423_1523360155059_212\" />\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10397-ajf115","name":"ajf115","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10397_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2416"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-30T20:13:53-08:00","id":797161,"title":"","content":"Edicts are indeed analogous to Utterances. I was thinking of a Sodom and Gomorrah type power, but Tier 3 wouldn't be anything so simple as just mundane flames, but a full-blown Firestorm.<br /> <br /> I've been thinking the splat over some more, and while a lot of my changes are waiting on Promethean 2e, I was pondering maybe changing out Lux for Humors, and tying Maxims to them. I'm not really in favour of using Humors as a measurable fuel Stat, but I'll play around with it, and see how it works. Perhaps instead of relics, they'll be replaced entirely by 2e Athanors, with Seba-like gifts from the Principle standing in as things mostly encountered by the Announced.<br /> <br /> I've also been considering a reworking of how Vocations and Sects relate, and how the Announced grow and change. Perhaps Vocations are simply loose groupings of more or less unique beings (like 2e Lineages), while the Y-splat isn't Sect but something like Refinements, much like Prometheans. Obviously there will be some changes in how they relate to Refinements, and I'm now thinking Sects should be Z-splats, like Lodges or Legacies. <br /> <br /> Considering how rare the Announced are, small groups of like-minded individuals makes more sense than global organizations. This is where they would differ strongly from the Arisen, since they don't have the advantage of shared history, total immortality, and an actual government structure they have inherited. Primary antagonists (other than each other) would probably be Alchemists or Penitentes, with Pandorans taking centre stage in specific circumstances.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":4152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","user":"Arcanist","date":". 12-30-2015, 11:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-02T09:57:48-08:00","id":798302,"title":"","content":"I see now. Would you mind if I just popped ideas for Edicts up on here every now and again or will that irritate you? I love the concept of Irin and would love to work as a kind of beta reader or something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/forum/core/<img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=423\" data-tempid=\"temp_423_1523360155059_212\" />\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10397-ajf115","name":"ajf115","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10397_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2416"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-03T08:21:15-08:00","id":799627,"title":"","content":"Wouldn't bother me at all. If you have other ideas, they'd be much appreciated. I'm also thinking of dropping hints that the Irin didn't always look like angels- but that they resemble local ideas of the Divine. It's just that from the Second Temple Era onwards, ideas of angels became commonplace, and they remain the most commonly recognized divine form in the world. That and the Grigori connect most strongly with the Book of Enoch story and that has influenced things.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-23T13:10:34-07:00","id":851952,"title":"","content":"is Irin still ongoing or has it been abandoned?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/forum/core/<img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=423\" data-tempid=\"temp_423_1523360155059_212\" />\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10397-ajf115","name":"ajf115","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10397_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2416"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-23T19:50:56-07:00","id":852197,"title":"","content":"It's on hold until Promethean 2e comes out","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-26T03:58:23-08:00","id":1022267,"title":"","content":"This may be necroing, but is Irin not going anywhere, now that Promethean 2e is out, or what?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/forum/core/<img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=423\" data-tempid=\"temp_423_1523360155059_212\" />\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10397-ajf115","name":"ajf115","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10397_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2416"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-07T12:45:47-07:00","id":1340068,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"4152\" data-vbnamecard=\"4152\">Arcanist</a>, would you be opposed to someone compiling the work that's already been done in a document or something similar, for easy viewing? Also, if you'd like help finishing it, I'm very interested in helping! This game has the potential to rival even Princess: the Hopeful, imo","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11866-thescottishfianna","name":"TheScottishFianna","avatar":"https://for